'''Alan D Albright'''<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Albright%20SJQ.pdf|title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees|website=Judiciary.senate.gov|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref>{{efn|"D" is Albright's middle name, it is not an initial}} (born November 24, 1959)<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.kwtx.com/content/news/Senate-confirms-new-Waco-US-district-judge-492703391.html|title=Senate confirms new Waco US district judge|work=KWTX|date=September 7, 2018}}</ref> is an American lawyer who was a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Texas]] from 2018 to 2026. He was previously a [[United States magistrate judge]] of the same court. Albright at one point oversaw a significant portion of patent litigation within the United States. In 2021, the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]] repeatedly rebuked him in a string of opinions for failing to transfer cases to more apt jurisdictions. A quarter of all patent lawsuits in the U.S. were once heard by Albright, who has been widely criticized for ignoring binding case law. However, following a docket-stripping order issued by Chief Judge Orlando Garcia, Albright's patent docket declined precipitously.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.law.com/texaslawyer/2022/07/25/the-western-district-of-texas-may-have-just-dethroned-the-king-of-patent-litigation/|title=The King of Patent Litigation Might Just Have Been Dethroned|website=Texas Lawyer|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref>

== Biography ==
Albright earned his [[Bachelor of Arts]] with honors from [[Trinity University (Texas)|Trinity University]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Texas School of Law]]. He taught [[trial advocacy]] at the [[University of Texas School of Law]] for several years as an [[adjunct professor]]. Before becoming a district judge, Albright was a [[Law firm partner|partner]] in the [[Austin, Texas|Austin]] office of [[Bracewell LLP]], where his practice focused on [[intellectual property]] law. He was in private practice at other firms in Austin from 1999 to 2015.<ref name="WHBio">{{Cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-tenth-wave-judicial-nominees/|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Tenth Wave of Judicial Nominees – The White House|website=Trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==
Albright served as a [[United States magistrate judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Texas]] from 1992 to 1999.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.mystatesman.com/news/trump-names-austin-lawyer-alan-albright-federal-bench/73ERaJa1F5RZdOmfNpI5OM/|title=Trump names Austin lawyer Alan Albright to federal bench|website=Mystatesman.com|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref> On January 23, 2018, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Albright to an undetermined seat on the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Texas]].<ref name="WHBio" /> On January 24, 2018, his nomination was sent to the [[United States Senate]]. He was nominated to the seat vacated by Judge [[Walter Scott Smith Jr.]], who retired on September 14, 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/eight-nominations-sent-senate-today-4/|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Eight Nominations Sent to the Senate Today}}</ref> On April 25, 2018, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/04/25/2018/nominations|title=Nominations &#124; United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=Judiciary.senate.gov|date=April 25, 2018 |access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref> On May 24, 2018, his nomination was reported out of committee by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/05-24-18%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – May 24, 2018, Senate Judiciary Committee|website=Judiciary.senate.gov|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref> On September 6, 2018, his nomination was confirmed by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/1523|title=PN1523 — Alan D. Albright — The Judiciary|date=January 24, 2018|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=September 6, 2018|language=en}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on September 10, 2018,<ref name=FJC>{{FJC Bio|nid=5198506|inline=yes}}</ref> and was sworn in by Chief Judge [[Orlando Luis Garcia]] on September 18, 2018. He resigned from the court on August 31, 2026.<ref name=FJC/>

=== Patent law ===
After Albright encouraged patent owners to file claims in the Western District of Texas, one fifth of the nation's patent cases were filed in the district.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Davis|first=Ryan|date=July 2, 2021|title=WDTX Now Has 25% Of All US Patent Cases - Law360|url=https://www.law360.com/articles/1400052/wdtx-now-has-25-of-all-us-patent-cases|url-status=live|access-date=2021-09-29|website=Law360.com|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210702222331/https://www.law360.com/articles/1400052/wdtx-now-has-25-of-all-us-patent-cases |archive-date=July 2, 2021 }}</ref> On September 24, 2021, the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]] rebuked Albright for refusing to follow the law limiting federal judges from retaining cases that should be transferred to another jurisdiction.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Kass|first=David|date=September 24, 2021|title=Fed. Circ. Rebukes Judge Albright For Still Avoiding Transfers - Law360|url=https://www.law360.com/articles/1425060/fed-circ-rebukes-judge-albright-for-still-avoiding-transfers|url-access=subscription|access-date=2021-09-29|website=Law360.com|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite court|url=https://scholar.google.com/scholar_case?case=15836740855634980474|litigants=In re Juniper Networks, Inc.|vol=14 |reporter=F.4th|opinion =1313|year=2021|court=Fed. Cir.}}</ref> On October 21, 2021, the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit|Federal Circuit]] issued a [[writ of mandamus]] transferring another patent case that Albright oversaw because of his prior denial of transfer. The Federal Circuit's opinion rebuked Albright's continuous denial of transfers from the Western District.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Kass|first=Daniel|date=October 22, 2021|title=Fed. Circ. Overrides Albright After He Ignored Implied Order - Law360|url=https://www.law360.com/articles/1433345/fed-circ-overrides-albright-after-he-ignored-implied-order|url-access=subscription|url-status=live|access-date=2021-10-22|website=Law360.com|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211022010456/https://www.law360.com/articles/1433345/fed-circ-overrides-albright-after-he-ignored-implied-order |archive-date=October 22, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite court|litigants=In re Dish Network, L.L.C.|url=https://scholar.google.com/scholar_case?case=12117226215138718052|vol=2021|reporter=WL|opinion=4911981|court=Fed. Cir.|date=Oct. 21, 2021}}</ref>

Due to Albright's actions, on November 2, 2021, Senators [[Thom Tillis]] and [[Patrick Leahy]] wrote a bipartisan letter asking Chief Justice [[John Roberts]] to direct the [[Judicial Conference of the United States]] to "conduct a study of actual and potential abuses" that result from an "absence of adequate rules regulating judicial assignment and venue for patent cases within a [single judicial] district.".<ref>{{cite web|title=Letter to the Chief Justice of the United States of America from Senators Thom Tillis and Patrick Leahy|url=https://patentlyo.com/media/2021/11/Letter-to-the-Chief-Justice-about-Judge-Albright.pdf|date=November 2, 2021|website=Patentlyo.com|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref><ref name="Witherspoon">{{Cite web|last=Witherspoon|first=Tommy|date=November 5, 2021|title=Senators slam Waco federal judge over 'extreme concentration' of patent cases|url=https://wacotrib.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/senators-slam-waco-federal-judge-over-extreme-concentration-of-patent-cases/article_e18ebf4e-3e50-11ec-8d59-e79aac25838a.html|url-status=live|access-date=2021-11-15|website=WacoTrib.com|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211106002134/https://wacotrib.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/senators-slam-waco-federal-judge-over-extreme-concentration-of-patent-cases/article_e18ebf4e-3e50-11ec-8d59-e79aac25838a.html |archive-date=November 6, 2021 }}</ref> The letter criticized Albright for having "openly solicited cases at lawyers' meetings" and for having "repeatedly ignored binding case law and abused his discretion," noting that Albright's decisions "resulted in a flood of mandamus petitions" being filed and granted "no fewer than 15 times in just the past two years.".<ref name="Witherspoon"/>

=== Judicial reassignment order for patent cases ===
Because Albright was the only district judge in the Waco Division of the Western District of Texas, any patent cases filed in Waco were guaranteed to be assigned to him. This effectively gave plaintiffs the power to choose the judge to whom their cases were assigned by filing in the Waco Division, resulting in a disproportionately large number of patent cases being heard by Albright. Faced with mounting criticism from scholars, U.S. Senators, and the Chief Justice of the United States, on July 25, 2022, Chief Judge Orlando Garcia stripped Albright of automatic assignment of new patent cases filed in the Waco Division.<ref name="txwd.uscourts.gov">{{cite web|url=https://www.txwd.uscourts.gov/wp-content/uploads/Standing%20Orders/District/Order%20Assigning%20the%20Business%20of%20the%20Court%20as%20it%20Relates%20to%20Patent%20Cases%20072522.pdf |title=Order Assigning the Business of the Court as it Relates to Patent Cases|website=Txwd.uscouts.gov|access-date=July 28, 2022}}</ref> The reassignment order stated that to "equitably distribute" patent cases, any new patent cases filed in the Waco Division on or after July 25, 2022, would be randomly assigned among twelve of the judges of the Western District of Texas, reducing Albright's case load to approximately 8% of new patent cases filed in Waco. The order only impacted judicial assignment of cases filed in the Waco Division, and only affects patent cases. Chief Judge Garcia's order was widely viewed by practitioners as signaling the demise of the Western District of Texas as a major venue for patent litigation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rudinger |first=Robert |date=July 29, 2022 |title=The Crumbling Of Judge Albright's Patent Empire |url=https://www.law360.com/ip/articles/1516439 |access-date=July 30, 2022 |website=Law360}}</ref>

== Return to private practice ==
On April 21, 2026, Albright told Bloomberg Law that he would be stepping down from his judgeship in August 2026 in order to return to private practice.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/litigation/trump-appointed-judge-albright-stepping-down-from-texas-bench|title=Judge Albright, Texas Patent Magnet, Leaving Bench in August (2)|website=Bloomberglaw.com|date=April 21, 2026|access-date=November 7, 2026}}</ref>

