'''Andrew Bray Davis'''<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.texasbar.com/AM/Template.cfm?Section=Find_A_Lawyer&template=/Customsource/MemberDirectory/MemberDirectoryDetail.cfm&ContactID=330342|title=State Bar of Texas &#124;&nbsp;Find A Lawyer &nbsp;&#124;&nbsp;Andrew Bray Davis|website=www.texasbar.com}}</ref><!--Reference for his full name.--> (known professionally as '''Andrew Davis'''; born 1985) serves as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Texas]]. Prior to his appointment to the bench, Davis was an attorney in private practice serving as a partner at the [[law firm]] of Lehotsky Keller Cohn LLP.  

==Early life and education==

Davis was born in 1985 in [[Madison, Wisconsin]].<ref name="SJQ">{{cite web |last=Davis |first=Andrew Bray |date=February 4, 2026 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/davis_sjq.pdf |website=www.judiciary.senate.gov |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=February 6, 2026}}</ref> He received his [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 2008 from [[Rice University]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] in 2012 from [[Columbia Law School]]. He served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[Sidney A. Fitzwater]] of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Texas]] and Judge [[Reena Raggi]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit]].<ref name="Truth Social">{{Cite web|url=https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/115850509804217257|title=Truth Social|website=Truth Social}}</ref><ref name="SJQ" />

==Career==

Davis served as a [[Law firm partner|partner]] at the law firm of Lehotsky Keller Kohn LLP at their [[Austin, Texas|Austin]] office until his appointment to the federal bench.<ref name="Lehotsky">{{Cite web|url=https://lkcfirm.com/andrew-davis-bio|title=Andrew Davis Bio|website=Lehotsky Keller Cohn}}</ref> He previously served as chief counsel to [[United States Senator]] [[Ted Cruz]] and as the assistant solicitor general of Texas.<ref name="Truth Social"/> Earlier in his career, he served as a trial and appellate litigator at [[Gibson Dunn|Gibson, Dunn & Crutcher LLP]] in [[Washington, D.C.]]<ref name="Lehotsky"/>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On January 6, 2026, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Davis to an unspecified seat on the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Texas]].<ref name="Truth Social"/> On January 29, 2026, Trump formally nominated Davis to the seat on the Western District of Texas vacated by Judge [[Earl Leroy Yeakel III]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/787/1|title=PN787-1 - Nomination of Andrew B. Davis for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=January 29, 2026|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref> On February 6, 2026, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 6, 2026 |title=Nominations |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/nominations-02-04-2026}}</ref> On March 5, 2026, his nomination was reported from the Judiciary Committee by a 12–10 [[party-line vote]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 5, 2026 |title=RESULTS OF EXECUTIVE BUSINESS MEETING |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/0eb50502-98e6-61ed-bd12-2132806a72a9/20260305_EBM_Results.pdf}}</ref> On April 16, 2026, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 49–48 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 16, 2026 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Andrew B. Davis to be U.S. District Judge for the Western District of Texas) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1192/vote_119_2_00085.htm}}</ref> On April 20, 2026, his nomination was confirmed by a 47–46 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 20, 2026 |title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Andrew B. Davis, of Texas, to be U.S. District Judge for the Western District of Texas) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1192/vote_119_2_00086.htm |website=senate.gov}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on April 30, 2026.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762122|inline=yes}}</ref>

