'''Mary Elizabeth "Beth" Phillips''' (born 1969) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Missouri]].

==Early life and education==
Phillips was born in [[Kirksville, Missouri]], in 1969.<ref name=main_justice_20091014>{{Cite news | first=Stephanie | last=Woodrow | url=http://www.mainjustice.com/2009/10/14/meet-beth-phillips/ | title=Meet Beth Phillips | work=Main Justice | date=October 14, 2009 | access-date=June 8, 2011 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110707133717/http://www.mainjustice.com/2009/10/14/meet-beth-phillips/ | archive-date=July 7, 2011 }}</ref> She received two degrees from the [[University of Chicago]], a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in 1991 and a [[Master of Arts]] in 1992.<ref name=whgov_20110607>{{Cite web | author=The White House: Office of the Press Secretary | url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/06/07/president-obama-nominates-four-united-states-district-court | title=President Obama Nominates Four to the United States District Court | date=June 7, 2011 | via=[[NARA|National Archives]] | work=[[whitehouse.gov]] | access-date=June 8, 2011}}</ref> Phillips then earned her [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Missouri School of Law]] in 1996.<ref name=whgov_20110607/><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393906|inline=yes}}</ref>

== Career ==
===U.S. attorney===

On September 30, 2009, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Phillips to serve as the [[United States Attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Missouri|Western District of Missouri]].<ref name=whgov_20090930>{{Cite web | author=The White House: Office of the Press Secretary | url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/president-obama-nominates-sanford-coats-beth-phillips-and-stephanie-villafuerte-be- | title=President Obama Nominates Sanford Coats, Beth Phillips and Stephanie Villafuerte to be U.S. Attorneys | date=September 30, 2009 | via=[[NARA|National Archives]] | work=[[whitehouse.gov]] | access-date=June 8, 2011}}</ref> She was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] on December 24, 2009.<ref name=main_justice_20091224>{{Cite news | first=Andrew | last=Ramonas | url=http://www.mainjustice.com/2009/12/24/senate-confirms-seven-u-s-attorneys/ | title=Senate Confirms Seven U.S. Attorneys | work=Main Justice | date=December 24, 2009 | access-date=June 8, 2011 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120326123738/http://www.mainjustice.com/2009/12/24/senate-confirms-seven-u-s-attorneys/ | archive-date=March 26, 2012 }}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov"/>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On June 7, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Phillips to a seat on [[United States District Court for the Western District of Missouri]].<ref name=whgov_20110607/> She would replace Judge [[Ortrie D. Smith]], who assumed senior status in 2011. She received a hearing before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on September 20, 2011, and her nomination was reported to the floor of the Senate by a [[voice vote]] on October 13, 2011. On March 6, 2012, the United States Senate confirmed Phillips' nomination by a 95–2 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1122/vote_112_2_00026.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Mary Elizabeth Phillips, of Missouri, to be U.S. District Judge)|access-date=2022-11-05}}</ref> She received her commission on March 22, 2012.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> She was sworn in on March 23, 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mow.uscourts.gov/judges/phillips.html|title=U.S. District Judge Beth Phillips – Western District of Missouri|work=uscourts.gov|access-date=23 June 2015}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov"/> She became chief judge on January 3, 2019.

