'''Brian Curtis Wimes''' (born January 18, 1966) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Missouri]] and [[United States District Court for the Western District of Missouri]], serving since 2026 as the chief judge of the latter. Despite his dual appointment, Wimes maintains chambers only in the Western District of Missouri.

==Biography==

Wimes graduated from the [[University of Kansas]], in 1990, with a [[Bachelor of General Studies]] in political science.  He briefly worked for the Kansas City school district prior to entering law school.  He graduated from the [[Thurgood Marshall School of Law]] with a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1994.  From 1994 to 1995, he worked as an Attorney Advisor for the [[U.S. Department of Justice]], Bureau of Prisons Litigation Branch, in [[Washington, D.C.]] From 1995 to 2001, he worked as an assistant prosecuting attorney in the [[Jackson County, Missouri|Jackson County]] Prosecutors Office.  From 2001 to 2007, he served as [[Drug Court]] Commissioner in Jackson County.  In 2007 he was appointed judge on the [[Missouri Circuit Courts|Jackson County Circuit Court]] by [[Missouri Governor]] [[Matt Blunt]], a position he held until his confirmation as a U.S. district judge.<ref name="whitehouse.gov">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/09/22/president-obama-nominates-judge-brian-c-wimes-serve-united-states-distri|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Judge Brian C. Wimes to Serve on the United States District Court|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=22 September 2011}}</ref><ref>[http://www.16thcircuit.org/Divs/div_bio.asp?div=DIV18 Judge Wime's Biography from the 16th Judicial Circuit Court] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120402222300/http://www.16thcircuit.org/Divs/div_bio.asp?div=DIV18 |date=April 2, 2012 }}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393956|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On September 22, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Wimes for district judge of the United States District Court for the Eastern and Western District of Missouri to the seat vacated by Judge [[Nanette Kay Laughrey]], who assumed [[senior status]] on August 27, 2011.<ref name="whitehouse.gov"/> He received a hearing before the [[United States Senate|Senate]] [[United States Senate Judiciary Committee|Judiciary Committee]] on November 14, 2011 and his nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]] on December 15, 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/ExecutiveBusinessMeetingResults-12-15-2011.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting - December 15, 2011|website=judiciary.senate.gov}}</ref> On April 23, 2012, the Senate confirmed his nomination by a 92–1 vote, with Utah Senator [[Mike Lee]] casting the lone nay vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=112&session=2&vote=00067|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Brian C. Wimes, of Missouri, to be U.S. District Judge for the Eastern and Western Districts of Missouri)|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> He received his commission on April 30, 2012,<ref name="fjc.gov"/> and was sworn in the same day.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mow.uscourts.gov/judges/wimes.html|title=Judge Brian C. Wimes – Western District of Missouri|website=www.mow.uscourts.gov}}</ref> He became the chief judge of the Western District in 2026.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

===Notable cases===
Wimes denied a request for a 19-year-old woman to be present for her father's execution for the death of Officer William McEntee. [[Kevin Johnson Jr.]] was executed on November 30. Missouri law prohibits anyone under 21 from witnessing an execution.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 26, 2022 |title=Judge denies 19-year-old's ask to attend father's execution |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/judge-denies-19-year-olds-attend-fathers-execution-93997394 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221126190228/https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/judge-denies-19-year-olds-attend-fathers-execution-93997394 |url-status=dead |archive-date=November 26, 2022 |access-date=December 6, 2022 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref>

On March 7, 2023, Wimes found a state law, signed by Governor [[Mike Parson]], regulating cooperation with federal authorities on firearms issues, to be unconstitutional as a violation of the [[Supremacy Clause]]. Missouri Attorney General [[Andrew Bailey (politician)|Andrew Bailey]] said he would challenge the decision and Republican congressman [[Eric Burlison]] denigrated the decision as being understandable supposedly because Wimes was an appointee of President [[Barack Obama]].<ref name=Bailey>[https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article272846365.html Federal judge tosses Missouri gun law, ruling it 'exposes citizens to greater harm'], ''[[Kansas City Star]]'', Jonathan Shorman and Kacen Bayless, March 7, 2023. Retrieved March 8, 2023.</ref>

