'''Chad Francis Kenney Sr.''' (born August 8, 1955) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania]]. He was previously a Judge of the [[Pennsylvania courts of common pleas|Delaware County Court of Common Pleas]].

== Biography ==

Kenney earned his [[Bachelor of Arts]], ''[[cum laude]]'', from [[Villanova University]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[Temple University Beasley School of Law]] where he was a member of the ''[[Temple Law Review]]''.

He began his legal career in 1980 as a [[civil litigator]] and later entered general practice.  From 1981 to 1983, he was a central legal staff lawyer for the [[Superior Court of Pennsylvania]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://ballotpedia.org/Chad_F._Kenney,_Sr. | title=Chad F. Kenney, Sr }}</ref> He practiced in Delaware County and Philadelphia as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]], [[Law firm partner|partner]], and a [[sole practitioner]], in both large and small firms. Prior to his election to the bench, Kenney was elected twice as [[Delaware County, Pennsylvania|Delaware County]] Sheriff. From 1996 to 2003, he served as a Pennsylvania State Republican Committee member.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://vettingroom.org/2018/05/18/judge-chad-kenney/|title=Judge Chad Kenney – Nominee for the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania|access-date=2019-06-29|language=en}}</ref> From 2003 to 2018, he served as a Judge of the Delaware County Court of Common Pleas. During his time on the state bench, Kenney presided over cases in the family, criminal, civil, and orphans' sections. In 2012, his judicial colleagues elected him to serve a five-year term as the President Judge.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-ninth-wave-judicial-nominees-tenth-wave-united-states-attorney-nominees/|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Ninth Wave of Judicial Nominees and Tenth Wave of United States Attorney Nominees|access-date=2018-04-03|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|language=en-US}}</ref> He stepped down in 2017 after his term ended.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.delcotimes.com/article/DC/20170614/NEWS/170619796|title=Judge Kevin Kelly sworn in as new president judge in Delco|access-date=2018-04-03|language=en}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==

On December 20, 2017, President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Kenney to serve as a United States District Judge of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[L. Felipe Restrepo]], who was elevated to the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]] on January 13, 2016.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/eight-nominations-sent-senate-today-3/|title=Eight Nominations Sent to the Senate Today|access-date=2018-04-03|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|language=en-US}}</ref> On May 9, 2018, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/05/09/2018/nominations|title=Nominations - United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov|date=May 9, 2018 }}</ref> On June 7, 2018, his nomination was reported out of committee by [[voice vote]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/06-07-18%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf Results of Executive Business Meeting – June 7, 2018, Senate Judiciary Committee]</ref> On October 11, 2018, his nomination was confirmed by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/1335|title=PN1335 — Chad F. Kenney — The Judiciary|last=|first=|date=December 20, 2017|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=October 11, 2018|language=en}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on October 24, 2018.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=5375101|inline=yes}}</ref>

== Notable cases ==
* In March 2019, Kenney dismissed a [[class action lawsuit]] against a [[landfill]] in [[Bethlehem, Pennsylvania]], ruling that "residents didn't provide any arguments to prove that Bethlehem Landfill Co. has an obligation to protect neighboring landowners from bad smells or other nuisance conditions."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://pennrecord.com/stories/512316532-bethlehem-landfill-isn-t-obligated-to-protect-neighbors-from-bad-odors-judge-rules|title=Bethlehem landfill isn't obligated to protect neighbors from bad odors, judge rules|work=Penn Record|access-date=2019-06-29|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wastedive.com/news/freemansburg-pennsylvania-landfill-lawsuit/527746/|title=Waste Connections wins landfill nuisance lawsuit in Pennsylvania|website=Waste Dive|language=en-US|access-date=2019-07-20}}</ref>

* In May 2019, Kenney awarded $33,000 in damages under the [[Telephone Consumer Protection Act of 1991|Telephone Consumer Protection Act]] (TCPA) for violating do-not-call requests. The plaintiff, James Everett Shelton, regularly brings lawsuits alleging violations of the TCPA, which allows $500 - $1500 in damages per call.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.readingeagle.com/news/article/king-of-prussia-man-23-wins-33000-after-suing-over-illegal-sales-calls|title=King of Prussia man, 23, wins $33,000 after suing over illegal sales calls|date=2019-05-13|website=Reading Eagle|language=en|access-date=2019-07-20}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pennrecord.com/stories/512476399-money-piles-up-for-serial-tcpa-lawsuit-filer-from-king-of-prussia-this-week-s-trial-looks-like-a-win|title=Money piles up for serial TCPA lawsuit-filer from King of Prussia; This week's trial looks like a win|last=Malfitano|first=Nicholas|website=pennrecord.com|language=en|access-date=2019-07-20}}</ref>

* In June 2019, Kenney denied a request by a Pennsylvania [[abortion clinic]] to ban a man engaged in [[sidewalk counseling]] from the street outside the clinic "while the court determines if he and another demonstrator have violated a federal law against impeding access to reproductive health care."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.mcall.com/news/police/mc-nws-allentown-womens-center-abortion-protester-denied-20190628-l4eppybgyjctlmhcfgcdsyjqh4-story.html|title=Judge denies Allentown Women's Center's request to ban 'sidewalk counselor'|work=The Morning Call|access-date=2019-06-28|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lehighvalleylive.com/news/2019/07/protester-is-allowed-to-continue-sidewalk-counseling-outside-bethlehem-area-abortion-clinic.html|title=Protester is allowed to continue 'sidewalk counseling' outside Bethlehem area abortion clinic|last=Miller|first=Rudy|date=2019-07-01|website=lehighvalleylive.com|language=en-US|access-date=2019-07-20}}</ref> The abortion clinic and the demonstrators eventually reached a settlement agreement.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lehighvalleylive.com/news/2019/12/bethlehem-area-abortion-clinic-settles-case-with-sidewalk-counselors.html|title=Bethlehem area abortion clinic settles case with 'sidewalk counselors'|last=lehighvalleylive.com|first=Rudy Miller {{!}} For|date=2019-12-06|website=lehighvalleylive|language=en|access-date=2019-12-09}}</ref>

* In March 2022, Kenney ruled a provision in the Pennsylvania Rules of Professional Conduct which barred attorneys from engaging in harassment and discrimination as unconstitutional and prohibited on First Amendment free speech grounds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Volokh |first=Eugene |date=March 24, 2022 |title=Court Strikes Down Pennsylvania Lawyer Speech Code |url=https://reason.com/volokh/2022/03/24/court-strikes-down-pennsylvania-lawyer-speech-code/ |access-date=March 24, 2022 |website=[[Reason_(magazine)]]}}</ref>

