'''David Louis Bunning''' (born 1966)<ref name="NKY encyclopedia">{{cite book |editor1-last=Tenkotte |editor1-first=Paul A. |editor2-last=Claypool |editor2-first=James C. |title=The Encyclopedia of Northern Kentucky |chapter=Bunning, David L. |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Zc0eBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA132 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Zc0eBgAAQBAJ |page=132 |date=February 5, 2015 |publisher=[[University Press of Kentucky]] |location=[[Lexington, Kentucky]] |isbn=9780813159966 |access-date=September 5, 2015}}</ref> is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Kentucky]]. Bunning is the son of former [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] Senator [[Jim Bunning]], a [[National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum|Hall of Fame]] baseball pitcher who represented [[Kentucky]] in the [[United States Senate]] from 1999 to 2011.<ref name=wp/>

==Life and career==
Bunning was born in [[Fort Thomas, Kentucky]], the son of Mary Catherine (Theis) and Jim Bunning. He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree from the [[University of Kentucky]] in 1988 and a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Kentucky College of Law]] in 1991.<ref name=wp/> He was a [[law clerk]] in the [[United States Attorney]]'s Office, Eastern District of Kentucky in 1991, and was an [[Assistant United States Attorney]] in that office from 1991 to 2002.{{citation needed|date=January 2023}}

=== Federal judicial service ===
Bunning was nominated by President [[George W. Bush]] on September 4, 2001, to a seat on the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Kentucky]], which was vacated by [[William Bertelsman]]. On December 10, 2001, representatives of the [[American Bar Association]]'s Standing Committee on Federal Judiciary testified before the [[United States Senate]] Committee of the Judiciary for the ABA's majority opinion that Bunning was unqualified for the position of a Federal District Court Judge due to his age of 35, a lack of complex civil case experience as a federal attorney, and his "middle-of-the-class law school" experience at the [[University of Kentucky]].<ref>{{Citation | last1 = Trimmier, Jr. | first1 =Roscoe | last2 =Weiner | first2  = David C. | title = Statement of Roscoe C Trimmier, Jr. and David C. Weiner, Standing Committee on Federal Judiciary, American Bar Association, concerning the nomination of David L. Bunning to be Judge of the United States District Court for the Eastern District of Kentucky, before the Committee of the Judiciary, United States Senate | place  = Washington D.C., United States | date = 10 December 2001|url=http://www.americanbar.org/content/dam/aba/migrated/scfedjud/statements/bunning.authcheckdam.pdf}}</ref> He was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] with a unanimous vote on February 14, 2002,<ref>{{cite web |title=Confirmation of judge bypasses ABA's objection |url=https://www.bgdailynews.com/confirmation-of-judge-bypasses-aba-s-objection/article_5260ce91-0a91-5afb-adf9-d82a81357e45.html |website=Bowling Green Daily News |date=4 March 2002 |access-date=21 March 2022}}</ref> and received his commission on February 19, 2002.<ref name=wp>{{cite news |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/post-nation/wp/2015/09/04/he-has-guts-judge-david-bunning-the-same-sex-marriage-decisions-unlikely-enforcer/ |title= 'He has guts': David Bunning, the same-sex marriage decision's unlikely enforcer |newspaper= The Washington Post |first1= Abby |last1= Phillip |first2= Sarah |last2= Larimer |date= September 4, 2015 |access-date= September 4, 2015}}</ref> He became chief judge on February 1, 2025.

Vice President [[JD Vance]] clerked for Bunning.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.businessinsider.com/jd-vance-venture-capital-opioid-nonprofit-tech-record-ohio-senate-2021-8/ |title= 'Hillbilly Elegy' author JD Vance is running for Senate as a savior of the Rust Belt. Insiders and experts say that reputation is unearned. |newspaper= Business Insider|first1= Meghan |last1= Morris |first2= Adam |last2= Wren |date= August 29, 2021 |access-date= January 24, 2026}}</ref>

==Notable cases==
{{main|Miller v. Davis}}

On September 3, 2015, Bunning issued a [[contempt of court]] ruling against [[Rowan County, Kentucky|Rowan County]] clerk [[Kim Davis]] and had her jailed for refusing to issue [[marriage license]]s to same-sex couples.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/09/03/politics/kentucky-clerk-same-sex-marriage-kim-davis/index.html |title=Kim Davis stands firm on same-sex marriage; the Kentucky clerk stays in jail |first1=Eliott C. |last1=McLaughlin |first2=Catherine E. |last2=Shoichet |work=CNN news |date=September 3, 2015 |access-date=September 4, 2015}}</ref> Bunning said in his ruling, "Our form of government will not survive unless we, as a society, agree to respect the U.S. Supreme Court's decisions, regardless of our personal opinions. Davis is certainly free to disagree with the Court's opinion, as many Americans likely do, but that does not excuse her from complying with it. To hold otherwise would set a dangerous precedent."<ref>{{Cite web|url = https://www.liberty.edu/media/9980/attachments/2015/081215_-_Order_-_Granting_PI.pdf|title = Liberty.edu|date = August 12, 2015|website = www.liberty.edu|last = Blevins|first = Walter|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150912173706/https://www.liberty.edu/media/9980/attachments/2015/081215_-_Order_-_Granting_PI.pdf|archive-date = September 12, 2015|url-status = dead}}</ref>

