'''Elizabeth Ann Wolford'''<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Elizabeth-Wolford-Senate-Questionnaire-Final.pdf|title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees|work=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|date=July 10, 2013}}</ref> (born 1966) is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of New York]]. She is the first woman to serve as a judge in the Western District<ref>[http://www.afj.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/06/President-Obama-Female-Firsts.pdf Making History: President Obama’s Female Judicial Nominees] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180928205158/https://www.afj.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/06/President-Obama-Female-Firsts.pdf |date=2018-09-28 }}, ''[[Alliance for Justice]]'' (June 11, 2013).</ref> and the first woman to be chief judge of the Western District of New York.<ref name=Chief>{{cite web|url=https://www.nywd.uscourts.gov/sites/nywd/files/WDNY%20New%20Chief%20Judge%20-%20with%20Photo.pdf|title=United States District Judge Elizabeth A. Wolford to become first woman to serve as Chief Judge of the Western District of New York|publisher=United States District Court for the Western District of New York|date=July 12, 2021|accessdate=July 14, 2021}}</ref>

==Biography==

Wolford was born in 1966, in [[Buffalo, New York|Buffalo]], New York.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buffalonews.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20130305/CITYANDREGION/130309548/1024|title="First Female Judge is on Horizon for WNY" The Buffalo News|publisher=|access-date=2013-05-17|archive-date=2016-03-04|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304105633/http://www.buffalonews.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20130305/CITYANDREGION/130309548/1024|url-status=dead}}</ref> She received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1989, from [[Colgate University]]. She received a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1992, from the [[University of Notre Dame Law School]]. Upon graduation from law school she joined the recently formed The Wolford Law Firm LLP as a partner, spending her entire career in private practice at that firm. Her practice was concentrated in the areas of commercial, employment and personal injury litigation before both federal and state courts. She served as President of the Greater Rochester Association for Women Attorneys from 2003 to 2004 and as President of the Foundation of the Monroe County Bar from 2010 to 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/05/16/president-obama-nominates-three-serve-united-states-district-court|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Three to Serve on the United States District Court|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=16 May 2013}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394321|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On May 16, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Wolford to serve as a United States District Judge of the United States District Court for the Western District of New York, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Charles J. Siragusa]], who assumed [[senior status]] on December 15, 2012. The [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] reported her nomination out of committee on August 1, 2013 by a [[voice vote]]. On December 12, 2013, the [[United States Senate]] invoked [[cloture]] on Wolford’s nomination by a 55–41 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=1&vote=00259|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Nomination of Elizabeth A. Wolford, of N.Y., to be U.S. District Judge)|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> Later that day, her nomination was confirmed by a 70–29 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1131/vote_113_1_00260.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Elizabeth A. Wolford, of New York, to be U.S. District Judge)|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> She received her judicial commission on December 17, 2013.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> She became chief judge on July 14, 2021.<ref name="Chief"/>

