'''James Donald Peterson''' (born 1957) is an American lawyer who serves as the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Wisconsin]].

==Biography==

Peterson was born in 1957.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lawyercentral.com/james-d-peterson-interactive-profile--20-695412.html|title=Attorney James D Peterson – Lawyer in Milwaukee WI|publisher=|access-date=March 28, 2016|archive-date=November 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181106132148/https://www.lawyercentral.com/james-d-peterson-interactive-profile--20-695412.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> He received his [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in 1979, [[Master of Arts]] degree in 1984 and [[Doctor of Philosophy]] in 1986 from the [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]]. After graduation, he was a faculty member at the [[University of Notre Dame]], where he taught film and television history. He received his [[Juris Doctor]] in 1998 from the [[University of Wisconsin Law School]], graduating [[Order of the Coif]]. He served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[David G. Deininger]] of the [[Wisconsin Court of Appeals]] from 1998 to 1999. From 1999 to 2014, he had been a shareholder at the [[Wisconsin]] law firm of Godfrey & Kahn, S.C., where he was a member of the litigation and intellectual property practice groups and led the firm's intellectual property litigation working group. Additionally, he served as an adjunct faculty member at the University of Wisconsin Law School, where he taught copyright law.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/11/07/president-obama-nominates-three-serve-united-states-district-courts|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Three to Serve on the United States District Courts|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=November 7, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gklaw.com/attorney.cfm?attorney_id=76|title=Godfrey & Kahn S.C.: Attorneys & Professionals – Godfrey & Kahn|publisher=|access-date=November 8, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130825200039/http://www.gklaw.com/attorney.cfm?attorney_id=76|archive-date=August 25, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394491}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On November 7, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Peterson to serve as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Wisconsin]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[John C. Shabaz]], who assumed [[senior status]] on January 20, 2009.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/11/07/presidential-nominations-sent-senate|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Presidential Nominations Sent to the Senate|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=November 7, 2013}}</ref> On February 6, 2014, his nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]], with 2 senators recorded as “no”.<ref>{{cite web|title=Executive Business Meeting — February 6, 2014|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/ExecutiveBusinessMeetingResults-02-06-2014.pdf|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=February 6, 2014}}</ref> Cloture was filed on his nomination on May 6, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://democrats.senate.gov/2014/05/06/cloture-filed-on-4-judicial-nominations-talwani-peterson-rosenstengal-rosenbaum/|title=Cloture filed on 4 judicial nominations (Talwani, Peterson, Rosenstengal, Rosenbaum)|publisher=|access-date=December 10, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161121144432/https://democrats.senate.gov/2014/05/06/cloture-filed-on-4-judicial-nominations-talwani-peterson-rosenstengal-rosenbaum/|archive-date=November 21, 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> On May 8, 2014, the [[United States Senate]] invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 56–40 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00135|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Nomination of James D. Peterson, of Wisconsin, to be U.S. District Judge)|publisher=United States Senate|access-date=May 8, 2014}}</ref> Later the day, his nomination was confirmed by a 70–24 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00138|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation James D. Peterson, of Wisconsin, to be U.S. District Judge)|publisher=United States Senate|access-date=May 8, 2014}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on May 12, 2014. He became chief judge on April 26, 2017.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

===Rulings===
====2026 AI CSAM case====
In August 2026, Peterson ruled in favor of Steven Anderegg, a defendant accused of using [[generative AI]] to create obscene images of minors, then messaging them to a teenage boy on [[Instagram]]. He dismissed a charge in opposition to [[Title 18 of the United States Code|federal law]], ruling that Anderegg has a constitutional [[First Amendment to the United States Constitution|First Amendment]] right to privately possess virtual child sexual assault material.<ref>{{cite news|title=Court Rules That Constitution Protects Private Possession of AI-Generated CSAM|url=https://www.techpolicy.press/court-rules-that-constitution-protects-private-possession-of-aigenerated-csam/}}</ref> In the decision, Peterson wrote that he had "misgivings" about ruling in Anderegg's favor, but concluded that the law and other past higher-court decisions dictated that he rule in favor of the defendant.<ref>{{cite court |litigants=UNITED STATES OF AMERICA v. STEVEN ANDEREGG |court=United States District Court for the
Western District of Wisconsin |date=25 August 2026 |url=https://media.ca7.uscourts.gov/cgi-bin/OpinionsWeb/processWebInputExternal.pl?Path=Y2026%2FD08-25%2FC%3A25-1354%3AJ%3ALee%3Aaut%3AT%3AfnOp%3AN%3A3597567%3AS%3A0&Submit=Display&fbclid=IwY2xjawT-gdlwZG9mBWV4dG4DYWVtAjEwAGJyaWQRMWdldTdzcU0wQkNXWXRyWHdzcnRjBmFwcF9pZBAyMjIwMzkxNzg4MjAwODkyAAEeZvH2uXkiOg7R4G42ADlX3QE0WQEQGy-WoFFFYsphqfE7NRqpTprRQUCvsNQ_aem_wFmqC5c2AZqPH5A3V-pamA |quote= |postscript= }}</ref><ref group=note>Referencing ''Hutto v. Davis'' (1982), Peterson quoted in his decision regarding Anderegg that "a precedent of this Court must be followed by the lower federal courts no matter how misguided the judges of those courts may think it to be."</ref>

