'''John Charles Hinderaker'''  (born 1968)<ref name="Questionnaire">[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/John%20Hinderaker%20SJQ%20-%20PUBLIC.pdf United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees: John Hinderaker]</ref> is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Arizona]].

== Education ==

Hinderaker earned his [[Bachelor of Arts]] in [[Business Economics]], with honors, from the [[University of California, Santa Barbara]], in 1991 and his [[Juris Doctor]], ''[[magna cum laude]]'', from the [[University of Arizona College of Law]] in 1996, where he was a member of the ''Arizona Law Review''.<ref name="WHBio">[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-judicial-nominees-8/ "President Donald J. Trump Announces Judicial Nominees" White House, November 6, 2019] {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Career ==

Upon graduation from law school, Hinderaker served as a [[law clerk]] to Judge [[John Roll]] and Magistrate Judge Raymond T. Terlizzi, both of the [[United States District Court for the District of Arizona]]. He was hired as an [[associate lawyer|associate]] at the law firm of [[Lewis Roca Rothgerber Christie]] in [[Tucson, Arizona]], in 1998 and became a [[law firm partner|partner]] in 2003, where his practice focused on [[commercial litigation]].<ref name="WHBio" />

=== State judicial service ===

From 2018 to 2020, he served as a Judge on the [[Pima County]] [[Arizona Superior Court|Superior Court]] after being appointed by [[Arizona Governor]] [[Doug Ducey]]. He was appointed to fill the vacancy caused by the elevation of Judge Sean Brearcliffe to the [[Arizona Court of Appeals]].<ref name="WHBio" /><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://azgovernor.gov/governor/news/2018/02/governor-ducey-appoints-john-hinderaker-and-scott-mcdonald-pima-county |title=Governor Ducey Appoints John Hinderaker and Scott McDonald to The Pima County Superior Court |date=February 27, 2018 |website=Office of the Arizona Governor |language=en |access-date=November 6, 2019 |archive-date=November 6, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191106192923/https://azgovernor.gov/governor/news/2018/02/governor-ducey-appoints-john-hinderaker-and-scott-mcdonald-pima-county |url-status=dead }}</ref> His tenure on the state court bench ended when he became a Federal district judge.

=== Federal judicial service ===

On November 6, 2019, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Hinderaker to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] for the [[United States District Court for the District of Arizona]]. On December 2, 2019, his nomination was sent to the Senate. He was recommended to Trump by Senator [[Kyrsten Sinema]]. While Hinderaker is a Democrat, he was appointed as a district judge by a Republican Governor.<ref name="VettingRoom" /> President Trump nominated Hinderaker to the seat vacated by Judge [[Raner Collins]], who assumed [[senior status]] on March 4, 2019.<ref>[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/eight-nominations-one-withdrawal-sent-senate-2/ "Eight Nominations and One Withdrawal Sent to the Senate", White House, December 2, 2019]</ref> A hearing on his nomination before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] was held on December 4, 2019.<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/12/04/2019/nominations Nominations for December 4, 2019]</ref> On March 5, 2020, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 16–6 vote.<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/030520%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf Results of Executive Business Meeting – March 5, 2020, Senate Judiciary Committee]</ref> On September 23, 2020, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on his nomination by a 71–26 vote.<ref>[https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1162/vote_116_2_00190.htm On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: John Charles Hinderaker to be U.S. District Judge for the District of Arizona)] United States Senate, September 23, 2020</ref> His nomination was confirmed later that day by a 70–27 vote.<ref>[https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=116&session=2&vote=00191 On the Nomination (Confirmation: John Charles Hinderaker, of Arizona, to be U.S. District Judge for the District of Arizona)] United States Senate, September 23, 2020</ref> He received his judicial commission on September 29, 2020.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=8938396|inline=yes}}</ref>

