'''John George Edward Marck''' (born 1982)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.texasbar.com/AM/Template.cfm?Section=Find_A_Lawyer&template=/Customsource/MemberDirectory/MemberDirectoryDetail.cfm&ContactID=393468|title=State Bar of Texas &#124;&nbsp;Find A Lawyer &nbsp;&#124;&nbsp;John George Edward Marck|website=www.texasbar.com}}</ref> is an American lawyer who serves as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of Texas]]. He previously served as the acting [[United States attorney]] for the Southern District of Texas prior to becoming a judge.

== Early life and education ==

Marck was born in 1982, in [[West Islip, New York]]. 

Marck attended college at several institutions including [[Farmingdale State College]] from 2002 to 2003, [[Hofstra University]] in 2003, [[Suffolk County Community College]] from 2004 to 2005 and again from 2007 to 2008 and [[Tulane University]] in 2007 before receiving his [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 2009 from [[Brooklyn College]].<ref name="SJCQ" /> He earned his [[Juris Doctor]] in 2013 from [[St. John's University School of Law]].<ref name="Truth Social">{{Cite web|url=https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/116331984748685770|title=Donald J. Trump (@realDonaldTrump)|website=Truth Social}}</ref><ref name="Justice Bio">{{Cite web|url=https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdtx/meet-us-attorney|title=Southern District of Texas &#124; Meet the U.S. Attorney &#124; United States Department of Justice|date=March 1, 2021|website=www.justice.gov}}</ref><ref name="SJCQ">{{cite web |last=Marck |first=John George Edward |date=April 9, 2026 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/marck_sjq.pdf |website=www.judiciary.senate.gov |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=April 9, 2026}}</ref>

== Career ==

He worked as a [[security guard]] with [[ABM Industries|ABM Security Services]] from 2009 to 2013, while attending law school.  He began his career as an assistant district attorney for the [[Bronx County District Attorney]] and the [[Brooklyn District Attorney]] in [[New York City]]. From 2021 to 2026, he served as an [[assistant United States attorney]] in the Southern District of Texas, including as associate deputy criminal chief in 2025 and as first assistant United States attorney from 2025 to 2026, before becoming Acting United States attorney in 2026. In 2026, he served as the Acting [[United States Attorney]] for the Southern District of Texas.<ref name="Truth Social"/><ref name="Justice Bio"/><ref name="SJCQ"/>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On April 1, 2026, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Marck to an undesignated seat on the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of Texas]].<ref name="Truth Social"/> On April 14, 2026, Trump nominated Marck to the seat vacated by Judge [[Micaela Alvarez]]. On April 29, 2026 he had his confirmation hearing with the Senate Judiciary Committee. On June 18, 2026 the Judiciary Committee advanced his nomination on a 12–10 [[party-line vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/930/7|title=PN930-7 - Nomination of John George Edward Marck for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=June 23, 2026|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref> On June 24, 2026 the Senate invoked cloture on his nomination by a 52–45 vote<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 24, 2026 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: John George Edward Marck to be U.S. District Judge for the Southern District of Texas) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1192/vote_119_2_00185.htm |access-date=June 28, 2026 |website=senate.gov}}</ref> and on the same day he was confirmed by the same 52–45 margin.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 28, 2026 |title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: John George Edward Marck, of T.X., to be U.S. District Judge for the Southern District of Texas) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1192/vote_119_2_00186.htm |access-date=June 24, 2026 |website=senate.gov}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on July 9, 2026.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762135|inline=yes}}</ref>

