''' Karen Anne Gren Scholer''' ({{nee|'''Gren'''}}; born 1957)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://cemetery.tspb.texas.gov/pub/user_form822.asp?pers_id=11773|title=Karen Gren Scholer|work=Texas State Cemetery|access-date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> is an American lawyer who serves as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Texas]].

== Biography ==
[[File:KGS Swearing In Ceremony.jpg|thumb|right|Scholer being sworn in in 2018]]
Scholer received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1979 from [[Rice University]]. She received a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1982 from [[Cornell Law School]]. She started her legal career as an associate at the law firm of Strasburger & Price, LLP, in [[Dallas]], where she was employed from 1982 to 1996. She was elevated to partner in 1989. From 1996 to 2000, she was a partner at the law firm of [[Andrews Kurth]] in [[Houston]].  In private practice, she focused on [[employment law|employment]], [[commercial law|commercial]], and [[competition law|anti trust law]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.martindale.com/attorney/hon-karen-gren-scholer-34110860/|title=Hon. Karen Gren Scholer Profile &#124; Dallas, TX Lawyer &#124; Martindale.com|website=www.martindale.com}}</ref>

In 2000, Scholer was elected as a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] to be a state district judge for the 95th Judicial District Court of [[Dallas County, Texas|Dallas County]] She was re-elected without opposition in 2004, serving in this position until 2008.<ref name=vetting>{{cite news |last1=Voruganti |first1=Harsh |title=Karen Gren Scholer – Nominee for the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Texas |url=https://vettingroom.org/tag/karen-gren-scholer/ |access-date=9 April 2020 |publisher=The Vetting Room |date=October 26, 2017}}</ref> In 2007, she also served as the presiding judge for the Dallas County Civil District Judges. From 2009 to 2013, she was a partner at the law firm of [[Jones Day]]. From 2014 to 2018, Scholer was a principal in the law firm of Carter Scholer Arnett Hamada & Mockler, PLLC, where she specialized in [[business law|business litigation]], complex [[tort]] litigation, and [[alternative dispute resolution]]. She was promoted to a co-[[managing partner]] in 2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2016/03/15/president-obama-nominates-six-serve-united-states-district-courts|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Six to Serve on the United States District Courts|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=March 15, 2016}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==

=== Expired nomination to Eastern District of Texas under Obama ===

On March 15, 2016, President [[Barack Obama]] agreed with [[U.S. Senator]]s [[John Cornyn]] and [[Ted Cruz]] to nominate Scholer to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Texas]] as part of a bipartisan package of nominees which included [[E. Scott Frost]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[Richard A. Schell]], who assumed [[senior status]] on March 10, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2016/03/15/presidential-nominations-sent-senate|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Presidential Nominations Sent to the Senate|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=March 15, 2016}}</ref> On September 7, 2016, a hearing before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] was held on her nomination.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/08/31/2016/nominations-09-07-16|title=Nominations &#124; United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov|date=September 7, 2016 }}</ref> Her nomination generated no controversy,<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.law.com/texaslawyer/2019/09/03/attorney-of-the-year-finalist-karen-gren-scholer/|title=Attorney of the Year Finalist: Karen Gren Scholer|first1=Zack Needles |last1=September 03|first2=2019 at 01:00|last2=AM|website=Texas Lawyer}}</ref> but the Senate had a large backlog of nominees and bills. Her nomination expired on January 3, 2017, with the end of the [[114th United States Congress|114th Congress]].

=== Nomination to Northern District of Texas under Trump ===

On September 7, 2017, Scholer was renominated to a district court by President [[Donald Trump]]. However, she was nominated to serve as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Texas]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[Jorge Antonio Solis]], who retired on May 1, 2016.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-seventh-wave-judicial-candidates/|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Seventh Wave of Judicial Candidates}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/nine-nominations-sent-senate-today/|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Nine Nominations Sent to the Senate Today}}</ref> On October 26, 2017, her nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/10-26-17%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – October 26, 2017|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On March 1, 2018, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on her nomination by a 96–1 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00043|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Karen Gren Scholer to be U.S. District Judge for the Northern District of Texas)|website=United States Senate}}</ref> On March 5, her nomination was confirmed by a 95–0 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1152/vote_115_2_00046.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Karen Gren Scholer, of Texas, to be U.S. District Judge for the Northern District of Texas)|website=United States Senate}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/2018/03/05/senate-confirms-former-dallas-judge-karen-gren-scholer-federal-bench|title=Senate confirms former Dallas judge for federal bench &#124; Politics|publisher=Dallas News|date=2018-01-18|access-date=2018-03-08}}</ref> She received her commission on March 6, 2018,<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=4268601|inline=yes}}</ref> and was sworn into office on March 7, 2018. Scholer is the first [[Asian Americans|Asian-American]] federal district judge in the state of Texas.<ref name="auto"/>

