'''Kelly Harrison Rankin''' (born 1967)<ref name="Questionnaire">{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/rankin_sjq.pdf|title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=January 23, 2024}}</ref> is an American lawyer who is serving as the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Wyoming]]. He previously served as the [[United States attorney]] for that district court from 2008 to 2009 as well as the chief [[United States magistrate judge]] of the same court from 2012 to 2024.

== Education ==

Rankin received a [[Bachelor of Science]] from the [[University of Wyoming]] in 1990 and a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Wyoming College of Law]] in 1994.<ref name="WHBio">{{Cite press release |title=President Biden Names Forty-Third Round of Judicial Nominees |date=December 19, 2023 |publisher=The White House |location=Washington, D.C. |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/12/19/president-biden-names-forty-third-round-of-judicial-nominees/ |access-date=December 19, 2023}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Career ==

From 1994 to 1995, Rankin was a deputy county attorney in the [[Lincoln County, Wyoming|Lincoln County]] Attorney's Office. From 1995 to 1998, he was a deputy county attorney in the [[Park County, Wyoming|Park County]] Attorney's Office and from 1999 to 2003, he served as the Park [[County Attorney]].<ref name = Questionnaire/> From 2003 to 2008, he served as an [[assistant United States attorney]] in the District of Wyoming. From 2008 to 2010, he served as the [[United States attorney]] for the District of Wyoming and then served as counsel to [[Wyoming Governor]] [[Dave Freudenthal]] in 2010. From 2010 to 2012, he served as criminal chief in the U.S. Attorney's Office for the District of Wyoming.<ref name="WHBio" /> On June 7, 2012, he assumed office as a [[United States magistrate judge]] for the District of Wyoming and was chief magistrate judge from 2012 to 2014.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kelly H. Rankin {{!}} United States Courts |url=https://www.uscourts.gov/judicial-milestones/kelly-h-rankin |access-date=December 19, 2023 |website=www.uscourts.gov |language=en}}</ref>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On December 19, 2023, President [[Joe Biden]] announced his intent to nominate Rankin to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Wyoming]].<ref name="WHBio" /> His nomination received support from Senators [[John Barrasso]] and [[Cynthia Lummis]].<ref>{{Cite press release|title=Barrasso Statement on the Nomination of Judge Kelly Rankin for U.S. District Court of Wyoming|url=https://www.barrasso.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/news-releases?ID=061AC203-75D6-4A9D-9991-C24DAA294E16|website=barrasso.senate.gov|access-date=December 19, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite press release|title=Lummis Applauds Rankin Nomination to U.S. District Court|url=https://www.lummis.senate.gov/press-releases/lummis-applauds-rankin-nomination-to-u-s-district-court/|website=lummis.senate.gov|access-date=December 22, 2023}}</ref> On January 10, 2024, his nomination was sent to the Senate. President Biden nominated Rankin to the seat vacated by Judge [[Nancy D. Freudenthal]], who assumed [[senior status]] on June 1, 2022.<ref name=“Nomination”>{{Cite press release |title=Nominations Press Release Attached |date=January 10, 2024|publisher=The White House |location=Washington, D.C. |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2024/01/10/nominations-press-release-attached/}}</ref> On January 24, 2024, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nominations|date=January 23, 2024|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/01/24/2024/nominations}}</ref> On February 29, 2024, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 20–1 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2024-02-29_-_ebm_-_results.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – February 29, 2024|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=February 29, 2024}}</ref> On March 7, 2024, the [[United States Senate]] confirmed his nomination by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/118th-congress/1294|title=PN1294 — Kelly Harrison Rankin — The Judiciary|website=United States Senate|date=March 7, 2024|access-date=March 7, 2024}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on March 12, 2024.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13761536|inline=yes}}</ref>

