'''Kristine Anne Gerhard Baker''' (born 1971) is an American lawyer. She is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Arkansas]].

==Early life and education==

Baker was born Kristine Anne Gerhard in 1971, in [[Colorado Springs, Colorado]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.martindale.com/Kristine-Gerhard-Baker/44782592-lawyer.htm?b=Y&opredir=1|title=Ms. Baker's biography at martindale.com|publisher=|access-date=November 4, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304052801/http://www.martindale.com/Kristine-Gerhard-Baker/44782592-lawyer.htm?b=Y&opredir=1|archive-date=March 4, 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> She received a Bachelor of Arts degree, ''summa cum laude'', in 1993 from [[Saint Louis University (United States)|Saint Louis University]]. She received her Juris Doctor, ''magna cum laude'', in 1996 from the [[University of Arkansas School of Law]]. 

== Career ==
She served as a law clerk for then Chief Judge [[Susan Webber Wright]] of the United States District Court for the Eastern District of Arkansas from 1996 to 1998. She then worked at the law firm of William & Anderson from 1998 to 2000. She joined the law firm of Quattlebaum, Grooms, Tull & Burrow, located in [[Little Rock, Arkansas]] in 2000, and became a partner in 2002. In practice, her legal specialties were commercial litigation, employment law, and First Amendment litigation.<ref name="Whitehouse">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/02/president-obama-nominates-kristine-gerhard-baker-united-states-district-|work=whitehouse.gov|title=President Obama Nominates Kristine Gerhard Baker to United States District Court|via=National Archives|date=November 2, 2011}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393971|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===
On November 2, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Baker to be a District Judge for the United States District Court for the Eastern District of Arkansas.<ref name="Whitehouse" /> She replaced District Judge [[James Maxwell Moody]] who took senior status on October 1, 2008. She received her hearing by the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on January 26, 2012, and her nomination was reported to the floor on February 16, 2012, by voice vote, with Senator [[Mike Lee]] recording the only no vote. On May 7, 2012, her nomination was confirmed by voice vote. She received her commission on May 8, 2012.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> She became chief judge on December 18, 2023.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Chief United States District Judge, Eastern District of Arkansas |date=December 18, 2023 |publisher=United States District Court for the Eastern District of Arkansas |url=https://www.are.uscourts.gov/news/chief-united-states-district-judge-eastern-district-arkansas |access-date=January 1, 2024}}</ref>

===Notable case===

On April 15, 2017, Baker blocked executions by the State of Arkansas, citing concerns over the use of [[midazolam]], a drug used for sedation. Arkansas had planned to execute eight prisoners in the two weeks before the expiration date on one of the state's lethal injection drugs.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Blinder|first1=Alan|title=Federal Judge Blocks Arkansas Executions|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/15/us/arkansas-executions.html|access-date=April 18, 2017|work=The New York Times|date=April 16, 2017|page=A1}}</ref>

