'''Linda Vivienne Parker''' (born 1958) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Michigan]].

==Biography==

Parker received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1980 from the [[University of Michigan]]. She received a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1983 from [[George Washington University Law School]]. From 1983 to 1985, she served as a [[law clerk]] to the [[District of Columbia Superior Court]]. She worked as a staff attorney at the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency]] from 1985 to 1989. From 1989 to 1994, she worked at the [[Detroit]] law firm of [[Dickinson Wright]], becoming a partner in 1992. From 1994 to 2000, she served as an executive [[assistant United States attorney]] in the Eastern District of Michigan. She served as director of the Michigan Department of Civil Rights from 2003 to 2008. From 2009 to 2014, she served as a judge of the Third Judicial Circuit Court of Michigan.<ref name="Whitehouse">{{cite web |url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/07/25/president-obama-nominates-six-serve-united-states-district-court |work=[[whitehouse.gov]] |title=President Obama Nominates Six to Serve on the United States District Court |via=[[National Archives and Records Administration]] |date=July 25, 2013 |publisher=[[White House Office of the Press Secretary]]}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394406|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On July 25, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Parker to serve as a United States District Judge of the United States District Court for the Eastern District of Michigan, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Robert Hardy Cleland]], who assumed [[senior status]] on March 1, 2013.<ref name="Whitehouse" /> On January 16, 2014, her nomination was reported out of committee by a 14–3 vote.<ref>{{cite web|title=Executive Business Meeting|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/ExecutiveBusinessMeetingResults-01-16-2014.pdf|publisher=[[United States Senate]]|work=[[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Committee on the Judiciary]]|access-date=January 16, 2014}}</ref> On March 11, 2014, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on her nomination by a 56–42 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1132/vote_113_2_00066.htm|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Nomination of Linda Vivienne Parker, of Michigan, to be U.S. District Judge)|publisher=[[United States Senate]]|date=March 12, 2014|access-date=November 5, 2022}}</ref> On March 12, 2014, her nomination was confirmed by a 60–37 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00071|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Linda Vivienne Parker, of Michigan, to be U.S. District Judge for the Eastern District of Michigan)|publisher=[[United States Senate]]|date=March 12, 2014|access-date=January 30, 2015}}</ref> She received her judicial commission on March 17, 2014.<ref name="fjc.gov" /> Her primary chambers are in [[Detroit, Michigan|Detroit]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Judge Linda V. Parker |url=https://www.mied.uscourts.gov/index.cfm?pageFunction=chambers&judgeid=46 |website=United States District Court – Eastern Michigan District |publisher=[[United States District Court for the Eastern District of Michigan]] |access-date=March 29, 2018}}</ref>

====Notable rulings====

In December 2020, Parker heard a lawsuit brought by attorneys for then-president [[Donald Trump]], attempting to reverse the Michigan results for the [[2020 U.S. presidential election]], one of the so-called "Kraken" suits. She dismissed the suit, saying it was full of "theories, conjecture and speculation" and adding that "the people of Michigan have spoken."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://detroit.cbslocal.com/2020/12/07/federal-judge-upholds-michigan-election-results-saying-the-people-have-spoken/ |title=Federal Judge Upholds Michigan Election Results Saying 'The People Have Spoken' |last=Brown |first=Bria |date=December 7, 2021 |work=[[CBS Detroit]] |access-date=July 14, 2021}}</ref>

In July 2021, Parker ordered all the attorneys who had argued Trump's case to appear before her, as she considered motions filed to impose sanctions on the attorneys for arguing that Trump had won the election and presenting evidence that she said they had not evaluated for accuracy.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/sidney-powell-disciplinary-hearing/2021/07/12/61d44ac4-e0c9-11eb-9f54-7eee10b5fcd2_story.html |title='This is really fantastical': Federal judge in Michigan presses Trump-allied lawyers on 2020 election fraud claims in sanctions hearing |last=Helderman |first=Rosalind A. |date=July 12, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=July 14, 2021}}</ref> On August 25, 2021, Parker ruled that "Plaintiffs' counsel filed this lawsuit in bad faith and for an improper purpose", demanding that the attorneys, including [[Sidney Powell]] and [[Lin Wood]], pay the legal fees of some of the defendants in the suit, and that they be referred to their respective state bars for disciplinary action.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/08/25/politics/judge-sanctions-powell-wood-kraken-lawsuits/index.html |title=Judge sanctions pro-Trump lawyers who brought 'frivolous' election fraud lawsuits |last=Sneed |first=Tierney |date=August 25, 2021 |work=[[CNN]] |access-date=August 25, 2021}}</ref>

