'''Lindsey Ann Freeman'''<ref>{{cite web |author=<!-- not stated --> |date=May 27, 2025 |title=UNITED STATES OF AMERICA, Plaintiff - Appellee, v. LUIS FRANCISCO SANCHEZ, JR., Defendant - Appellant. |url=https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/244458.U.pdf |website=www.ca4.gov |location= |publisher=United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit |access-date=August 23, 2025}}</ref> (born 1983) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of North Carolina]]. She previously served as an [[assistant United States attorney]] in the Middle District of North Carolina.

==Early life and education==

Freeman was born in 1983 in [[Austin, Texas]].<ref name="SJCQ">{{cite web |date=September 18, 2025 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/freeman_sjq.pdf |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=September 18, 2025}}</ref> She received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, ''[[magna cum laude]]'', in 2005 from [[Harvard University]], majoring in [[social studies]]. She received a [[Juris Doctor]], ''[[magna cum laude]]'' and ''[[Order of the Coif]]'', in 2011 from the [[University of Pennsylvania Law School]], where she served as comments editor of the [[University of Pennsylvania Law Review]]. She served as a [[law clerk]] in the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]] from 2011 to 2012.<ref name="SJCQ"/>

==Career==

In 2012, Freeman was hired as [[counsel]] for the law firm of [[O'Melveny & Myers LLP]], where she focused on [[antitrust]], [[product liability]], and [[white collar criminal]] law.<ref name="SJCQ"/>

Freeman served as [[counsel]] in the [[Office of Legal Policy]] in the [[United States Department of Justice]] from 2017 to 2019, Chief of Staff of that same office from 2019 to 2020 and Chief of Staff and Deputy Attorney General from 2020 to 2021.<ref name="SJCQ"/> In 2021, she became an [[Assistant United States Attorney]] in the Middle District of North Carolina.

=== Federal judicial service ===

On August 22, 2025, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Freeman to a seat on the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of North Carolina]] vacated by [[Catherine Eagles]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://breakingthenews.net/Article/Trump-nominates-new-district-court-judges/64698461|title=Trump nominates new district court judges|website=breakingthenews.net}}</ref> The nomination was transmitted to the [[United States Senate]] on September 15, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/520/2|title=PN520-2 - Nomination of Lindsey Ann Freeman for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=September 17, 2025|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref>  On September 17, 2025, a confirmation hearing was held for her and other nominees before the Senate Judiciary Committee<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/nominations-09-17-2025|title=LOCATION CHANGE: Nominations &#124; United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov}}</ref> On October 9, 2025, the committee advanced her nomination by a 15–7 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 9, 2025 |title=RESULTS OF EXECUTIVE BUSINESS MEETING |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/7724bc11-ea93-6871-6706-e3abbb21e374/2025-10-09_EBM_Results.pdf |access-date=December 4, 2025 |website=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary}}</ref> On December 2, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on her nomination by a 61–36 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 2, 2025 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Lindsey Ann Freeman to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of North Carolina) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00626.htm |access-date=December 4, 2025 |publisher=United States Senate}}</ref> Later that day, her nomination was confirmed by a 60–39 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 2, 2025 |title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Lindsey Ann Freeman, of North Carolina, to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of North Carolina)|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00627.htm |access-date=December 4, 2025 |publisher=United States Senate}}</ref> She received her judicial commission on December 19, 2025.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762086|inline=yes}}</ref>

