'''Lorna Gail Tiangco Schofield'''<ref>{{cite web | url=https://usa.inquirer.net/10419/first-fil-federal-judge-lorna-schofield-no-filipino-consciousness-growing | title=First Fil-Am Federal Judge Lorna Schofield: 'I had no Filipino consciousness growing up' | date=February 23, 2018 | access-date=October 24, 2018 | archive-date=October 24, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024035513/https://usa.inquirer.net/10419/first-fil-federal-judge-lorna-schofield-no-filipino-consciousness-growing | url-status=live }}</ref> (born 1956) is a [[Senior status|senior]] [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York]].

==Biography==

Schofield was born in 1956,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lawyercentral.com/lorna-g-schofield-interactive-profile--20-481836.html|title=Attorney Lorna G Schofield – Lawyer in New York NY|website=www.lawyercentral.com|access-date=March 28, 2016|archive-date=April 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160408025639/http://www.lawyercentral.com/lorna-g-schofield-interactive-profile--20-481836.html|url-status=live}}</ref> grew up in [[New Haven, Indiana]], and graduated from New Haven Senior High School in 1974. She was elected as [[Governor]] of [[Boys/Girls State|Hoosier Girls State]], a program of the American Legion Auxiliary, in 1973. Schofield received her [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree from [[Indiana University]] in 1977. She received her [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[New York University School of Law]] in 1981.

From 1981 to 1984, she was an associate at the law firm of [[Cleary, Gottlieb, Steen & Hamilton]]. From 1984 to 1988, she served as an [[assistant United States attorney]] for the Southern District of New York, prosecuting domestic terrorism, smuggling and tax fraud. She became an associate at the law firm of [[Debevoise & Plimpton]] LLP in [[New York City]] in 1988 and was promoted to partner in 1991. She specialized in complex civil litigation and white collar [[criminal defense]].

In addition, she has been heavily involved with the [[American Bar Association]], holding a number of leadership positions, including Chair of the Section on Litigation.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2012/04/25/president-obama-nominates-four-serve-us-district-court|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Four to Serve on the US District Court|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=April 25, 2012|access-date=March 3, 2021|archive-date=January 19, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170119090450/https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2012/04/25/president-obama-nominates-four-serve-us-district-court|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394086|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On April 25, 2012, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Schofield to serve as a United States district judge of the United States District Court for the Southern District of New York, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Shira A. Scheindlin]]. Schofield is the first Filipino American in the history of the United States to serve as an Article III federal judge.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.napaba.org/napaba/showpage.asp?code=PR-lornaschofield012312|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120223031347/http://www.napaba.org/napaba/showpage.asp?code=PR-lornaschofield012312|url-status=dead|archive-date=February 23, 2012|title=National Asian Pacific American Bar Association}}</ref> Schofield testified before the United States Senate Judiciary Committee on June 6, 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/hearings/hearing.cfm?id=4ade2847e53a40f8a7873e62d6e0d7ba|title=U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee|publisher=|access-date=June 7, 2012|archive-date=September 15, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120915121128/http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/hearings/hearing.cfm?id=4ade2847e53a40f8a7873e62d6e0d7ba|url-status=live}}</ref> The committee reported Schofield's nomination to the full Senate on July 12, 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/Materials112thCongress.cfm|title=Judicial Nominations Materials: 112th Congress|access-date=December 13, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121212042626/http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/Materials112thCongress.cfm |archive-date=December 12, 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Senate confirmed Schofield on December 13, 2012, by a 91–0 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1122/vote_112_2_00228.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Lorna G. Schofield, of New York, to be U.S. District Judge)|access-date=November 5, 2022|archive-date=November 6, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221106020920/https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1122/vote_112_2_00228.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> She received her commission on December 13, 2012. She assumed [[senior status]] on January 1, 2025.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

