'''Matthew Emile Orso'''<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://portal.ncbar.gov/error.html?aspxerrorpath=/Verification/viewer.aspx|title=North Carolina State Bar|website=portal.ncbar.gov}}</ref> (born 1978) is an American lawyer who serves as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of North Carolina]].

==Early life and education==

Orso was born in 1978 in [[Highland, Illinois]].<ref name="SJCQ">{{cite web |last=Orso |first=Matthew Emile |date=September 18, 2025 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/orso_sjq.pdf |website=www.judiciary.senate.gov |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=September 18, 2025}}</ref> He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, ''[[cum laude]]'', in 2001 from the [[University of Dayton]], majoring in history. He received a [[Juris Doctor]], ''[[magna cum laude]]'', in 2009 from the ''[[Saint Louis University School of Law]]'', where he served as [[editor in chief]] of the [[Saint Louis University Law Journal]]. He served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[Robert J. Conrad]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of North Carolina]] from 2009 to 2011.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.troutman.com/professionals/matthew-e-orso.html|title=Matthew E. Orso|website=Troutman Pepper Locke - Matthew E. Orso}}</ref>

==Career==

From 2011 to 2012, Orso worked as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] for Smith Parsons, PLLC in [[Charlotte, North Carolina|Charlotte]]. From 2012 to 2023, he worked as an associate and, from 2018, [[Law firm#Partnership|partner]] for [[McGuireWoods]] LLP in its Charlotte office.  From 2023 to 2025, Orso was a partner with the [[law firm]] of [[Troutman Pepper Locke]] at its [[Charlotte, North Carolina|Charlotte]] office.<ref name="SJCQ"/> He focused on [[regulatory law]], [[administrative law]],and [[white collar criminal defense]], and he often advised clients who were subject to regulatory inquiry and prosecution.<ref name="auto"/>

===Federal judicial service===

On August 22, 2025, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Orso to a seat on the [[United States District Court for the Western District of North Carolina]] vacated by Judge [[Robert J. Conrad]]. The nomination was transmitted to the [[United States Senate]] on September 15, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/520/3|title=PN520-3 - Nomination of Matthew E. Orso for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=September 17, 2025|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref> On October 9, 2025, his nomination was favorably reported out of committee by a 14–8 vote.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/7724bc11-ea93-6871-6706-e3abbb21e374/2025-10-09_EBM_Results.pdf | title=Results of executive business meeting | date=October 9, 2025 | website=www.judiciary.senate.gov}}</ref>  On December 3, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 56–40 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 3, 2025 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Matthew E. Orso to be U.S. District Judge for the Western District of North Carolina) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00628.htm |access-date=December 14, 2025 |website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> Later that day, his nomination was confirmed by a 57–41 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 3, 2025 |title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Matthew E. Orso, of North Carolina, to be U.S. District Judge for the Western District of North Carolina) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00629.htm |access-date=December 14, 2025 |website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on December 12, 2025.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762088|inline=yes}}</ref>

