'''Matthew William Brann''' (born July 25, 1965) is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Pennsylvania]].

==Early life and education==
Brann was born in [[Elmira, New York]], on July 25, 1965.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bwcs-law.com/attorney.html|title=Biography from Brann, Williams, Caldwell & Sheetz|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111222171610/http://www.bwcs-law.com/attorney.html|archive-date=2011-12-22}}</ref> He received his [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1987 from the [[University of Notre Dame]]. He received his [[Juris Doctor]] in 1990 from [[Penn State Dickinson Law]] at [[Pennsylvania State University]].

== Career ==
From 1990 to 1991, Brann served as a [[law clerk]] with the Court of Common Pleas in [[Bradford County, Pennsylvania]]. He became an associate at Brann, Williams, Caldwell & Sheetz in 1991 and a partner at that firm in 1995. He also spent years as a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] official in [[Pennsylvania]] and was active in the [[Federalist Society]] and [[National Rifle Association]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.citizensvoice.com/news/judiciary-committee-hears-from-two-pennsylvania-nominees-for-federal-bench/article_be7de885-1d45-5e16-af73-965b1c077a53.html|date=June 27, 2012|title=Judiciary committee hears from two Pennsylvania nominees for federal bench|first=Laura|last=Legere|website=Wilkes-Barre Citizens' Voice|access-date=November 22, 2020|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117220430/https://www.citizensvoice.com/news/judiciary-committee-hears-from-two-pennsylvania-nominees-for-federal-bench/article_be7de885-1d45-5e16-af73-965b1c077a53.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In private practice, he focused on tort, contract, commercial, and real estate litigation.<ref name="Whitehouse">{{cite press release|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2012/05/17/president-obama-nominates-two-serve-us-district-court|title=President Obama Nominates Two to Serve on the US District Court|date=17 May 2012|access-date=17 May 2012|archive-date=16 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216162645/https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2012/05/17/president-obama-nominates-two-serve-us-district-court|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394106|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On May 17, 2012, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Brann to be a United States District Judge for the United States District Court for the Middle District of Pennsylvania, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Thomas I. Vanaskie]], who was elevated to the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]] in 2010. Senator [[Pat Toomey]] recommended Brann as part of a paired nomination with a recommendation by Senator [[Bob Casey Jr.]]<ref>{{Cite web|title=Senate Confirms PA Middle District Judicial Nominees|url=https://www.casey.senate.gov/newsroom/releases/senate-confirms-pa-middle-district-judicial-nominees|access-date=2020-11-22|website=Sen. Bob Casey|language=en|archive-date=November 11, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111142442/https://www.casey.senate.gov/newsroom/releases/senate-confirms-pa-middle-district-judicial-nominees|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Tweet by Max Kennerly|url=https://twitter.com/maxkennerly/status/1330308465600974848|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122175543/https://twitter.com/maxkennerly/status/1330308465600974848|archive-date=November 22, 2020|access-date=2020-11-22|website=Twitter|language=en}}</ref> The [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] held a hearing on his nomination on June 27, 2012, and reported it to the floor on July 19.<ref name="Whitehouse" /> The Senate confirmed his nomination by [[unanimous consent]] on December 21. Brann received his commission on December 27.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> He entered on duty as a United States District Judge on January 17, 2013. He sits at the United States Courthouse and Federal Building in [[Williamsport, Pennsylvania]].<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

In September 2019, a three-judge panel of the Third Circuit Court of Appeals admonished Brann for making conclusions a jury should address and for glossing over serious disputes regarding the facts of a case.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Beauge|first=John|date=2019-09-13|title=Appeals court admonishes judge for ruling on suit filed by two fired Pa. prison guards|url=https://www.pennlive.com/news/2019/09/appeals-court-admonishes-judge-for-ruling-on-suit-filed-by-two-fired-pa-prison-guards.html|access-date=2020-11-27|website=pennlive|language=en-US|location=Williamsport|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190917232101/https://www.pennlive.com/news/2019/09/appeals-court-admonishes-judge-for-ruling-on-suit-filed-by-two-fired-pa-prison-guards.html|archive-date=2019-09-17|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Notable rulings===
{{See also|Post-election lawsuits related to the 2020 United States presidential election#Donald J. Trump for President v. Boockvar}}
Brann presided over the case ''Donald J. Trump for President v. Boockvar'', in which the [[Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign]] sought to block the certification of the nearly seven million [[2020 United States presidential election in Pennsylvania|presidential election]] votes cast in Pennsylvania.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/election-results/pennsylvania-2020/|title=Pennsylvania 2020 live election results|newspaper=The Washington Post|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210107025734/https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/election-results/pennsylvania-2020/|archive-date=January 7, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> This case was one of the [[Post-election lawsuits related to the 2020 United States presidential election|lawsuits challenging results]] from states [[Joe Biden]] won in that election. During an appearance before the court, Trump's lead attorney, [[Rudy Giuliani]], said there was "widespread, nationwide voter fraud", but under questioning by Brann, Giuliani said, "This is not a fraud case."<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://time.com/5914377/donald-trump-no-evidence-fraud/|title=In Court, Trump's Lawyers Aren't Claiming 'Massive' Fraud|magazine=Time|date=November 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201120201822/https://time.com/5914377/donald-trump-no-evidence-fraud|archive-date=November 20, 2020|url-status=live}}</ref>

Brann dismissed the case [[Prejudice (legal term)|with prejudice]] on November 21, 2020, citing "strained legal arguments without merit and speculative accusations" that were "unsupported by evidence". He also ruled that an evidentiary hearing was not needed.<ref>{{cite web |author1=Judge Brann |title=DONALD J. TRUMP v KATHY BOOCKVAR |url=https://www.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057.202.0_1.pdf |access-date=November 22, 2020 |pages=10, 11 |language=en |date=November 21, 2020 |quote=At the conclusion of the argument, I determined that an evidentiary hearing (previously scheduled to take place on November 19, 2020) was no longer needed and cancelled that proceeding. Instead, I imposed a new briefing schedule in light of the FAC's filing, which arguably mooted the initial motions to dismiss.}}</ref> He said the Trump team's argument "like [[Frankenstein's Monster]], has been haphazardly stitched together from two distinct theories in an attempt to avoid controlling precedent" and characterized the requested remedy to disqualify the votes as "unhinged from the underlying right being asserted".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/us-judge-dismisses-trump-campaign-lawsuit-in-pa/2020/11/21/cc097fbe-2c50-11eb-9b14-ad872157ebc9_story.html|title=In scathing opinion, federal judge dismisses Trump campaign lawsuit in Pennsylvania|first=Jon|last=Swaine|newspaper=The Washington Post|access-date=November 22, 2020|archive-date=November 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122031105/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/us-judge-dismisses-trump-campaign-lawsuit-in-pa/2020/11/21/cc097fbe-2c50-11eb-9b14-ad872157ebc9_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.inquirer.com/news/pennsylvania-election-lawsuit-trump-dismissed-matthew-brann-certification-vote-results-20201121.html|title='Not how the Constitution works': Federal judge tosses Trump suit seeking to disrupt Pa. election results|first=Jeremy|last=Roebuck|work=Philadelphia Inquirer|date=November 21, 2020|access-date=November 22, 2020|archive-date=November 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122042037/https://www.inquirer.com/news/pennsylvania-election-lawsuit-trump-dismissed-matthew-brann-certification-vote-results-20201121.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=''Donald J. Trump for President, Inc. v. Boockvar'', No. 4:20-CV-02078 (M.D. Pa. 2020)|url=https://www.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057.202.0_1.pdf|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122001656/https://www.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057/gov.uscourts.pamd.127057.202.0_1.pdf|archive-date=November 22, 2020}}</ref>

The Trump campaign appealed to the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]], where a three-judge panel ruled on November 27 that the District Court under Brann had been correct in preventing the Trump campaign from amending its complaint a second time.<ref name=Watson1>{{cite news |last1=Watson |first1=Kathryn |title=Trump loses appeal in Pennsylvania: "Calling an election unfair does not make it so" |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-pennsylvania-appeal-loss-2020-election/ |access-date=November 30, 2020 |work=[[CBS News]] |date=November 28, 2020}}</ref><ref name=Tide>{{cite news |title=Federal appeals court slams Trump campaign efforts to turn tide in Pennsylvania ruling, saying 'claims have no merit' |url=https://lite.cnn.com/en/article/h_7b19716ee9d6faa1fb9171e7e318c9bf |access-date=November 30, 2020 |work=[[CNN]] |date=November 27, 2020 |archive-date=November 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201127224950/http://lite.cnn.com/en/article/h_7b19716ee9d6faa1fb9171e7e318c9bf |url-status=dead }}</ref> The panel also praised the District Court under Brann, saying it had demonstrated "fast, fair, patient handling of this demanding litigation".<ref name=Tide/>

On August 21, 2025, Brann ruled in a 77-page order that President [[Donald Trump]] had unlawfully extended [[Alina Habba]]'s appointment as United States Attorney for New Jersey.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rivard |first1=Ry |title=Alina Habba 'unlawfully' working as US attorney in New Jersey, judge rules |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/08/21/alina-habba-new-jersey-us-attorney-ruling-00518559 |access-date=22 August 2025 |agency=Politico}}</ref><ref>''United States v. Giraud''. No. 1:24-CR-00768 https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.njd.557842/gov.uscourts.njd.557842.144.0.pdf 795 F. Supp. 3d 560, 568-73 (D.N.J. 2025).</ref> Habba had initially held the position under a 120-day interim appointment, but was never confirmed by the U.S. Senate. Brann wrote, "Faced with the question of whether Ms. Habba is lawfully performing the functions and duties of the office of the United States Attorney for the District of New Jersey, I conclude that she is not".

On March 9, 2026, Brann ruled that a triumvirate of attorneys was unconstitutionally appointed to lead the office of the U.S. Attorney for New Jersey.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bromwich |first=Jonah E. |last2=Tully |first2=Tracey |date=2026-03-09 |title=In Scathing Ruling, Judge Says 3 Trump Prosecutors Are in Unlawful Roles |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/09/nyregion/us-attorney-nj-prosecutors.html |access-date=2026-03-10 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>''United States v. Naviwala''. No. 2:24-CR-00099. ''United States v. Torres''. No. 2:24-CR-00378. https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.njd.540663/gov.uscourts.njd.540663.317.0_2.pdf</ref>

