'''Myong Jin Joun''' (born 1971)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees|date=November 15, 2022|publisher=[[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary]]|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Joun%20SJQ%20Public%20Final.pdf}}</ref> is an American lawyer serving as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts]]. He previously served as an associate judge of the [[Boston Municipal Court]].

== Education ==

Joun earned a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree from the [[University of Massachusetts Boston]] in 1994 and a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[Suffolk University Law School]] in 1999.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 29, 2022 |title=President Biden Names Twenty-Fourth Round of Judicial Nominees |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/07/29/president-biden-names-twenty-fourth-round-of-judicial-nominees/ |access-date=July 29, 2022 |website=The White House |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Career ==

From 1999 to 2007, Joun worked as an  associate at Howard Friedman P.C. in [[Brookline, Massachusetts|Brookline]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Congratulations to our former colleague Myong Joun on becoming a Judge - Blog - Law Offices of Howard Friedman, P.C. |url=http://www.civil-rights-law.com/blog/2015/1/12/congratulations-to-our-former-colleague-myong-joun-on-becomi.html |access-date=July 29, 2022 |website=www.civil-rights-law.com |language=en}}</ref> He was a solo practitioner from 2007 to 2014. In 2014, Governor [[Deval Patrick]] nominated Joun to serve as an associate judge of the [[Boston Municipal Court]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Myong Joun |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Myong_Joun |access-date=July 29, 2022 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref> Joun also served in the [[United States Army]] and [[Massachusetts National Guard]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Biden nominates local judge for federal appointment to Southern District of Ohio bench |url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/biden-nominates-local-judge-federal-235025034.html |access-date=July 31, 2022 |website=finance.yahoo.com |date=July 31, 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref> serving in the [[26th Infantry Division (United States)#Cold War|1-101st Battalion]] ([[26th Infantry Division (United States)|26th Infantry Division]]) and the [[182nd Infantry Regiment (United States)#Back to infantry|1-182nd Battalion]] ([[42nd Infantry Division (United States)|42nd Infantry Division]]).<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-07-18 |title=Judge Myong Joun |url=https://afj.org/nominee/judge-myong-joun/ |access-date=2024-12-02 |website=Alliance for Justice |language=en-US}}</ref> Joun has worked with the [[American Civil Liberties Union|ACLU]] of Massachusetts,<ref>{{cite web | url=https://lawlink.com/profile/5602/myong-joun | title=Myong Joun - Criminal Law Lawyer in Arlington, Massachusetts (MA) }}</ref> the [[National Lawyers Guild]],<ref name="NAPABA">{{Cite press release |title=NAPABA Applauds the Nominations of Justice Myong J. Joun and Judge Rita F. Lin |date=July 29, 2022 |publisher=National Asian Pacific American Bar Association |url=https://www.napaba.org/news/612743/-NAPABA-Applauds-the-Nominations-of-Justice-Myong-J.-Joun-and-Judge-Rita-F.-Lin.htm |access-date=August 4, 2022}}</ref> the Massachusetts Law Reform Institute, the [[American Bar Foundation]] and the Massachusetts Bar Foundation.<ref name="NAPABA" />

=== Federal judicial service ===

On July 29, 2022, President [[Joe Biden]] announced his intention to nominate Joun to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts]]. On August 1, 2022, his nomination was sent to the Senate. He has been nominated to a seat vacated by Judge [[George A. O'Toole Jr.]], who assumed [[senior status]] on January 1, 2018.<ref>{{Cite press release|title=Nominations Sent to the Senate|date=August 1, 2022|publisher=The White House|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/08/01/nominations-sent-to-the-senate-78/}}</ref> On November 15, 2022, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nominations|date=November 15, 2022|publisher=[[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary]]|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/11/15/2022/nominations}}</ref> On December 8, 2022, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 12–10 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/EBM%20Results%20-%202022-12-08.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – December 8, 2022|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|accessdate=December 8, 2022}}</ref> On January 3, 2023, his nomination was returned to the President under [[Standing Rules of the United States Senate, Rule XXXI|Rule XXXI, Paragraph 6]] of the [[United States Senate]]. He was renominated on January 23, 2023.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Nominations Sent to the Senate |date=January 23, 2023 |publisher=The White House |location=Washington, D.C. |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/01/23/nominations-sent-to-the-senate-92/}}</ref> On February 9, 2023, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 12–9 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2023-02-09%20-%20EBM%20-%20Results.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – February 9, 2023|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|accessdate=February 9, 2023}}</ref> On July 12, 2023, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 51–46 vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 12, 2023 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Myong J. Joun to be United States District Judge for the District of Massachusetts) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1181/vote_118_1_00181.htm |access-date=July 12, 2023 |website=United States Senate}}</ref> Later that day, his nomination was confirmed by a 52–46 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1181/vote_118_1_00182.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Myong J. Joun, of Massachusetts, to be United States District Judge for the District of Massachusetts)|website=United States Senate|date=July 12, 2023|access-date=July 12, 2023}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on July 14, 2023.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13760711|inline=yes}}</ref> He became the first [[Asian American]] man on the federal bench in Massachusetts.<ref name="Reuters">{{Cite web|last1=Raymond|first1=Nate|date=July 29, 2022|title=Biden nominates abortion rights lawyer in U.S. Supreme Court case to federal judgeship|url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/world/us/biden-nominates-abortion-rights-lawyer-us-supreme-court-case-federal-judgeship-2022-07-29/|access-date=July 29, 2022|website=Reuters|language=en}}</ref>

==== Notable rulings ====
{{See also|Department of Education v. California|United States Department of Education#2025 workforce reduction and reorganization|label 2=McMahon v. New York}}
In March 2025, Joun issued a temporary restraining order on the freezing of teacher training grants that were deemed by the Trump administration to include funding for [[diversity, equity, and inclusion]], but on April 4, 2025, the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] reinstated the freeze in a 5–4 decision.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Liptak |first=Adam |last2=VanSickle |first2=Abbie |date=2025-04-04 |title=Supreme Court Lets Trump Suspend Grants to Teachers |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/04/us/supreme-court-trump-teacher-grants.html |access-date=2025-04-05 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

