'''Nancy Ellen Brasel'''<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Brasel%20SJQ.pdf United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees]</ref> (born 1969) is a [[United States federal judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Minnesota]].

== Early life and education ==
Born in [[England]],<ref>{{cite web |last1= Voruganti |first1= Harsh |title= Judge Nancy Brasel – Nominee to the U.S. District Court for the District of Minnesota |url= https://vettingroom.org/2018/04/09/judge-nancy-brasel/#ftnt1 |publisher= Vetting Room |access-date= July 10, 2018 |date= April 9, 2018}}</ref> Brasel earned her [[Bachelor of Arts]] from [[Trinity University (Texas)|Trinity University]] in 1991, where she was inducted into [[Phi Beta Kappa]], her [[Master of Arts]] from the [[University of Texas at Austin]] in 1993, and her [[Juris Doctor]], ''[[magna cum laude]]'', from the [[University of Minnesota]] in 1996, where she was inducted into the [[Order of the Coif]] and served as a managing editor of the ''[[Minnesota Law Review]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Montemayer |first1= Stephen |title= Trump picks Hennepin County judge, Minneapolis attorney for federal judgeships |url= http://www.startribune.com/trump-picks-hennepin-county-judge-minneapolis-attorney-for-federal-judgeships/473828593/ |access-date= July 10, 2018 |publisher= Star Tribune |date= February 12, 2018}}</ref>

==Career==
After graduating from law school, Brasel served as a [[law clerk]] to Judge [[Donald P. Lay]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit]] from 1996 to 1997. After her clerkship, Brasel worked in private practice as an [[associate lawyer|associate]] with the [[Minneapolis]] law firm of Leonard, Street and Deinard (now Stinson).

In 1999, Brasel became a [[law firm partner|partner]] at Greene Espel, P.L.L.P., where her practice focused on [[business law]] and [[employment law]]. Before her judicial appointment, she spent three years as an [[assistant United States Attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the District of Minnesota]], where she prosecuted [[drug law|narcotics]] and [[firearms law|firearms]] offenses and [[white-collar crime]]. 

In 2011, [[Minnesota Governor]] [[Mark Dayton]] appointed Brasel to the state [[District Court of Minnesota]] for the Fourth Judicial District in [[Hennepin County]], where she served until becoming a federal judge.<ref>[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-eleventh-wave-judicial-nominees/ "President Donald J. Trump Announces Eleventh Wave of Judicial Nominees" White House, February 12, 2018] {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Nelson |first1= Tim |title= Trump picks two new federal judges for Minn. |url= https://www.mprnews.org/story/2018/02/12/trump-picks-federal-judges-for-minnesota |access-date= July 10, 2018 |publisher= Minnesota Public Radio |date= February 12, 2018}}</ref> She is a former chair of the board of directors for the Domestic Abuse Project in Minneapolis.<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2011-09-14 |title= Gov. Dayton appoints Nancy Brasel, Philip Carruthers to fill Fourth Judicial District vacancies |url= https://www.leg.mn.gov/docs/2018/other/181224/governor/newsroom/index2c5f.htm |url-status= live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240805001205/https://www.leg.mn.gov/docs/2018/other/181224/governor/newsroom/index2c5f.htm |archive-date= 2024-08-05 |access-date= 2026-05-25 |publisher= Office of Governor Mark Dayton and Lt. Governor Tina Smith}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==
In 2017, Senator [[Amy Klobuchar]] recommended Brasel to the [[First presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]] as a federal judge.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://vettingroom.org/2018/04/09/judge-nancy-brasel/ |title= Judge Nancy Brasel – Nominee to the U.S. District Court for the District of Minnesota |date= April 9, 2018}}</ref> On February 12, 2018, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Brasel to an undetermined seat on the [[United States District Court for the District of Minnesota]] as part of a bipartisan package of judicial nominees that included Minneapolis lawyer [[Eric Tostrud]]. On February 15, her nomination was sent to the Senate. Trump nominated Brasel to the seat vacated by Judge [[Ann D. Montgomery]], who assumed [[senior status]] on May 31, 2016.<ref>[https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/1643 PN1643 — Nancy E. Brasel — The Judiciary, congress.gov]</ref> Brasel was rated "unanimously well qualified" by the [[American Bar Association]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= Montemayor |first1= Stephen |title= Minnesota federal judicial nominees tested on Capitol Hill |url=http://www.startribune.com/minnesota-federal-judicial-nominees-tested-on-capitol-hill/479422633/ |access-date= July 10, 2018 |publisher= Star Tribune |date= April 11, 2018}}</ref> On April 11, 2018, a hearing on her nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/04/11/2018/nominations-1 United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for April 11, 2018]</ref> On May 10, her nomination was reported out of committee by [[voice vote]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting%2005-10-18.pdf Results of Executive Business Meeting – May 10, 2018, Senate Judiciary Committee]</ref> On August 28, her nomination was confirmed by voice vote.<ref>{{Cite web |url= https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/1643|title=PN1643 — Nancy E. Brasel — The Judiciary |date= February 15, 2018 |website= [[United States Senate]] |access-date= August 28, 2018 |language= en}}</ref> She received her judicial commission on September 13, 2018.<ref>{{FJC Bio |nid= 5153461|inline= yes}}</ref>

=== Notable rulings ===
In October 2020, Brasel upheld a state court ruling that ballots received seven days after the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 election]] must be counted.<ref>{{Cite web |date= October 12, 2020 |title= Federal judge upholds counting of late-arriving ballots in Minnesota |url= https://www.minnpost.com/glean/2020/10/federal-judge-upholds-counting-of-late-arriving-ballots-in-minnesota/ |access-date= October 13, 2020 |website= MinnPost |language= en-US}}</ref>

In February 2026, Brasel ruled that [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) had likely violated the constitutional rights of thousands of detainees at the [[Whipple Building|Bishop Henry Whipple Federal Building]] in Minneapolis.<ref name= ":0">{{Cite web |last= Karnowski |first= Steve |last2= Boone |first2= Rebecca |date= 2026-02-13 |title= A judge orders DHS to give Minnesota detainees swift access to lawyers before transfers |url= https://apnews.com/article/whipple-minnesota-detainees-attorneys-lawsuit-d2af86f555357badfb02b14953c02a08 |access-date= 2026-02-13 |website= AP News |language= en}}</ref> Brasel's emergency restraining order noted that detainees faced numerous barriers to accessing legal counsel and that "in planning for [[Operation Metro Surge]], the government failed to plan for the constitutional rights of its civil detainees".<ref name= ":0" /> Her order required the government to give non-citizens in custody the opportunity to contact legal counsel within an hour of their detention and before any transfer out of state.<ref name= ":0" />

In May 2026, Brasel sentenced [[Aimee Bock]] to more than 41 years in prison for her part in the [[Feeding Our Future]] fraud scheme.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Bettin |first= Anthony |last2= Leone |first2= Beret |date= 2026-05-21 |title= Feeding Our Future fraud ringleader Aimee Bock sentenced to 41+ years in prison |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/feeding-our-future-fraud-ringleader-aimee-bock-sentenced/ |url-status= live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260521155854/https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/feeding-our-future-fraud-ringleader-aimee-bock-sentenced/ |archive-date= 2026-05-21 |access-date= 2026-05-21 |website= CBS News |place= Minneapolis |language= en-US}}</ref>

