'''Nancy Jo Rosenstengel''' (née '''Niemeier'''; born 1968) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of Illinois]] and former [[Court clerk|clerk of court]] of the same court.

==Biography==

Rosenstengel received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, cum laude, in 1990 from the [[University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign]]. She received a [[Juris Doctor]], cum laude, in 1993 from the [[Southern Illinois University School of Law]]. 

She was an associate at the law firm of Sandberg, Phoenix & von Gontard (now Sandberg Phoenix) from 1993 to 1998, where her practice primarily involved products liability, medical malpractice and Jones Act litigation. 

From 1998 to 2009, she served as a career law clerk to Judge [[G. Patrick Murphy]] of the United States District Court for the Southern District of Illinois. From 2009 to 2014, she served as [[Court clerk|Clerk of Court]] in the same district.<ref name="whitehouse.gov">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/11/07/president-obama-nominates-three-serve-united-states-district-courts|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Three to Serve on the United States District Courts|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=7 November 2013}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394496|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On November 7, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Rosenstengel to serve as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of Illinois]],<ref name="whitehouse.gov"/> to the seat that was vacated by Judge [[G. Patrick Murphy]], who would assumeme [[senior status]] on December 1, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/11/07/presidential-nominations-sent-senate|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Presidential Nominations Sent to the Senate|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=7 November 2013}}</ref> 

On February 6, 2014 her nomination was reported out of committee.<ref>{{cite web|title=Executive Business Meeting|url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/hearings/hearing.cfm?id=138603a26950ad873303535a6318f123&2|work=United States Senate|publisher=Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=6 February 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140214063426/http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/hearings/hearing.cfm?id=138603a26950ad873303535a6318f123&2|archive-date=14 February 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> Cloture was filed on her nomination on May 6, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://democrats.senate.gov/2014/05/06/cloture-filed-on-4-judicial-nominations-talwani-peterson-rosenstengal-rosenbaum/|title=Cloture filed on 4 judicial nominations (Talwani, Peterson, Rosenstengal, Rosenbaum)|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161121144432/https://democrats.senate.gov/2014/05/06/cloture-filed-on-4-judicial-nominations-talwani-peterson-rosenstengal-rosenbaum/|archive-date=2016-11-21}}</ref> On Thursday May 8, 2014 the [[United States Senate|Senate]] invoked [[cloture]] on her nomination by a 54–42 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00136|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Nomination of Nancy J. Rosenstengel, of Illinois, to be U.S. District Judge)|publisher=United States Senate|access-date=8 May 2014}}</ref> Later that day, her nomination was confirmed by a 95–0 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00139|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Nancy J. Rosenstengel, of Illinois, to be U.S. District Judge)|publisher=United States Senate|access-date=8 May 2014}}</ref> 

She received her judicial commission on May 12, 2014.<ref name="fjc.gov" /> Rosenstengel was sworn in as the judge on May 19, 2014.<ref name="investiture">{{cite web|url=http://www.ilsd.uscourts.gov/TheCourt.aspx|title=The Court: Additional Historical Highlights|publisher=[[United States District Court for the Southern District of Illinois]]}}</ref> A formal investiture ceremony occurred on July 2, 2014.<ref name="investiture" /> She became chief judge on April 1, 2019, after [[Michael Joseph Reagan]] retired.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bnd.com/news/local/article230695469.html|title=With children grown, Belleville mom moves on to become new chief judge of U.S. court|last=Smith|first=Carolyn|date=June 21, 2019|publisher=[[Belleville News-Democrat]]|access-date=January 1, 2020}}</ref>

