'''Patrick Joseph Schiltz''' (born 1960) is an American lawyer and jurist serving as a [[Senior status|senior]] [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Minnesota]]. He was appointed in 2006 by President [[George W. Bush]]. Before becoming a federal judge, Schiltz was a law professor at the [[University of St. Thomas (Minnesota)|University of St. Thomas]] and the [[University of Notre Dame]].

==Early life and education==
Schiltz was born in 1960, in [[Duluth, Minnesota]], and grew up there.<ref name= ":0">{{FJC Bio| nid= 1392381}}</ref> He graduated from the [[College of St. Scholastica]] in 1981 with a [[Bachelor of Arts]], [[Latin honors|''summa cum laude'']], in history, with a minor in [[sociology]].<ref name= "Welter2025" /> He was a legislative aide to U.S. Senator [[David Durenberger]] from 1981 to 1982, then attended [[Harvard Law School]], where he was an editor of the ''[[Harvard Law Review]]''.<ref name= ":0" /><ref name= ":1">{{Cite web| title= District Court judge to speak at commencement May 13| url= http://www.css.edu/about/news-center/st-scholastica-news/district-court-judge-to-speak-at-commencement-may-13.html| website= www.css.edu| access-date= 2020-05-18| archive-date= 2020-02-12| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200212185414/http://www.css.edu/about/news-center/st-scholastica-news/district-court-judge-to-speak-at-commencement-may-13.html| url-status= live}}</ref> He graduated in 1985 with a [[Juris Doctor]], ''magna cum laude''.<ref name="Welter2025" />

==Career==
After law school, Schiltz was a [[law clerk]] from 1985 to 1986 to Judge [[Antonin Scalia]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit|U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit]]. Schiltz had accepted an offer to clerk for Justice [[Sandra Day O'Connor]] on the [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] the next year, but in July 1986, shortly before Schiltz's clerkship with Scalia ended, President [[Ronald Reagan]] nominated Scalia to the Supreme Court. Scalia asked Schiltz to help him prepare for his confirmation hearings, and after Scalia was confirmed, he asked Schiltz to clerk for him during his first year on the Supreme Court. With O'Connor's permission, Schiltz clerked for Scalia at the Supreme Court from 1986 to 1987.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="Welter2025" />

Following his clerkships, Schiltz joined the law firm [[Faegre & Benson]] (now part of [[Faegre Drinker]]) in [[Minneapolis]].<ref name=":1" /> He represented the [[National Football League]], the [[Minnesota Vikings]], and the [[Minnesota Timberwolves]] in [[antitrust]] and [[contract law]]; the [[Star Tribune]] and other media clients in access and [[libel]] litigation; and the [[Evangelical Lutheran Church in America]] and other religious organizations in [[tort]] and [[employment law|employment matters]].

Schiltz left private practice in 1995 to join the faculty of [[Notre Dame Law School]], where he taught [[civil procedure]], [[evidence (law)|evidence]], and [[sports law]]. While at Notre Dame, Schiltz wrote "On Being a Happy, Healthy, and Ethical Member of an Unhappy, Unhealthy, and Unethical Profession",<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Schiltz|first=Patrick J.|title=On Being a Happy, Healthy, and Ethical Member of an Unhappy, Unhealthy, and Unethical Profession|journal=Vanderbilt Law Review |date=May 1999 |volume=52 |issue=4 |page=871 |url=https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol52/iss4/2/}}</ref> one of the most widely read law-review articles ever published. The ''[[Vanderbilt Law Review]]'' published the article and made it the focus of a symposium, and an op-ed in the [[The Washington Post|''Washington Post'']] later called the article one of nine works every law student should read.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Krauss|first=Michael|date=2015-07-03|title=Entering law school? Here's what to read this summer.|language=en-US|newspaper=Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/entering-law-school-heres-what-to-read-this-summer/2015/07/03/ba935458-13a1-11e5-9ddc-e3353542100c_story.html|access-date=2020-05-18|issn=0190-8286}}</ref> One of Schlitz's star students at Notre Dame was future Supreme Court justice [[Amy Coney Barrett]], whom Schiltz helped get a clerkship with Justice Scalia.<ref name="Welter2025">{{cite journal |last1=Welter |first1=Elizabeth |title=Judicial Profile: Hon. Patrick J. Schiltz |journal=The Federal Lawyer |date=Summer 2025 |pages=22–31 |url=https://www.fedbar.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Schiltz-profile_Summer25.pdf}}</ref>

In 2000, Schiltz left Notre Dame to become the founding associate dean of the [[University of St. Thomas School of Law]] in Minnesota. He had primary responsibility for almost every significant aspect of creating the school, from hiring the faculty to designing the building. In 2002, Schiltz was named the St. Thomas More Chair in Law, the first endowed chair at the School of Law.<ref name=":1" />

From 1997 to 2006, Schiltz served as the Reporter to the Advisory Committee on the [[Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure]]. Among those who served on the Committee during Schiltz’s tenure were future Supreme Court Justices [[John Roberts|John G. Roberts, Jr.]] and [[Samuel Alito|Samuel A. Alito, Jr]].<ref name=":1" />

In October 2026, the Justice Department asked that Schiltz be removed from an immigration case, saying comments he made to ''The New York Times'' raised questions about impartiality. Schiltz scheduled a hearing on the request for November 23, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-10-21 |title= Top Justice Department official asks Minnesota judges to step aside from immigration cases |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/justice-department-minnesota-immigration-cases-judges/ |access-date= 2026-10-22 |website= www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===
On December 14, 2005, President [[George W. Bush]] nominated Schiltz to serve on the [[United States District Court for the District of Minnesota]] after Judge [[Richard H. Kyle]] assumed [[senior status]]. The [[United States Senate]] confirmed Schiltz's appointment on April 26, 2006, and he received his commission on April 28.<ref name=":0" /> He became Chief Judge of the federal district court on July 1, 2022.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=District of MN Announces New Chief Judge Patrick J. Schiltz |date=June 30, 2022 |publisher=United States District Court for the District of Minnesota |url=https://www.mnd.uscourts.gov/sites/mnd/files/2022-0630USDCMNNewsReleaseNewChiefJudge.pdf |access-date=July 10, 2022}}</ref> He took senior status on July 1, 2026.<ref name=":0"/>

====Reaction to Operation Metro Surge====
{{Main|Operation Metro Surge#Lawsuits}}

In January 2026, after the Trump administration launched [[Operation Metro Surge]] in the [[Twin Cities]] area, Schlitz ordered [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) acting director [[Todd Lyons]] to appear in court to explain why ICE had not complied with an earlier court order for the release of Juan Tobay Robles from detention.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.startribune.com/patience-is-at-an-end-federal-judge-orders-ice-director-to-appear-in-court/601571714| title='Patience is at an end': Federal judge orders ICE director to appear in court| first= Sofia| last= Barnett| newspaper= Minnesota Star Tribune| date= January 28, 2026| access-date= 2026-02-01| archive-date= 2026-01-29| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260129020111/https://www.startribune.com/patience-is-at-an-end-federal-judge-orders-ice-director-to-appear-in-court/601571714| url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Rubino |first= Kathryn |date= 2026-01-27 |title= Bush-Appointed Federal Judge To ICE: Comply With Court Courts. ICE: You've Got Us There! |url= https://abovethelaw.com/2026/01/bush-appointed-federal-judge-to-ice-comply-with-court-courts-ice-youve-got-us-there/ |access-date= 2026-01-28 |website= Above the Law |language= en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Ashley Oliver |first= Bill Melugin |date= 2026-01-27 |title= Federal judge threatening ICE director with contempt donated to group helping illegal immigrants |url= https://www.foxnews.com/politics/federal-judge-threatening-ice-director-contempt-once-supported-group-helping-illegal-immigrants |access-date= 2026-01-28 |website= Fox News |archive-date= 2026-01-27 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260127235318/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/federal-judge-threatening-ice-director-contempt-once-supported-group-helping-illegal-immigrants |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Feuer |first= Alan |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/27/us/politics/ice-director-minnesota-contempt.html |title= Judge Orders ICE Chief to Appear in Court Over Potential Contempt |access-date= 2026-01-28 |archive-date= 2026-01-27 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260127205326/https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/27/us/politics/ice-director-minnesota-contempt.html |url-status= live }}</ref> Robles was then released before the date on which Lyons had been ordered to appear.<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-01-28 |title= Judge rips into ICE but backs off demand for agency head to appear in court |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/28/immigration-minnesota-order-00754053 |access-date= 2026-01-28 |website= POLITICO |language= en}}</ref> Schiltz found that ICE had violated at least 96 court orders in Minnesota since January 1, 2026.<ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.fox9.com/news/ice-violated-least-96-court-orders-january| title= ICE violated at least 96 court orders in January| first= Paul| last= Blurne| publisher= Fox 9 Twin Cities| date= January 28, 2026| access-date= January 29, 2026| archive-date= January 29, 2026| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260129051045/https://www.fox9.com/news/ice-violated-least-96-court-orders-january| url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/01/28/us/minneapolis-shooting-ice-minnesota| title= Minnesota Judge Says ICE Violated Nearly 100 Court Orders| publisher= New York Times| date= January 28, 2026| access-date= January 26, 2026| archive-date= January 28, 2026| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260128165102/https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/01/28/us/minneapolis-shooting-ice-minnesota| url-status=live}}</ref>

After Schiltz and other district judges spoke with ''The New York Times'' in mid-September 2026 about Metro Surge and said the government had "created a grave threat to the rule of law",<ref>{{cite news |last1= Schwartz |first1= Mattathias |title=Trump's Minnesota Surge Threatened Democracy, Judge Warns |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/17/us/politics/minnesota-judge-trump-immigration.html?unlocked_article_code=1.FFE.yaeq.SVlpnWQYnV5Q&smid=url-share |access-date= October 2, 2026 |work= The New York Times |date= September 17, 2026}}</ref> U.S. Attorney General [[Todd Blanche]] announced his department had filed ethics charges against them with the [[Eighth Circuit Court of Appeals]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= McVan |first1= Madison |title= Justice Department alleges misconduct by 'all but one' Minnesota federal judges |url= https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/09/30/justice-department-alleges-misconduct-by-all-but-one-minnesota-federal-judge/ |access-date= 2 October 2026 |work=[[Minnesota Reformer]] |date= 30 September 2026}}</ref> Schiltz later defended his and his colleagues' comments in a public forum about the operation, saying he had both a right and a duty to defend [[judicial independence]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= McVan |first1= Madison |title= Schiltz: 'I did not violate any ethics rules' |url= https://minnesotareformer.com/2026/10/02/schiltz-i-did-not-violate-any-ethics-rules/ |access-date= October 2, 2026 |work= Minnesota Reformer |date= October 2, 2026}}</ref> The Trump administration's complaint<ref>{{cite web |title=Complaint |url=https://www.justice.gov/sites/default/files/2026-09/complaint_of_judicial_misconduct_file_stamped.pdf |website=[[justice.gov]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260930164725/https://www.justice.gov/sites/default/files/2026-09/complaint_of_judicial_misconduct_file_stamped.pdf |archive-date=September 30, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> was signed by [[Trent McCotter]] and [[Stanley Woodward Jr.]]<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Sarah N. |last2=Quinn |first2=Melissa |title=Justice Department files judicial misconduct complaint against federal judges in Minnesota |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/justice-department-files-judicial-misconduct-complaint-against-federal-judges-in-minnesota/ |work=[[CBS News]] |date=September 30, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> The administration previously filed [[James Boasberg#Dismissed DOJ misconduct complaint|a misconduct complaint]] against [[James Boasberg]] which was dismissed.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schwartz |first1=Mattathias |last2=Barrett |first2=Devlin |title=Justice Dept. Tries to Rebuke Federal Judges for Speaking to The Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/30/us/politics/justice-department-complaint-judges-schiltz.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=September 30, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

