'''Robert Austin Huffaker Jr.''' (born 1973)<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pyw8AQAAIAAJ |title=The Martindale-Hubbell Law Directory |date=2003 |publisher=Martindale-Hubbell Law Directory, Incorporated |pages=349 |isbn=9781561605514 |language=en}}</ref> is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Alabama]].

== Biography ==

Huffaker received a [[Bachelor of Engineering]] from [[Vanderbilt University]] and a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Alabama School of Law]]. Huffaker served as a commissioner of the Alabama Securities Commission and as a member of the Alabama Civil Jury Charge Committee.<ref name="WHBio">{{cite web |url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-judicial-nominees-united-states-marshal-nominee-3/ |title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Judicial Nominees and United States Marshal Nominee |last= |first= |date=July 1, 2019 |access-date=July 1, 2019 |via=[[NARA|National Archives]] |work=[[whitehouse.gov]] |quote=}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==

On July 1, 2019, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Huffaker to serve as a United States District Judge of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Alabama]]. Huffaker is nominated to the seat vacated by Judge [[William Keith Watkins]], who assumed [[senior status]] on January 31, 2019. On July 8, 2019, his nomination was sent to the [[United States Senate]].<ref>[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/four-nominations-sent-senate/ "Four Nominations Sent to the Senate", White House, July 8, 2019]</ref> On July 31, 2019, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/07/31/2019/nominations United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for July 31, 2019]</ref> On October 17, 2019, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 19–3 vote.<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/101719%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf Results of Executive Business Meeting – October 17, 2019, Senate Judiciary Committee]</ref> On December 3, 2019, the Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 88–4 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=116&session=1&vote=00373 |title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: R. Austin Huffaker, Jr., to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of Alabama) |last=|first=|date=December 3, 2019|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=December 4, 2019|language=en}}</ref> On December 4, 2019, his nomination was confirmed by a 89–4 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=116&session=1&vote=00381|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: R. Austin Huffaker, Jr., of Alabama, to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of Alabama)|last=|first=|date=December 4, 2019|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=December 4, 2019|language=en}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on December 12, 2019.<ref name="FJC">{{FJC Bio|nid=7465656|inline=yes}}</ref> He became chief judge in 2026.<ref name="FJC"/>

