'''Rebecca Christine Grady Jennings''' ({{nee|'''Grady'''}}; born 1978)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Jennings%20SJQ1.pdf|title=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees: Rebecca Grady Jennings|publisher=}} {{PD notice}}</ref> is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Kentucky]].

==Biography==
Jennings received her [[Bachelor of Arts]] from [[Emory University]] and [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[Washington College of Law]] at [[American University]], where she served as a senior associate on the ''American University International Law Review.''

In 2000 she was a student attorney for the American University Community and Economic Development Law Clinic and later a legal intern for [[District of Columbia Superior Court]] Judge Nan R. Shuker. In 2001 she was an equal justice foundation fellow for C-CAPE in [[Beaufort, North Carolina]].

She began her legal career as a law clerk to Judge [[William Joseph Haynes Jr.]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Tennessee]]. Before becoming a judge, she worked as chair of Middleton Reutlinger's litigation practice, and her practice focused on civil litigation at both the trial and appellate levels in state and federal courts.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2017/09/07/president-donald-j-trump-announces-seventh-wave-judicial-candidates|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Seventh Wave of Judicial Candidates}}</ref>

==Federal judicial service==
On September 7, 2017, President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Jennings to serve as a United States District Judge of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Kentucky]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[John G. Heyburn II]], who assumed [[senior status]] on April 1, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2017/09/07/nine-nominations-sent-senate-today|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=Nine Nominations Sent to the Senate Today}}</ref> A hearing on her nomination before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] was held on November 15, 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/11/15/2017/nominations|title=Nominations - United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov|date=November 15, 2017 }}</ref> On December 7, 2017, her nomination was reported out of committee by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting%2012-07-17.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – December 7, 2017|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref>  On April 12, 2018, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on her nomination by a 94–2 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00073|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Rebecca Grady Jennings, of Kentucky, to be U.S. District Judge for the Western District of Kentucky)|website=United States Senate}}</ref> Her nomination was confirmed later that day by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/989|title=PN989 – Nomination of Rebecca Grady Jennings, of Kentucky, for The Judiciary, 115th Congress (2017–2018)|date=September 7, 2017|website=www.congress.gov|access-date=April 13, 2018}}</ref> She received her commission on April 19, 2018.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=4477671|inline=yes}}</ref>

==Notable cases==

On April 21, 2022, Jennings temporarily blocked a Kentucky law that would ban abortions after 15 weeks. Jennings has not yet ruled on the law's constitutionality but agreed that more time is needed to determine "specifically determine which individual provisions and subsections are capable of compliance."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/21/politics/kentucky-abortion-law-planned-parenthood/index.html|title=Federal judge temporarily blocks new Kentucky abortion law from being enforced|website=CNN|date=April 21, 2022|access-date=April 23, 2022}}
</ref>

On July 21, 2025, Jennings sentenced former Kentucky police officer Brett Hankison to 33 months of imprisonment for using excessive force during the [[Breonna Taylor|lethal raid that led to Breonna Taylor's death]]. He was the first police officer held criminally accountable in this case.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-21 |title=Judge gives ex-officer nearly 3 years in Breonna Taylor raid, rebuffs DOJ call for no prison time |url=https://apnews.com/article/breonna-taylor-raid-hankison-sentence-4da93877da13f506be42cc1c738cab2b |access-date=2025-07-24 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> Her ruling was in dissent from the [[Pam Bondi|Trump Department of Justice]]'s recommendation of just one day in prison.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mystal |first=Elie |date=2025-07-23 |title=Trump World Will Never Allow Justice for Breonna Taylor |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/society/breonna-taylor-brett-hankison-sentence/ |access-date=2025-07-24 |language=en-US |issn=0027-8378}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Jennings is married to Patrick Jennings, who works at Commonwealth Alliances, a lobbying firm.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Wolfson|first1=Andrew|last2=Costello|first2=Darcy|title=Louisville lawyer would be first woman judge to serve exclusively in Western District|url=https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/2017/09/07/louisville-lawyer-would-first-woman-judge-serve-exclusively-western-district/642934001/|access-date=14 April 2018|publisher=Louisville Courier Journal|date=September 7, 2017}}</ref>

