'''Robert Anthony Molloy''' (born 1975) is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[District Court of the Virgin Islands]] and former judge of the [[United States Virgin Islands Superior Court]].

== Education ==

Molloy earned a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree from [[Hampton University]], a [[Juris Doctor]] from American University's [[Washington College of Law]], and a [[Master of Business Administration]] from the American University Kogod School of Business.<ref name="WHBio">[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-judicial-nominees-united-states-marshal-nominee-2/ "President Donald J. Trump Announces Judicial Nominees and United States Marshal Nominee" White House, May 29, 2019] {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Legal career ==

Molloy served as a [[law clerk]] to Judge [[Raymond L. Finch]] of the [[District Court of the Virgin Islands]]. He also served as an Assistant Attorney General of Labor in the Virgin Islands Office of Collective Bargaining.<ref name="WHBio"/>

== Judicial service ==

On July 27, 2013, [[Governor of the Virgin Islands|Virgin Islands Governor]] [[John de Jongh Jr.]] announced Molloy as a nominee for the [[St. Croix]] District of the [[United States Virgin Islands Superior Court]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://stcroixsource.com/2013/07/27/governor-sends-judicial-nominees-legislature/ |title=Governor Sends Judicial Nominees to Legislature |date=July 27, 2013 |website=St. Croix Source |language=en-US |access-date=May 31, 2019}}</ref> From 2013 to 2020, he served as a Judge of the [[United States Virgin Islands Superior Court]].<ref name="WHBio"/>

== Federal judicial service ==

On May 29, 2019, [[Donald Trump|President Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Molloy to serve as a [[United States federal judge|judge]] of the [[District Court of the Virgin Islands]]. On June 12, 2019, his nomination was sent to the Senate. President Trump nominated Molloy to the seat occupied by Judge [[Curtis V. Gomez]], whose term of appointment—ten years and until his successor is "chosen and qualified"—was set to expire.<ref>[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/six-nominations-two-withdrawals-sent-senate/ "Six Nominations and Two Withdrawals Sent to the Senate", White House, June 12, 2019]</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/48/1614|title=48 U.S. Code § 1614 – Judges of District Court|website=LII / Legal Information Institute|language=en|access-date=August 8, 2019}}</ref> His nomination was supported by Delegate [[Stacey Plaskett]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://sottvi.news/congresswoman-stacey-plaskett-releases-statement-after-president-trump-appoints-judge-robert-molloy-to-the-united-states-district-court-of-the-virgin-islands/ |title=Congresswoman Stacey Plaskett Releases Statement After President Trump Appoints Judge Robert Molloy to the United States District Court of the Virgin Islands |date=May 30, 2019 |website=State of the Territory News |language=en-US |access-date=May 31, 2019}}</ref> On June 26, 2019, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/06/26/2019/nominations United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for June 26, 2019]</ref> On July 18, 2019, his nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/071819%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – July 18, 2019|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On February 24, 2020, the [[United States Senate]] invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 88–1 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1162/vote_116_2_00053.htm|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Robert Anthony Molloy to be Judge for the District Court of the Virgin Islands)|website=United States Senate|date=February 24, 2020}}</ref> On February 25, 2020, his nomination was confirmed by a 97–0 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1162/vote_116_2_00054.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Robert Anthony Molloy, of the Virgin Islands, to be Judge for the District Court of the Virgin Islands)|website=United States Senate|date=February 24, 2020}}</ref> He assumed office on April 27, 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vid.uscourts.gov/sites/vid/files/PressReleaseJudgeRobertAMolloy.pdf|title=The District Court of the Virgin Islands Welcomes the Honorable Robert A. Molloy|publisher=District Court of the Virgin Islands|work=Clerk's Office|access-date=May 7, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=District Court of the V.I. Welcomes Robert A. Molloy to Federal Bench |url=https://stthomassource.com/content/2020/04/28/district-court-of-the-v-i-welcomes-robert-a-molloy-to-the-federal-bench/ |date=April 29, 2020 |website=St. Thomas Source |language=en-US |access-date=May 7, 2020}}</ref> He became Chief Judge on April 27, 2021.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vid.uscourts.gov/sites/vid/files/District%20CourtPress%20Release-012-2021.pdf|title=Chief Judge Position at the District Court of the Virgin Islands rotates to the Honorable Robert A. Molloy effective Tuesday, April 27, 2021|publisher=District Court of the Virgin Islands|date=April 27, 2021|accessdate=April 30, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=April 29, 2021 |title=District Court of V.I. Rotates to Chief Judge Robert A. Molloy |url=https://stthomassource.com/content/2021/04/29/district-court-of-vi-rotates-to-chief-judge-robert-a-molloy/ |access-date=April 30, 2021 |website=St. Thomas Source |language=en-US}}</ref>

