'''Robert James Shelby''' (born March 13, 1970)<ref name="martindale1">{{cite web|url=http://www.martindale.com/Robert-J-Shelby/1718431-lawyer.htm |title=Profile: Robert J. Shelby |publisher=martindale.com |date=August 10, 2010 |access-date=December 21, 2013}}</ref> is an American attorney and judge serving as a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Utah]].

==Early life, military service, and education==
Shelby was born in [[Fort Atkinson, Wisconsin]].<ref name="martindale1"/> He served in the [[19th Special Forces Group]], 1457th [[Combat Engineer Battalion|Engineer Battalion]] of the [[Utah Army National Guard]] from 1988 to 1996, and was on active duty during [[Operation Desert Storm]] in 1991. He received several military awards for his service, including the [[Achievement Medal#Army, Air Force, Space Force, and Coast Guard|United States Army Achievement Medal]] for [[Gulf War|Desert Storm]] and the [[National Defense Service Medal]]. He was honorably discharged with the rank of [[Specialist (rank)#Specialist (1955–present)|specialist]].<ref name="confirmation testimony">{{cite journal |title=Confirmation Hearings on Federal Appointments |url=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-112shrg76131/html/CHRG-112shrg76131.htm |publisher=[[U.S. Government Printing Office]] |volume=Senate Hearing 112–72 |issue=pt. 7 |access-date=December 22, 2013}}</ref> He received his [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1994 from [[Utah State University]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] in 1998 from the [[University of Virginia School of Law]].<ref name="whitehouse1" />

== Career ==

In 1999, he served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[John Thomas Greene Jr.]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Utah]]. He was an associate at Snow, Christensen & Martineau in [[Salt Lake City]] from 2000 to 2005. From 2005 to 2011, he was an associate at Burbridge, Mitchell & Gross. From 2011 to 2012, he again practiced at Snow, Christensen & Martineau as a [[shareholder]]. His practice focused on complex [[commercial litigation]] and catastrophic [[personal injury]] cases on behalf of both plaintiffs and defendants.<ref name="whitehouse1">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/30/president-obama-nominates-three-serve-us-district-court-bench |title=President Obama Nominates Three to Serve on the US District Court Bench |date=November 30, 2011 |via=[[NARA|National Archives]] |work=[[whitehouse.gov]] |access-date=December 21, 2013}}</ref> Shelby served on the Salt Lake County Bar Association's Executive Committee since 2002, and as its vice chairman since 2011. He served on the Utah Supreme Court's Advisory Committee on Rules of Civil Procedure and its Ethics and Discipline Committee.<ref name="confirmation testimony" /> He is a registered Republican.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/us/utah-ruling-on-marriage-puts-judge-in-spotlight.html|title=Utah Judge Unexpected as a Hero to Gay People|author=Jack Healy|newspaper=New York Times|date=December 29, 2013}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394051|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On November 30, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Shelby to be district judge of the United States District Court for the District of Utah,<ref name="whitehouse1" /> to the seat vacated by Judge [[Tena Campbell]], who assumed senior status on January 1, 2011. He received a hearing before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on March 28, 2012, and his nomination was reported to the floor on April 26, 2012, by [[voice vote]]. Both Senators from Utah, [[Orrin Hatch]] and [[Mike Lee]], endorsed his nomination, with Sen. Lee describing Shelby as "pre-eminently qualified" and predicting that he would be "an outstanding judge." Hatch highly lauded Shelby: "A man of keen intellect, Robert Shelby...has demonstrated an unwavering commitment to the law".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hatch.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/releases?ID=0b691732-90c9-4ed3-a8b9-404f686d062f|title=Hatch on Nomination of Robert Shelby to U. S. District Court for Utah – Press Releases – United States Senator Orrin Hatch|website=www.hatch.senate.gov|access-date=January 3, 2014|archive-date=January 4, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140104023103/http://www.hatch.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/releases?ID=0b691732-90c9-4ed3-a8b9-404f686d062f|url-status=dead}}</ref> In the early hours of September 22, 2012, on what was officially still the legislative day of September 21, the Senate confirmed Shelby in a [[voice vote]]. He received his commission on September 25, 2012.<ref name="fjc.gov" /> He became chief judge on October 1, 2018.<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1046801668346535936 |user=MichelleLQuist |title=Judge Robert J. Shelby is the new chief judge of the U.S. District Court for the District of Utah. Judge Nuffer is stepping down as chief, but will remain an active judge in the southern region. @UtahStateBar |date=October 1, 2018}}</ref>

=== Notable decisions ===
{{Main|Kitchen v. Herbert}}
*On December 20, 2013, Shelby struck down [[Utah Constitutional Amendment 3|Amendment 3]] of [[Utah]]’s [[Constitution of Utah|State Constitution]], which defined marriage as a union solely between a man and a woman, opening the way for [[Same-sex marriage in Utah|same-sex marriage in the state]]. He found that Amendment 3 was in violation of the [[United States Constitution|U.S. Constitution]]’s [[Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution|14th Amendment]], which guarantees due process and equal protection.<ref>{{cite news|author=Emiley Morgan |url=http://www.deseretnews.com/article/865592784/Federal-judge-rules-Utahs-ban-on-same-sex-marriage-unconstitutional.html?pg=1 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131221120834/http://www.deseretnews.com/article/865592784/Federal-judge-rules-Utahs-ban-on-same-sex-marriage-unconstitutional.html?pg=1 |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 21, 2013 |title=Gay couples wed after federal judge overturns Utah's same-sex marriage ban |newspaper=[[Deseret News]] |access-date=December 21, 2013}}</ref> This highly significant ruling set off a series of other district court decisions that overturned bans in several other states. His ruling was affirmed by the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit|Tenth Circuit Court of Appeals]] on June 25, 2014. On October 6, 2014, the U.S. Supreme Court declined the review the Tenth Circuit's ruling, [[Same-sex marriage in Utah|legalizing same-sex marriage in Utah]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=Eckholm|first=Erik|date=December 20, 2013|title=Federal Judge Rules That Same-Sex Marriage Is Legal in Utah (Published 2013)|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/21/us/utahs-gay-marriage-ban-is-ruled-unconstitutional.html|access-date=November 17, 2020|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>
*On September 10, 2024, he would enjoin the [[Utah Social Media Regulation Act]] for violating the first amendment.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/9/11/24241685/utah-netchoice-social-media-child-safety-law-blocked|title=Utah social media law requiring age verification blocked by judge|first=Emma|last=Roth|date=September 11, 2024|website=The Verge}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Seariac |first1=Hanna |date=September 10, 2024 |title=Judge blocks Utah social media law |url=https://www.ksl.com/article/51124722/judge-blocks-utah-social-media-law |website=www.ksl.com}}</ref>
*On April 17, 2025, Shelby dismissed a lawsuit filed in October 2023 accusing [[The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints]] of fraud in its handling of tithing funds, primarily due to the plaintiffs missing the three-year statute of limitations after the [[The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints#Financial controversy|information regarding the church's financial holdings]] became public in late 2019 and early 2020.<ref name="Brown 2025">{{cite web |last=Brown |first=Matthew |title=Judge dismisses lawsuit against Mormon church over how it uses donations |website=AP News |date=April 18, 2025 |url=https://apnews.com/article/mormon-church-tithing-lawsuit-latter-day-saints-b92cfbe01592ca720a241dc750d34e6b |access-date=April 25, 2025}}</ref>

