'''Rudolph Contreras''' (born December 6, 1962) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of Columbia]]. He served as Presiding Judge on the [[United States Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court]] from 2021 to 2023.

In December 2017 he briefly presided over the case of former [[National Security Advisor (United States)|National Security Advisor]] [[Michael Flynn]], accepting Flynn's guilty plea, but was later recused from the case.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trump-russia-flynn-idUSKBN1E202V|title=Judge presiding over Michael Flynn criminal case is recused: court|newspaper=Reuters |date=8 December 2017|access-date=26 January 2019|via=www.reuters.com}}</ref>

==Early life and education==

Contreras was born in 1962 in [[Staten Island]], [[New York (state)|New York]].<ref name="fjc.gov" /> His parents were immigrants from [[Cuba]], and he grew up in [[Miami]].<ref name="blt_20110729">{{Cite news | first=Zoe | last=Tillman | url=http://legaltimes.typepad.com/blt/2011/07/former-colleagues-weigh-in-on-rudolph-contreras-nomination.html | title=Former Colleagues Weigh In on Rudolph Contreras Nomination | work=[[Legal Times|The Blog of Legal Times]] | date=July 29, 2011 | access-date=August 16, 2011}}</ref> 

Contreras graduated from [[Florida State University]] in 1984 with a [[Bachelor of Science]]. From 1985 to 1988, he worked for the ''[[Miami Herald]]'' as an account executive. He then attended the [[University of Pennsylvania Law School]], graduating in 1991 with a [[Juris Doctor]] and [[Order of the Coif]] honors.<ref name=whgov_20110728>{{Cite web | author=The White House: Office of the Press Secretary | url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/07/28/president-obama-nominates-two-united-states-district-court | title=President Obama Nominates Two to the United States District Court | date=July 28, 2011 | via=[[NARA|National Archives]] | work=[[whitehouse.gov]] | access-date=August 16, 2011}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393936|inline=yes}}</ref>

== Career ==

From 1991 to 1994, Contreras was an associate at [[Jones Day]]. In 1994 he was a government attorney in the [[District of Columbia]]. From 1994 to 2012, he served as an [[assistant United States attorney]], in the District of Columbia from 1994 to 2003 and in the District of Delaware and as chief of the civil division from 2003 to 2006. He returned as an AUSA to the District of Columbia from 2006 to 2012, serving concurrently as chief of the civil division.<ref name="whgov_20110728"  /><ref name="fjc.gov" />

=== Federal judicial service ===

On July 28, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Contreras to fill a vacancy on the [[United States District Court for the District of Columbia]]<ref name="whgov_20110728"/> to replace Judge [[Ricardo M. Urbina]], who assumed senior status in 2011. On October 4, 2011, the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] held a hearing on his nomination and on November 3, 2011, reported his nomination to the floor of the Senate. On March 22, 2012, the Senate confirmed Contreras in a [[voice vote]]. He received his commission on March 23, 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/112th-congress/858|title=PN858 — Rudolph Contreras — The Judiciary|website=congress.org}}</ref>

In April 2016 Chief Justice [[John Roberts]] appointed Contreras to the [[United States Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court]] for a term starting May 19, 2016.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://fas.org/irp/agency/doj/fisa/court2016.html|title=Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court 2016 Membership|website=fas.org}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov"/> On May 19, 2021, Contreras was named the Presiding Judge.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fisc.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/FISC%20FISCR%20Judges%20June%202021.pdf|title=FOREIGN INTELLIGENCE SURVEILLANCE COURT FOREIGN INTELLIGENCE SURVEILLANCE COURT OF REVIEW Current and Past Members June 2021 |accessdate=August 31, 2021}}</ref>

=== Notable cases ===

On November 17, 2016, Contreras dismissed a lawsuit against [[U.S. Senate Majority Leader]] [[Mitch McConnell]] seeking to compel a vote on the [[U.S. Supreme Court]] nomination of [[Merrick Garland]], finding that the plaintiff, who had simply said he was a voter, had no [[Standing (law)|standing]] to sue.<ref>{{cite news|last1=DeBonis|first1=Mike|title=Judge dashes Merrick Garland's final, faint hope for a Supreme Court seat|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/powerpost/wp/2016/11/18/judge-dashes-merrick-garlands-final-faint-hope-for-a-supreme-court-seat/|access-date=November 23, 2016|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=November 18, 2016}}</ref>

In 2017, Contreras was assigned the case of ''United States of America v. [[Michael Flynn|Michael T. Flynn]]'', the former [[National Security Advisor (United States)|National Security Adviser]] to President [[Donald Trump]]. The two-page indictment was released on December 1, 2017.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.yahoo.com/entertainment/michael-flynn-expected-plead-guilty-143525910.html|title=Michael Flynn Pleads Guilty to Lying to FBI in Russia Probe|access-date=December 8, 2017|language=en-US}}</ref> Contreras accepted Flynn's guilty plea to one count of [[making false statements]] to the FBI in the course of their investigation into [[Russian interference in the 2016 U.S. presidential election]]. The guilty plea was part of a plea bargain with the [[Special Counsel investigation (2017–present)|Special Counsel investigation]] led by [[Robert Mueller|Robert S. Mueller III]].<ref name=":0" /> On December 7, 2017, Contreras was recused from further sentencing hearings scheduled to take place in the future.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2017/12/07/michael-flynn-judge-sentencing-287001|title=Judge recuses in Michael Flynn case|date=December 7, 2017|work=[[Politico]]|access-date=December 8, 2017}}</ref> The case was reassigned to District Judge [[Emmet G. Sullivan]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trump-russia-flynn/u-s-judge-presiding-over-michael-flynn-criminal-case-is-recused-court-idUSKBN1E202V|title=Judge presiding over Michael Flynn criminal case is recused: court|date=December 8, 2017|work=[[Reuters]]|access-date=December 8, 2017}}</ref> According to several reputable sources, text messages show that [[Peter Strzok]], a veteran FBI counterintelligence official who worked on the Flynn case as part of Mueller's team, knew Contreras.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/texts-show-judge-who-recused-himself-in-flynn-case-was-friendly-with-fbi-agent-involved-in-probe/2018/03/16/3b3736f2-293d-11e8-b79d-f3d931db7f68_story.html|title=Texts show judge who recused himself in Flynn case was friendly with FBI agent involved in probe|first1=Ellen|last1=Nakashima|first2=Spencer S.|last2=Hsu|first3=Matt|last3=Zapotosky|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationalreview.com/2018/03/peter-strzok-lisa-page-meeting-michael-flynn-judge/|title=Strzok and Page Plotted Covert Meeting with Presiding Judge in Michael Flynn Case|date=March 16, 2018|website=nationalreview.com|access-date=January 26, 2019}}</ref>

On August 6, 2020, Contreras dismissed a lawsuit House Republicans filed against [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|U.S. House Speaker]] [[Nancy Pelosi]] that challenged [[proxy voting]] rules adopted during the 2020 [[COVID-19 pandemic]]. He ruled that the Constitution's "[[Speech or Debate Clause]]" prohibited lawsuits over Congress's legislative efforts, concluding, "the Court can conceive of few other actions, besides actually debating, speaking, or voting, that could more accurately be described as 'legislative' than the regulation of how votes may be cast".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/510972-judge-throws-out-house-gop-lawsuit-over-proxy-voting|title=Judge throws out House GOP lawsuit over proxy voting|first=Ian|last=Swanson|date=August 6, 2020|website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://wcyb.com/news/connect-to-congress/judge-throws-out-gop-lawsuit-challenging-proxy-voting|title=Judge throws out GOP lawsuit challenging proxy voting|first=Austin |last=Denean|date=August 6, 2020|website=WCYB}}</ref>

On March 5, 2021, Contreras ruled that the states of [[Illinois]], [[Nevada]], and [[Virginia]] had ratified the [[Equal Rights Amendment]] too late for the amendment to be valid, as they did so after the congressionally imposed 1982 deadline.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/regulation/court-battles/541892-federal-judge-rules-states-were-too-late-in-ratifying-equal-rights|title=Judge rules states were too late in ratifying Equal Rights Amendment|first=Tal|last=Axelrod|date=March 5, 2021|website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref>

On March 4, 2025, Contreras ruled that President [[Donald Trump]] had unlawfully terminated [[Merit Systems Protection Board]] Chair Cathy Harris. Harris had been dismissed via a two-sentence email on February 10, 2025, with no reason given. Contreras found that under the [[Civil Service Reform Act of 1978]], MSPB members can be removed only for "inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office", and ordered Harris reinstated. Citing the 1935 Supreme Court precedent ''[[Humphrey's Executor v. United States]]'', he wrote that "direct political control over the MSPB would neuter the Civil Service Reform Act's statutory scheme."<ref>{{cite news |last=Cohen |first=Zachary |title=Cathy Harris: 'Merit board' chair was unlawfully fired by Trump, judge rules |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/04/politics/cathy-harris-merit-board-fired-trump/index.html |newspaper=CNN |date=March 4, 2025 |access-date=December 26, 2025}}</ref> The [[U.S. Department of Justice|Justice Department]] immediately appealed, and on March 28, 2025, the [[United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit|D.C. Circuit]] granted an emergency stay, effectively allowing Trump to remove Harris while the case continued.<ref>{{cite web |title=Harris v. Bessent |url=https://www.democracydocket.com/cases/washington-d-c-merit-systems-protection-board-unlawful-dismissal-challenge/ |website=Democracy Docket |access-date=December 26, 2025}}</ref>

On May 29, 2025, Contreras ruled that Trump's tariffs imposed under the [[International Emergency Economic Powers Act]] (IEEPA) were unlawful. In ''[[Learning Resources, Inc. v. Trump]]'', two Illinois-based toy manufacturers challenged Trump's tariffs on imports from China, Canada, Mexico, and other countries. Contreras found that IEEPA "does not authorize the president to impose the tariffs set forth" in Trump's executive orders, writing, "the power to regulate is not the power to tax". Unlike the [[United States Court of International Trade|Court of International Trade]], which issued a nationwide injunction the previous day, Contreras's order applied only to the two plaintiffs.<ref>{{cite news |title=Court strikes down most of Trump's tariffs, ruling them illegal |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/court-strikes-dow-trumps-liberation-day-tariffs-ruling-them-illegal/ |publisher=CBS News |date=May 29, 2025 |access-date=December 26, 2025}}</ref> The case was consolidated with others, and on February 20, 2026, the Supreme Court ruled that the IEEPA tariffs were unlawful.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Breuninger |first=Kevin |date=2026-02-20 |title=Supreme Court strikes down Trump tariffs, rebuking president's signature economic policy |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/20/supreme-court-trump-tariffs-ruling.html |access-date=2026-02-27 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref>

