'''Sara Lynn Darrow''' (née '''Frizzell'''; born 1970)<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/SaraDarrow-PublicQuestionnaireUpdate.pdf |title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees |work=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |date=April 13, 2011}}</ref> is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Central District of Illinois]]. She was formerly an assistant United States attorney in the [[United States District Court for the Central District of Illinois]], where she was chief of the [[violent crime]]s section.<ref name="SLDarrow">{{cite web |url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2010/11/17/president-obama-nominates-six-united-states-district-court-111710 |title=President Obama Nominates Six to the United States District Court, 11/17/10 |author= |date=November 17, 2010 |via=[[NARA|National Archives]] |work=[[whitehouse.gov]] |access-date=December 21, 2010}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Darrow  earned her [[Bachelor of Arts]] in 1992 from [[Marquette University]] and her [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Saint Louis University School of Law]] in 1997.<ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393741|inline=yes}}</ref>

==Career==
After graduating from law school, Darrow worked in the law offices of Clarence Darrow in [[Rock Island, Illinois]].<ref name="SLDarrow2">{{cite web |url=http://durbin.senate.gov/showRelease.cfm?releaseId=328643 |title=Durbin: White House Nominates Darrow to Fill Federal Judgeship in Central District |author= |date=November 17, 2010 |publisher=United States Senator Dick Durbin |access-date=December 21, 2010}}</ref>  Between 1999 and 2003, she worked in the [[Henry County, Illinois]] State's Attorney's Office in [[Cambridge, Illinois]]. In 2003 Darrow became an [[assistant United States attorney]] for the Central District of Illinois, where she prosecuted significant criminal cases, involving [[firearm]]s, [[Illegal drug trade|drugs]], [[gang]]s, [[fraud]], [[money laundering]], and [[corruption]].<ref name="SLDarrow"/><ref name="fjc.gov"/>

===Federal judicial service===
On the recommendation of Senator [[Dick Durbin]], Darrow was nominated to the United States District Court for the Central District of Illinois by President [[Barack Obama]] on November 17, 2010 to fill a vacancy created by Judge [[Joe Billy McDade]], who assumed [[senior status]].<ref name="SLDarrow2"/> The United States Senate confirmed Darrow by [[unanimous consent]] on August 2, 2011,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/112thCongress.cfm |title=Judicial Nominations and Confirmations: 112th Congress |website=judiciary.senate.gov |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110108213625/http://judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/112thCongress.cfm |archive-date=2011-01-08}}</ref> and she received her commission the next day.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> She started her term as chief judge on March 12, 2019, which ended on March 12, 2026.

==Personal life==
Darrow's husband Clarence Michael Darrow serves as an Illinois Circuit Court judge. Her father-in-law [[Clarence A. Darrow]] also served as an Illinois state court judge and served in the [[Illinois General Assembly]].<ref>[https://www.isba.org/ibj/2016/04/thehonorabledarrows Illinois Bar Journal-The Honorable Darrows]</ref><ref>[https://www.ourcampaigns.com/CandidateDetail.html?CandidateID=139517 Our Campaigns.com-Clarence Michael Darrow]</ref><ref>[https://www.ourcampaigns.com/CandidateDetail.html?CandidateID=77818 Our Campaigns.com-Clarence A. Darrow]</ref>

