'''Rachelle Lynne "Shelly" Deckert Dick''' (born 1960) is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Louisiana]]. She is the first female judge to serve in the Middle District.<ref>[http://www.afj.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/06/President-Obama-Female-Firsts.pdf Making History: President Obama’s Female Judicial Nominees], ''[[Alliance for Justice]]'' (June 11, 2013).</ref>

==Biography==

Dick was born Rachelle Lynne Deckert in 1960 in [[El Paso, Texas|El Paso]], [[Texas]]. She received her [[Bachelor of Business Administration]] degree, ''cum laude'', from the [[University of Texas]] in 1981 and her [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Paul M. Hebert Law Center]] in 1988. From 1988 to 1994, she worked at the law firm of Gary, Field, Landry and Dornier in [[Baton Rouge]]. From 1994 to 2013, she was a partner at the law firm of Forrester, Dick & Clark. During her private career, she litigated a wide variety of cases in both state and federal courts. From 2008 to 2013, she also served as an [[Latin tag#Ad hoc|ad hoc]] hearing officer appointed by the Louisiana Workforce Commission to hear [[workers' compensation (United States)|workers' compensation cases]] and other [[administrative law|administrative matters]].<ref name=Whitehouse>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2012/04/25/president-obama-nominates-four-serve-us-district-court|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Four to Serve on the US District Court|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=25 April 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/112thCongressJudicialNominations/upload/ShellyDick-PublicQuestionnaire.pdf|title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394191|inline=yes}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On April 25, 2012, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Dick to serve as a United States district judge for the United States District Court for the Middle District of Louisiana, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Ralph E. Tyson]].<ref name=Whitehouse/> Senator [[David Vitter]] initially refused to return his [[Blue slip (U.S. Senate)|blue slip]], effectively blocking her nomination.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://theadvocate.com/home/3015030-125/vitter-blocking-nominee |title=&#124; Home &#124; the Advocate — Baton Rouge, Louisiana |access-date=2012-07-07 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402102157/http://theadvocate.com/home/3015030-125/vitter-blocking-nominee |archive-date=2015-04-02 }}</ref> On November 28, 2012, Senator Vitter announced that he would return his "blue slip", permitting confirmation proceedings to proceed on Dick's nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://theadvocate.com/news/4546540-123/vitter-removes-block-on-judicial|title="Vitter Removes Block on Judicial Nominee", The Advocate, November 28, 2012|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130513122922/http://theadvocate.com/news/4546540-123/vitter-removes-block-on-judicial|archive-date=May 13, 2013}}</ref> She received a hearing on December 12, 2012. On January 2, 2013, her nomination was returned to the President, due to the sine die adjournment of the Senate. On January 3, 2013, she was renominated to the same office. Her nomination was reported by the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on February 28, 2013, by voice vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/01/03/president-obama-re-nominates-thirty-three-federal-judgeships|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Re-nominates Thirty-Three to Federal Judgeships|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=3 January 2013}}</ref> Her nomination was confirmed by [[voice vote]] on May 9, 2013. She received her commission on May 10, 2013. She became chief judge on July 18, 2018.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

