'''Timothy Lloyd Brooks''' (born July 17, 1964) is the [[United States federal judge|chief judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Arkansas]].

==Biography==

Brooks was born on July 17, 1964, in [[Detroit]], Michigan, but was raised on a farm in [[Washington County, Arkansas]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.martindale.com/Timothy-L-Brooks/62963-lawyer.htm|title=Timothy Lloyd Brooks Lawyer Profile on Martindale.com|website=www.martindale.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://trellis.law/judge/timothy.l.brooks|title=Judge Timothy L. Brooks:Professional Background and Legal Expertise|publisher=Trellis|accessdate=March 18, 2026}}</ref> He graduated from [[Fayetteville High School (Arkansas)|Fayetteville High School]] in 1982. He received his [[Bachelor of Business Administration|Bachelor of Science in Business Administration]] in 1986 from the [[University of Arkansas]]. He received his [[Juris Doctor]] in 1989 from the [[University of Arkansas School of Law]]. He spent his entire private practice career at the [[Fayetteville, Arkansas|Fayetteville]], Arkansas law firm Taylor Law Partners, LLP., starting as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] in 1989, becoming a [[Law firm partner|partner]] in 1993. His former practice focused on representing individual plaintiffs and corporate clients in complex [[civil litigation]] in both federal and state courts, with an emphasis on [[commercial law|commercial]] and [[medical malpractice]] cases.<ref name="Whitehouse">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/06/07/president-obama-nominates-two-serve-district-court-judges|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Two to Serve as District Court Judges|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=7 June 2013}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1394376|inline=yes}}</ref>

==Federal judicial service==

On June 7, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Brooks to serve as a United States District Judge of the United States District Court for the Western District of Arkansas, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Jimm Larry Hendren]], who assumed [[senior status]] on December 31, 2012.<ref name="Whitehouse" /> His nomination was reported out of committee on January 16, 2014 by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/ExecutiveBusinessMeetingResults-01-16-2014.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – January 17, 2014|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=July 30, 2023}}</ref> The [[United States Senate]] invoke clotured on his nomination on March 5, 2014 by a 59–41 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1132/vote_113_2_00053.htm|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on the Nomination of Timothy L. Brooks, of Arkansas, to be U.S. District Judge)|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> His nomination was confirmed later that day by a 100–0 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1132/vote_113_2_00054.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Timothy L. Brooks, of Arkansas, to be U.S. District Judge)|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on March 7, 2014.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

===Notable rulings===
On August 31, 2023, Brooks blocked an Arkansas law that requires children under 18 to get parental consent before accessing most social media websites, finding the law is likely unconstitutional. Brooks said in his 50-page ruling that NetChoice is likely to succeed in showing the law is unconstitutionally vague and fails to define which platforms are subject to it.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-08-31 |title=Judge blocks law requiring parental consent for kids to use social media |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/08/31/judge-arkansas-law-parental-consent-social-media-00113696 |access-date=2024-09-10 |website=[[Politico]]}}</ref>

On March 16, 2026, Brooks blocked an Arkansas law requiring the Ten Commandments to be displayed in classrooms, finding it to be in violation of the [[Establishment Clause]] and the [[Free Exercise Clause|free exercise rights]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/education/5787547-aransas-school-districts-ten-commandments/|title=Judge permanently blocks Ten Commandments displays at several Arkansas school districts|first=Lexi Lonas|last=Cochran|publisher=The Hill|date=March 17, 2026|accessdate=March 18, 2026}}</ref> In his written judgment, Brooks stated, among other things, that "nothing could possibly justify hanging the Ten Commandments - with or without historical context - in a calculus, chemistry, French, or woodworking class, to name a few."<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/arkansas-ten-commandments-public-schools-law-struck-down-federal-judge/|title=Arkansas law requiring Ten Commandments be displayed in public schools struck down by federal judge|publisher=CBS News|date=March 18, 2026|accessdate=March 18, 2026}}</ref>

