'''Thomas Shawn Kleeh''' (born September 14, 1974)<ref>{{Cite web |title=Attorney Thomas Shawn Kleeh |url=https://www.lawyercentral.com/thomas-shawn-kleeh-interactive-profile--20-705051.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012134835/https://www.lawyercentral.com/thomas-shawn-kleeh-interactive-profile--20-705051.html |archive-date=October 12, 2018 |access-date=March 21, 2022 |website=Lawyer Central}}</ref> is the [[United States federal judge|chief United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of West Virginia]].

== Biography ==

Kleeh was born and raised in [[Wheeling, West Virginia]] and graduated from Wheeling Central High School.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Snoderly |first=JoAnn |title='I've never had dreams this big': Judge Kleeh closes in on one year as federal judge in NCWV |url=https://www.wvnews.com/news/wvnews/i-ve-never-had-dreams-this-big-judge-kleeh-closes-in-on-one-year-as/article_c14bd6d4-f322-56a5-9d0f-be8b7253d902.html |access-date=2022-12-15 |website=WV News |date=October 13, 2019 |language=en}}</ref> He earned his [[Bachelor of Business Administration]] from [[West Virginia University]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[West Virginia University College of Law]], where he was inducted into the [[Order of the Coif]].<ref name=whitehouse/>

From 1999 to 2018, he practiced with Steptoe & Johnson PLLC, and specialized in labor and employment issues covering both the public and private sectors. He rose to become a member of the firm before becoming a judge. He also served as counsel to the [[West Virginia Senate]]'s Committee on the Judiciary.<ref name=whitehouse>{{Cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-eleventh-wave-judicial-nominees/|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Eleventh Wave of Judicial Nominees – The White House|website=trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==

In the summer of 2017, Kleeh met with Senators [[Shelley Moore Capito]] and [[Joe Manchin]] and received their recommendation to the Trump administration for a federal judgeship.<ref name=":0" /> On February 12, 2018, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intent to nominate Kleeh to an undetermined seat on the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of West Virginia]]. On February 15, 2018, his nomination was sent to the Senate. President Trump nominated Kleeh to the seat vacated by Judge [[Irene Patricia Murphy Keeley]], who assumed [[senior status]] on August 12, 2017.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/1644|title=PN1644 - Nomination of Thomas S. Kleeh for The Judiciary, 115th Congress (2017-2018) &#124; Congress.gov &#124; Library of Congress|date=October 11, 2018 }}</ref> On April 25, 2018, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/04/25/2018/nominations|title=Nominations &#124; United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov|date=April 25, 2018 }}</ref> On May 24, 2018, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 14–7 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/05-24-18%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – May 24, 2018, Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On October 11, 2018, the [[United States Senate]] confirmed his nomination by a 65–30 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00239|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Thomas S. Kleeh, of West Virginia, to be U.S. District Judge for the Northern District of West Virginia)|last=|first=|date=October 11, 2018|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=October 11, 2018|language=en}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on November 5, 2018. He became Chief Judge on March 19, 2022.<ref name="fjc">{{FJC Bio|nid=5374581}}</ref>

=== Notable cases ===

In 2021, he sentenced serial killer [[Reta Mays]] to life in prison without parole plus 20 years for 7 murders and 1 attempted murder. 
<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.militarytimes.com/veterans/2021/05/11/former-va-health-worker-sentenced-life-in-prison-for-murdering-seven-patients-via-insulin-poisoning/|title=Former VA health worker sentenced to life in prison for murdering seven patients via insulin poisoning|work=Military Times|first=Leo|last=Shane III|date=May 11, 2021|access-date=December 21, 2021}}</ref> 

As of 2022, Kleeh is overseeing the trial for the murder of [[Whitey Bulger]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Harvey |first=Matt |title=Jury selection & trial set for Dec. 13 in slaying of Whitey Bulger |url=https://www.wvnews.com/news/wvnews/jury-selection-trial-set-for-dec-13-in-slaying-of-whitey-bulger/article_363e66c6-5556-11ed-a020-3f526e02e249.html |access-date=2022-12-15 |website=WV News |date=October 26, 2022 |language=en}}</ref>

==== Second Amendment ====
{{primary sources|section|date=February 2024}}
On August 31, 2023, Kleeh upheld the [[Domestic Violence Offender Gun Ban]] and the [[National Firearms Act]] as applied to short-barreled shotguns under ''[[New York State Rifle & Pistol Association, Inc. v. Bruen|New York State Rifle Pistol Association, Inc. v. Bruen]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 31, 2023 |title=US v. Delauder Denial of Dismissal |url=https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.wvnd.56449/gov.uscourts.wvnd.56449.29.0.pdf |access-date=February 10, 2024 |website=CourtListener}}</ref>

On December 1, 2023, Kleeh declared that the minimum age requirement for handgun purchases is unconstitutional under ''[[New York State Rifle & Pistol Association, Inc. v. Bruen]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 1, 2023 |title=Brown v. ATF Opinion |url=https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.wvnd.54471/gov.uscourts.wvnd.54471.40.0.pdf |access-date=February 10, 2024 |website=CourtListener}}</ref>

