'''Tilman Eugene "Tripp" Self III''' (born 1968) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the  [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Georgia]] and a former [[Judge]] of the [[Georgia Court of Appeals]].

== Education and career ==

Born in 1968,<ref name="BS">{{cite book |last=Hubbell |first=Martindale |date=April 2000 |title=Martindale-Hubbell Law Directory: Georgia, Hawaii, Idaho (Volume 7 - 2000) |url=https://archive.org/details/martindalehubbel07mart|url-access=registration |publisher=[[Martindale-Hubbell]] |isbn=9781561603763 }}</ref> Self received a [[Bachelor of Business Administration|Bachelor of Science in Business Administration]] degree in 1990 from [[The Citadel, The Military College of South Carolina|The Citadel]], graduating 9th out of his class of 516.<ref name="gaappeals.us">{{cite web|url=http://www.gaappeals.us/biography/bio_judges.php?jname=Tilman+Self|title=Court of Appeals of Georgia: Tilman Self, Judge|website=www.gaappeals.us}}</ref> He served four years as a Field Artillery Officer in the [[United States Army]]. He received a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1997 from the [[University of Georgia School of Law]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.martindale.com/macon/georgia/tilman-eugene-self-iii-881519-a/|title=Hon. Tilman Eugene Self, III Judge Profile on Martindale.com|website=www.martindale.com}}</ref> In the summer of 1990 he was a laborer for his father's construction business and in 1996 he was a research assistant to professor Ronald D. Carlson at the [[University of Georgia School of Law]]. He engaged in the private practice of law for nine years at the [[Macon, Georgia|Macon]], [[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]] law firm of Sell & Melton, LLP. Self was elected to the [[Georgia Superior Courts|Macon County Superior Court]] from Macon in 2006, beginning service on January 1, 2007, and winning re-election in 2010 and 2014. He was chief judge from January 1, 2014, to January 1, 2017, with his tenure running from the retirement of his predecessor, S. Phillip Brown, to his elevation.<ref>{{cite web | title=Phil Brown, Bibb chief judge, set to retire at the end of 2013 | website=Macon Telegraph | date=12 November 2013 | url=https://www.macon.com/news/special-reports/lauren-giddings-murder/article30125484.html | access-date=2 December 2023}}</ref> On November 9, 2016, [[Georgia Governor]] [[Nathan Deal]] appointed Self to the [[Georgia Court of Appeals]] to the seat vacated by [[Michael P. Boggs]], with his term beginning January 1, 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.macon.com/news/local/article113694044.html|title=Judge Tripp Self appointed to Georgia Court of Appeals}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.macon.com/news/local/article122709929.html|title=Following the law not as easy as it sounds, outgoing judge will tell you}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.macon.com/news/local/article121761293.html |title=Macon judge takes oath of office for higher court |last=Lee |first=Maggie |date=December 19, 2016 |publisher=[[The Telegraph (Macon)|The Telegraph]] |access-date=April 3, 2018}}</ref> David L. Mincey III succeeded him, by appointment, on the Superior Court bench.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.macon.com/news/local/article133146999.html|title=New judge named to Middle Georgia court}}</ref> He became the 83rd judge of the Court of Appeals and ended his tenure on the state bench after being confirmed to the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Georgia]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Tilman_E._Self_III|title=Tilman E. Self III|website=Ballotpedia|accessdate=December 2, 2023}}</ref><ref name="gaappeals.us"/>

== Federal judicial service ==

On July 13, 2017, President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Self to serve as a United States District Judge of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of Georgia]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[C. Ashley Royal]], who assumed [[senior status]] on September 1, 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2017/07/13/president-donald-j-trump-announces-fifth-wave-judicial-candidates|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Fifth Wave of Judicial Candidates|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=July 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2017/07/13/ten-nominations-sent-senate-today|title=Ten Nominations Sent to the Senate Today|date=July 13, 2017|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170714065923/https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2017/07/13/ten-nominations-sent-senate-today|archive-date=July 14, 2017|df=mdy-all}}</ref> On October 4, 2017, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/10/04/2017/nominations|title=Nominations &#124; United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|date=October 4, 2017|website=www.judiciary.senate.gov|accessdate=December 2, 2023}}</ref> On October 26, 2017, his nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/10-26-17%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – October 26, 2017|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On March 1, 2018, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on his nomination by an 85–12 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00044|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Tilman Eugene Self III to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of Georgia)|website=United States Senate|date=March 1, 2018}}</ref> On March 5, 2018, his nomination was confirmed by an 85–11 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00047|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Tilman Eugene Self III, of Georgia, to be U.S. District Judge for the Middle District of Georgia)|website=United States Senate|date=March 5, 2018}}</ref> He received his commission on March 7, 2018.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=4268606|inline=yes}}</ref>

==Notable cases==

In February 2022, Self issued an [[injunction]] forbidding the Air Force from requiring an officer to be vaccinated against [[COVID-19]], as against her religious objections. Responding to the Air Force's argument that vaccinations were necessary to promote military readiness, Self questioned the efficacy of the COVID vaccine, asking whether "a COVID-19 vaccine really provides more sufficient protection [than "natural immunity"]?"<ref>{{cite web|url=https://reason.com/volokh/2022/02/16/preliminary-injunction-bars-forced-retirement-based-on-air-force-officers-religious-refusal-of-vaccination/#more-8171060 |title=Preliminary Injunction Bars Forced Retirement Based on Air Force Officer's Religious Refusal of Vaccination|last=Volokh|first=Eugene|publisher=The Volokh Conspiracy|date=February 16, 2022}}</ref>

==Memberships==

He has been a member of the [[Federalist Society]] since 2008.<ref name="Questionnaire">{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Self%20SJQ.pdf|title=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees: Tilman Eugene Self, III|accessdate=December 2, 2023}}</ref>

