'''Arthur Marvin Quattlebaum Jr.''' (born August 2, 1964) is a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit]]. He was formerly a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the District of South Carolina]].

== Biography ==
Quattlebaum was born on August 2, 1964, in [[Durham, North Carolina]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lawyercentral.com/a-marvin-quattlebaum-jr-interactive-profile--20-590980.html|title=Attorney A Marvin Quattlebaum Jr – Lawyer in Columbia SC|website=www.lawyercentral.com|access-date=August 4, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170805014908/http://www.lawyercentral.com/a-marvin-quattlebaum-jr-interactive-profile--20-590980.html|archive-date=August 5, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> He received his [[Bachelor of Arts]], ''cum laude'', from [[Rhodes College]] and his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of South Carolina School of Law]], where he was a member of the ''South Carolina Law Review''.

He started his legal career as an associate at [[Nelson Mullins Riley & Scarborough]], where he was a partner before becoming a judge. His nationwide trial practice focused on complex civil litigation in federal courts. On the basis of this experience, he was invited to serve as a fellow in the [[American College of Trial Lawyers]]. From 2011 to 2012, he served as the president of the [[South Carolina Bar]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2017/08/03/president-donald-j-trump-announces-sixth-wave-judicial-candidates-and|title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Sixth Wave of Judicial Candidates and Fifth Wave of U.S. Attorney Candidates|date=August 3, 2017|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|access-date=August 4, 2017}}</ref>  And, in 2021, he is serving as the Chair of Greenville's YMCA Board.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hassold|first=Kim|title=Through his faith, Marvin Quattlebaum has made helping others a priority in his life|url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/talk-greenville/2021/01/19/through-his-faith-marvin-quattlebaum-has-made-helping-others-priority-his-life/4212882001/|access-date=2021-09-08|website=The Greenville News|language=en-US}}</ref>

== Federal judicial service ==

=== District court service ===
On August 3, 2017, President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Quattlebaum to serve as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the District of South Carolina]], to the seat vacated by Judge [[Cameron McGowan Currie]], who assumed [[senior status]] on October 3, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2017/08/03/eighteen-nominations-sent-senate-today|title=Eighteen Nominations Sent to the Senate Today|date=August 3, 2017|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|access-date=August 4, 2017}}</ref> On October 4, 2017, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/10/04/2017/nominations United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for October 4, 2017]</ref> On October 26, 2017, his nomination was reported out of committee by a [[voice vote]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/10-26-17%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – October 26, 2017|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On February 28, 2018, the [[United States Senate]] invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 69–29 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00041|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture on A. Marvin Quattlebaum Jr., of South Carolina, to be U.S. District Judge for the District of South Carolina)|website=United States Senate|date=February 28, 2018}}</ref> On March 1, 2018, his nomination was confirmed by a 69–28 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00042|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation A. Marvin Quattlebaum, Jr. of South Carolina, to be U.S. District Judge for the District of South Carolina)|date=March 1, 2018|website=United States Senate|access-date=March 1, 2018}}</ref> The senators who voted against confirming Quattlebaum did so not because they found him unqualified, but as a protest vote over the fact that in 2013 and 2016, the Senate had not advanced two black nominees for the same South Carolina judgeship.<ref>{{cite web|last=Byrd|first=Caitlin|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/chuck-schumer-votes-against-s-c-judicial-nominee-because-he/article_8b9f1890-1d6b-11e8-8533-0f7cc33319a9.html|title=Chuck Schumer votes against South Carolina federal judge nominee because he's white &#124; Palmetto Politics|publisher=postandcourier.com|date=March 1, 2018|access-date=March 8, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Dumain |first=Emma |url=http://www.mcclatchydc.com/news/politics-government/article202884204.html |title=Senate Dems oppose S.C. judge nominee &#124; McClatchy Washington Bureau |publisher=Mcclatchydc.com |date=March 1, 2018 |access-date=March 8, 2018}}</ref> Quattlebaum received his judicial commission on March 6, 2018.<ref name="fjc">{{FJC Bio|nid=4263096|inline=yes}}</ref> His service on the district court terminated on September 6, 2018, upon elevation to the court of appeals.<ref name="fjc"/>

=== Court of appeals service ===

[[File:A. Marvin Quattlebaum (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|250px|U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee hearing]]

On April 26, 2018, President Trump announced his intent to nominate Quattlebaum to serve as a United States circuit judge of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit]].<ref name="WHBio">{{cite web |url=https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/president-donald-j-trump-announces-thirteenth-wave-judicial-nominees-seventh-wave-united-states-marshal-nominees/ |title=President Donald J. Trump Announces Thirteenth Wave of Judicial Nominees and Seventh Wave of United States Marshal Nominees |date=April 26, 2018 |via=[[NARA|National Archives]] |work=[[whitehouse.gov]] |access-date=April 26, 2018 }} {{PD-notice}}</ref> On May 7, 2018, his nomination was sent to the [[United States Senate|Senate]]. He was nominated to the seat being vacated by Judge [[William Byrd Traxler Jr.]], who announced his intention to assume [[senior status]] on August 31, 2018.<ref>[https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/presidential-actions/fifteen-nominations-sent-senate-today-2/ "Fifteen Nominations Sent to the Senate Today" White House, May 7, 2018]</ref> On June 20, 2018, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/06/20/2018/nominations United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for June 20, 2018]</ref> On July 19, 2018, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 15–6 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting%2007-19-18.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – July 19, 2018|publisher=Senate Judiciary Committee}}</ref> On August 15, 2018, the Senate invoked cloture on Quattlebaum's nomination by a 61–28 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00182|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: A. Marvin Quattlebaum, Jr. to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Fourth Circuit)|date=August 15, 2018|website=United States Senate|access-date=August 16, 2018}}</ref> On August 16, 2018, his nomination was confirmed by a 62–28 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=2&vote=00183|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation A. Marvin Quattlebaum Jr., of South Carolina, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Fourth Circuit)|date=August 16, 2018|website=United States Senate|access-date=August 16, 2018}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on September 4, 2018.<ref name="fjc"/>

==== Notable opinions ====

* ''Peltier v. Charter Day School Inc.'', --- F.4th ----, 2021 WL 3483288 (Aug. 9, 2021)''.''  In a challenge to a charter school's dress code, the court reversed the district court on two grounds.  First, the court held that the charter school was not a state actor and thus not subject to an equal protection claim. More importantly, the court found that claims of sex discrimination related to a dress code are not categorically excluded from the scope of Title IX.  Thus, the case was sent back to the district court for further proceedings on the Title IX claim.<ref>{{Cite web|first1=Debra Cassens|last1=Weiss|date=10 August 2021|publisher=American Bar Association|title=School dress codes mandating skirts for girls may violate Title IX, 4th Circuit rules|url=https://www.abajournal.com/news/article/school-dress-codes-mandating-skirts-for-girls-may-violate-title-ix-4th-circuit-rules|access-date=2021-09-08|website=ABA Journal|language=en}}</ref>
* ''Eline v. Town of Ocean City'', Maryland, 7 F.4th 214 (4th Cir. 2021). Rejecting a § 1983 action challenging a city ordinance prohibiting women from baring their breasts in public, the court found the ordinance did not violate the plaintiff's equal protection rights.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Barakat|first=Matthew|title=Federal appellate judges uphold Ocean City law banning topless sunbathing for women|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2021/08/04/federal-appellate-judges-uphold-ocean-city-law-banning-topless-sunbathing-for-women/|access-date=2021-09-08|website=The Baltimore Sun|date=August 4, 2021 }}</ref>

