'''William Lindsay Osteen Jr.''' (born August 8, 1960) is a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of North Carolina]].

==Education and career==

Osteen was born in [[Greensboro]], [[North Carolina]]. He received a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree from [[University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill]] in 1983. He received a [[Juris Doctor]] from [[University of North Carolina School of Law]] in 1987. He was in private practice of law in Greensboro from 1987 to 2007.

===Federal judicial service===

Osteen was nominated by President [[George W. Bush]] on January 9, 2007 to serve as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the Middle District of North Carolina]]. He was nominated to the seat vacated by his father, Judge [[William Lindsay Osteen Sr.]] He was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] on September 10, 2007, and received his commission on September 19, 2007.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=1392616|inline=yes}}</ref> He served as chief judge from November 1, 2012 to November 3, 2017.

=== Notable ruling ===

He struck down North Carolina's 'life of the mother only' 20-week abortion ban in March 2019. His judgement pushed the date of which abortions could be performed to the date of viability, which is later for many women.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://chapelboro.com/news/state-news/north-carolina-20-week-abortion-limit-formally-struck-down|title=North Carolina 20-Week Abortion Limit Formally Struck Down|date=2019-05-28|website=Chapelboro.com|language=en-US|access-date=2019-05-28|archive-date=2019-05-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190528140111/https://chapelboro.com/news/state-news/north-carolina-20-week-abortion-limit-formally-struck-down|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-north-carolina-abortion-idUSKCN1R7263|title=Federal judge ends North Carolina ban on abortions after 20 weeks|date=2019-03-26|work=Reuters|access-date=2019-05-28|language=en|archive-date=2019-05-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190528153627/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-north-carolina-abortion-idUSKCN1R7263|url-status=live}}</ref> However, Osteen reinstated the NC 20-week abortion ban, with exceptions for urgent medical emergencies, after the June 2022 U.S. Supreme Court decision overturning ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' erased the legal foundation for his 2019 ruling that had struck down the 1973 state law. Judge Osteen stated “Neither this court, nor the public, nor counsel, nor providers have the right to ignore the rule of law as determined by the Supreme Court."

