'''Catharina Jacoba Hendrika Dubbelday Haynes'''{{efn|born with last name Dubbeldam, later changed to Dubbelday}}<ref name="Congressional hearings">{{Cite web|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/005628473|title=Confirmation hearings on federal appointments: hearing before the Committee on the Judiciary, United States Senate, One Hundred Tenth Congress, first session.|date=June 24, 2007|website=Catalog.hathitrust.org|access-date=June 24, 2026|via=HathiTrust}}</ref> (born November 9, 1963) is a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit]].

==Background==
Haynes was born in [[Melbourne, Florida|Melbourne]], [[Florida]].  Her father was a Dutch immigrant from Indonesia<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://obits.dallasnews.com/us/obituaries/dallasmorningnews/name/pieter-dubbelday-obituary?id=31210527|title=Pieter Dubbelday Obituary (1928 - 2021) - Dallas, TX - Dallas Morning News|website=Obits.dallasnews.com|access-date=June 24, 2026}}</ref>.  She graduated from [[Satellite High School]], [[Satellite Beach, Florida|Satellite Beach]], Florida, in 1980.<ref name="Florida Tech News">{{cite web|url=https://news.fit.edu/archive/satellite-high-graduate-becomes-circuit-judge-u-s-court-of-appeals/|website=Florida Institute of Technology News|access-date=10 May 2023|title=Satellite High Graduate Becomes Circuit Judge, U.S. Court of Appeals|date=12 December 2008 }}</ref> She received a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in Psychology from the [[Florida Institute of Technology]] in 1983 at the age of 19, graduating first in her class.<ref name="Florida Tech News" /> She graduated second in her class from [[Emory University School of Law]] in 1986 with her [[Juris Doctor]] at the age of 22. While at Emory, she was an editor for the [[Emory Law Journal#Publications|Emory Law Journal]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/infocus/judicialnominees/haynes.html|website=The White House (President George W. Bush)|access-date=25 January 2017|title=Judicial Nominations - Judge Catharina Haynes}}</ref> She became a member of the [[State Bar of Texas]] in 1986.

After law school, Haynes was in private practice in Texas from 1986 to 1998 with the firms of Thompson & Knight, as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] from 1986 to 1988 and then [[Baker Botts]], as an associate from 1988 to 1995, and then became a [[Law firm partner|partner]] in 1995. {{citation needed|date=August 2020}}

In 1998, Haynes was elected as a Republican to the 191st District Court in [[Dallas]], [[Texas]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2002 General Election - District Judge, 191st Judicial District |url=https://elections.sos.state.tx.us/elchist95_race967.htm |website=Texas Secretary of State}}</ref> She was re-elected in 2002. As a state district court judge, she presided over jury and bench trials involving civil litigation. After narrowly losing re-election in 2006<ref>{{cite web|url=https://assets01.aws.connect.clarityelections.com/Assets/Connect/RootPublish/dallas-tx.connect.clarityelections.com/ElectionDocuments/2006/November072006/061107_Total%20Cumm%20Report.pdf |format=PDF|title=General and Special Elections : UNOFFICIAL RESULTS|date= November 7, 2006|location=Dallas County, Texas|website=Assets01.aws.connect.clarityelections.com|access-date=June 24, 2026}}</ref>, she returned to Baker Botts as a partner, where she practiced complex business litigation and maintained an appellate and [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] practice. {{citation needed|date=August 2020}}

==Federal judicial service==
On the recommendation of Senators [[John Cornyn]] and [[Kay Bailey Hutchison]], Haynes was nominated on July 17, 2007 by President [[George W. Bush]] to fill a vacancy on the Fifth Circuit created by Judge [[Harold R. DeMoss Jr.]], who assumed [[senior status]] on July 1, 2007. Haynes received a hearing before the [[Senate Judiciary Committee]] on February 21, 2008, and was voted out of committee on April 3, 2008. She was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] by [[unanimous consent]] on April 10, 2008, just under nine months after her nomination. She received her commission on April 18, 2008.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=1392681|inline=yes}}</ref> She maintains chambers in Dallas.

