'''Dorothy Wright Nelson''' (born '''Dorothy Jean Wright'''; September 30, 1928) is a [[Senior status|senior]] [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit]].

==Education and career==
Born in [[San Pedro, California|San Pedro]], [[California]],<ref>{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p5IrAQAAIAAJ|title=The World Who's who of Women|first=Ernest|last=Kay|date=April 27, 1975|publisher=Melrose Press|isbn=9780900332401 |via=Google Books}}</ref> Wright received an [[Bachelor of Arts|Artium Baccalaureus]] degree from the [[University of California, Los Angeles]] in 1950, a [[Juris Doctor]] from [[UCLA School of Law]] in 1953, and a [[Master of Laws]] from the [[USC Gould School of Law]] in 1956. She was a research associate fellow at the Gould School of Law from 1953 to 1956. She was in private practice in [[Los Angeles]], California from 1954 to 1957. She was a member of the faculty of the Gould School of Law from 1957 to 1980. She was an instructor from 1957 to 1958. She was an assistant professor from 1958 to 1961. She was an associate professor from 1961 to 1967. She was an associate dean from 1965 to 1967. She was an interim dean from 1967 to 1969 and because of her achievement she was named Woman of the Year by the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]''.<ref>{{cite news
 | title =Times woman of the year, Dorothy Wright Nelson, Dean of USC Law School, holds unique position
 | author=Dorothy Townsend
 | newspaper =The Los Angeles Times
 | location =Los Angeles, California
 | page =49
 | date =December 23, 1968
| url =https://www.newspapers.com/clip/6309238/profile_and_interview_of_bahai_dorothy/ 
 | access-date = November 7, 2016}}</ref> She was a professor from 1967 to 1980. She was a dean from 1969 to 1980. She was an adjunct professor of law at the Gould School of Law starting 1980.<ref name="auto">{{FJC Bio|1742|nid=1385621|name=Dorothy Wright Nelson<!--(1928–)-->}}</ref>

==Federal judicial service==
Nelson was nominated by President [[Jimmy Carter]] on September 28, 1979, to the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit]], to a new seat created by 92 Stat. 1629. She was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] on December 19, 1979, and received her commission on December 20, 1979. She assumed [[senior status]] on January 1, 1995.<ref name="auto"/> She published an article in the ''Southern California Law Review''.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Nelson|first=Dorothy W.|title =Introduction to the Effects of Gender in the Federal Courts: The Final Report of the Ninth Circuit Gender Bias Task Force|journal=Southern California Law Review|volume=67|pages=731–|year=1993–1994|url=http://heinonline.org/HOL/LandingPage?collection=journals&handle=hein.journals/scal67&div=34&id=&page=|access-date=March 31, 2012}}</ref> She is the author of a book, [https://www.amazon.com/materials-judicial-administration-American-casebook/dp/B0006CAEGW ''Judicial Administration and the Administration of Justice''], published by West Lawbook.

===Supreme Court consideration===
In 1973 there was discussion she might be nominated to the US Supreme Court in the news.<ref>{{cite news|last=Barth|first=Ilene|title=If a seat opens: Will Nixon choose a woman for the Supreme Court?|newspaper=The Lincoln Star|location=Lincoln, Nebraska|page=88|date=January 7, 1973|url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/332247/the_lincoln_star|access-date=February 5, 2014}}</ref>

===Notable cases===
On February 1, 2018, Nelson ruled that the County of Maui violated the [[Clean Water Act]] when it discharged pollutants without a permit.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://cdn.ca9.uscourts.gov/datastore/opinions/2018/02/01/15-17447.pdf|title=Hawaii Wildlife Fund v. County of Maui|website=ca9.uscourts.gov|date=February 1, 2018|access-date=February 18, 2024}}</ref> The Supreme Court mostly affirmed in [[County of Maui v. Hawaii Wildlife Fund]].

==Personal life==
In 1950, Wright married James F. Nelson (1927–2011), a longtime [[Los Angeles County Superior Court|Los Angeles Municipal Court]] judge.<ref name="sbsun">{{cite news |last1=Sweeney |first1=Joan |title=Petticoat Revolution Happening in Court |url=https://cdnc.ucr.edu/cgi-bin/cdnc?a=d&d=SBS19691013.1.15&srpos=1&e=-------en--20--1--txt-txIN-%22Dorothy+Wright%22+%22james+f.+nelson%22------- |access-date=October 1, 2018 |work=San Bernardino Sun |agency=UPI |publisher=UCR California Digital Newspaper Collection |date=October 13, 1969 |page=15}}</ref> The couple had two children.<ref name="sbsun"/>

She is an active member of the [[Baháʼí Faith]] and served in the [[National Spiritual Assembly]] of the Baháʼís of United States for many years. She became a Baháʼí following the suggestion to explore the religion from Donald Barrett in 1954 along with about 70 others across a decade.<ref>{{cite AV media|people=Dorothy Wright Nelson|title=An interview with the Honorable Dorothy Nelson, Judge, Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals|series=H. Dale Hilton Living History Project of the Emerti Center at the University of Southern California|medium=Video|publisher=USC on YouTube|date=October 12, 2007|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IlhOQ3vRixE}}</ref><ref>{{cite AV media|people=Dorothy Wright Nelson|title=Dorothy Nelson Oral History Interview|series=Women Trailblazers in the Law collection of the American Bar Association|medium=Television|publisher=C-Span.org|date=October 21, 2007|url=http://www.c-span.org/video/?292825-1/dorothy-nelson-oral-history-interview}}</ref> Barrett would go on to serve at the Baháʼí World Center in 1979.<ref>{{cite news|title=Lawyer to Serve Baha'i Faith|newspaper=Pittsburgh Post-Gazette|location=Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania|page=2|date=September 22, 1979|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=1129&dat=19790922&id=955RAAAAIBAJ&sjid=6W0DAAAAIBAJ&pg=5532,3498272|access-date=June 30, 2014}}</ref> In 1989, she was awarded an honorary Doctor of Laws (LL.D.) degree from [[Whittier College]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.whittier.edu/alumni/poetnation/honorary|title=Honorary Degrees {{!}} Whittier College|website=www.whittier.edu|access-date=February 20, 2020}}</ref>

