'''Emil Joseph Bove III''' ({{IPAc-en|eɪ|m|ɪ|l|_|b|o:|'|v|iː}}; {{Respell|AY|mill|_|BOH|vee}}; born 1981) is an American attorney who has served as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]] since September 2025. Before that, he served at the [[United States Department of Justice|Department of Justice]] as [[Associate deputy attorney general|principal associate deputy attorney general]] from January to September 2025, including as acting [[United States Deputy Attorney General|deputy attorney general]] from January to March 2025.

Bove studied public policy and economics at the [[University at Albany, SUNY]] and graduated from [[Georgetown University Law Center]] in 2008. He clerked for judges [[Richard J. Sullivan]] and [[Richard C. Wesley]] and became an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] at [[Sullivan & Cromwell]] before returning to federal employment as [[assistant United States attorney]] for the Southern District of New York in 2012. Bove was appointed co-chief of the office's terrorism and international narcotics unit in October 2019. He resigned in December 2021 and later joined [[Donald Trump]]'s legal team in September 2023.

On November 14, 2024, President-elect Donald Trump announced Bove would serve as principal associate deputy attorney general in his incoming administration. Bove was appointed acting deputy attorney general in January 2025 and served in the position until [[Todd Blanche]] was confirmed in March. Bove was involved in several controversies at the Department of Justice, including the dismissal of a criminal corruption case against [[Eric Adams]], the [[mayor of New York City]], and multiple [[Whistleblowing|whistleblower]] allegations that he suggested ignoring a [[Federal judiciary of the United States|federal court]] order to lawyers in a case involving the deportation of [[Venezuelans]] to [[El Salvador]].

On May 28, 2025, President Trump announced his nomination of Bove to serve on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit. Bove was subsequently confirmed by the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] on July 24, 2025. Bove assumed judgeship on September 2, 2025.

==Early life and education (1981–2008)==
[[File:Georgetown Law (53839144407).jpg|thumb|upright=0.9|alt=A building behind a green field and trees.|left|[[Georgetown University Law Center]], where Bove studied (pictured in 2024)]]
Emil Joseph Bove III{{Sfn|"Sarah Samis, Emil Bove III". The New York Times}} was born in 1981 in [[Geneva, New York]],{{Sfn|Bove|2025}} and was raised in [[Seneca Falls, New York]].{{Sfn|"Trump attorney, Seneca Falls native Emil Bove tapped for top DOJ role". FingerLakes1.com}} His father, Emil Bove Jr., is an attorney.{{Sfn|Shaw|1981}} The elder Bove served as an [[Attorney General of New York|assistant New York attorney general]] at the [[Rochester, New York|Rochester]] office.{{Sfn|"Sarah Samis, Emil Bove III". The New York Times}} In 1999, the younger Bove graduated [[salutatorian]] from [[Mynderse Academy]], where he participated in the school's soccer, basketball, and lacrosse teams.{{Sfn|Cotterill|2024}} He went on to graduate from the [[University at Albany, SUNY]] ''[[summa cum laude]]'' in 2003 with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in public policy and economics.{{Sfn|"Trump attorney, Seneca Falls native Emil Bove tapped for top DOJ role". FingerLakes1.com}} At SUNY Albany, Bove captained the [[Albany Great Danes men's lacrosse]] team. He was named the [[America East Conference]] Male Scholar Athlete in 2003.{{Sfn|Porter|2023}} After graduating, Bove worked as a [[paralegal]] in the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York]] before leaving in 2005 to attend [[Georgetown University Law Center]],{{Sfn|Goldman|Thrush|Weiser|Haberman|2025}} graduating in 2008 with a [[Juris Doctor]].{{Sfn|"Trump attorney, Seneca Falls native Emil Bove tapped for top DOJ role". FingerLakes1.com}} He was the editor-in-chief of ''[[The Georgetown Law Journal]]''{{'}}s Annual Review of Criminal Procedure.{{Sfn|Porter|2023}}

==Career==
===Clerkship and private practice (2008–2011)===
From 2008 to 2009, Bove [[law clerk|clerked]] for Judge [[Richard J. Sullivan]] of the United States District Court for the Southern District of New York.{{Sfn|"Sarah Samis, Emil Bove III". The New York Times}} The following year, he clerked for Judge [[Richard C. Wesley]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit]].{{Sfn|Olechowski|2010}} After his clerkships, Bove was employed as an associate at [[Sullivan & Cromwell]].{{Sfn|"Who is Emil Bove?: Acting deputy attorney general expected in court for Adams hearing". WNBC}}

===Assistant United States attorney and return to private practice (2012–2021)===
In 2012, [[Preet Bharara]], the [[United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York]], hired Bove as an [[assistant United States attorney]].{{Sfn|Goldman|Thrush|Weiser|Haberman|2025}} Bove was appointed co-chair of the office's terrorism and international narcotics unit in October 2019.{{Sfn|Goldman|Thrush|Weiser|Haberman|2025}} Prominent prosecutions Bove led included those against [[Nicolás Maduro]],{{Sfn|Ordoñez|2016}} [[Cesar Sayoc]],{{Sfn|Cheney|Orden|2023}} [[Tony Hernández]],{{Sfn|Ernst|2019}} [[Ahmad Khan Rahimi]],{{Sfn|Reid|Gannon|Lybrand|2025}} and [[Fabio Lobo]].{{Sfn|Raymond|2016}} In 2018, he sought a supervisory position; Bove was denied a promotion after a group of defense attorneys wrote a letter expressing concerns about his legal tactics.{{Sfn|Orden|2025}} Bove assisted in identifying numerous participants of the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack|January 6 Capitol attack]].{{Sfn|Rabinowitz|Perez|Reid|Polantz|2025}} He resigned in December 2021.{{Sfn|Adams|Ernst|2022}}

Bove joined Chiesa, Shahinian & Giantomasi in their [[New York City]] office in January 2022.{{Sfn|Adams|Ernst|2022}} In September 2023, he became a [[Law firm partner|partner]] at Blanche Law, a law firm founded by [[Todd Blanche]]. Days later, Bove joined [[Donald Trump]]'s criminal defense team.{{Sfn|Cheney|Orden|2023}} In the state court [[Criminal trial of Donald Trump in New York|criminal trial of Trump in New York]], he was second chair to Blanche on Trump's defense team.{{Sfn|Epstein|2024}}{{Sfn|Protess|Bromwich|Haberman|Rashbaum|2024}} Bove represented Trump in the federal [[Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (classified documents case)|classified documents]] and [[Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (election obstruction case)|election obstruction cases]].{{Sfn|Thrush|Goldman|Bromwich|Haberman|2025}}

==Department of Justice (January–September 2025)==
{{See also|2025 U.S. Department of Justice resignations|March 2025 American deportations of Venezuelans|J.G.G. v. Trump}}
On November 14, 2024, President-elect Donald Trump announced that Bove would serve as [[Associate deputy attorney general|principal associate deputy attorney general]].{{Sfn|Barrett|2024}} On January 20, 2025, Bove was appointed acting [[United States Deputy Attorney General|deputy attorney general]].{{Sfn|Gurman|2025}} Within days, he sent a memorandum threatening to prosecute local officials who refuse to comply with requests from the department following through on Trump's [[Immigration policy of the second Donald Trump administration|immigration policy]].{{Sfn|Thrush|2025}} Bove later stated that [[Carla B. Freedman]], the [[United States Attorney for the Northern District of New York]], was investigating [[Tompkins County, New York|Tompkins County]] sheriff Derek Osborne, who allegedly allowed a Mexican citizen to be released from jail after pleading guilty to assault in the third degree.{{Sfn|Maag|Sullivan|2025}} That month, he instructed the leadership of the [[Federal Bureau of Investigation]] to compile a list of prosecutors involved in [[Criminal proceedings in the January 6 United States Capitol attack|criminal proceedings in the January 6 Capitol attack]].{{Sfn|Goldman|Barrett|Thrush|2025}} Hours later, over twelve federal prosecutors in the [[United States Attorney for the District of Columbia|United States attorney's office for the District of Columbia]] who investigated the attack were dismissed.{{Sfn|Thrush|Barrett|Feuer|Sullivan|2025}} Bove moved to exert greater authority over the bureau, accusing acting director [[Brian Driscoll]] and his deputy, [[Robert Kissane]], of "insubordination" in February for refusing to provide the list of names he requested.{{Sfn|Thrush|Goldman|2025}} According to ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'', he threatened to fire Driscoll.{{Sfn|Gurman|Barber|Viswanatha|2025}} Senator [[Dick Durbin]] accused [[Kash Patel]] of directing the dismissals of career civil servants that Bove carried out.{{Sfn|Savage|2025}}

Communications between federal prosecutors and the legal team of [[Eric Adams]], the [[mayor of New York City]], had gone through Bove since he took office, according to ''[[The New York Times]]''.{{Sfn|Haberman|Rashbaum|Barrett|Bromwich|2025}} Bove moved to dismiss[[Investigations into the Eric Adams administration|federal corruption charges]] against Adams in February, arguing that the indictment interfered with the [[2025 New York City Democratic mayoral primary|New York City Democratic mayoral primary]].{{Sfn|Rashbaum|Rubinstein|Thrush|Rothfeld|2025}} The move to dismiss the case led to several [[2025 U.S. Department of Justice resignations|resignations]], including [[Danielle Sassoon]], the acting [[United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York]],{{Sfn|Gurman|Ramey|Fanelli|2025}} [[Hagan Scotten]], an assistant United States attorney in the district, and five prosecutors associated with the Department of Justice's [[Public Integrity Section]].{{Sfn|Scannell|Perez|Rabinowitz|Herb|2025}} In court, Bove told Judge [[Dale Ho]] that there was no ''quid pro quo''.{{Sfn|Katersky|Reinstein|2025}} When he dismissed the case, Ho stated that the situation "smacks of a bargain" where the indictment was dismissed "in exchange for immigration policy concessions".{{Sfn|Mangan|2025}}

In June 2025, [[Erez Reuveni]], a former lawyer for the department, filed a [[Whistleblowers in the second Trump administration#Allegations against Emil Bove|whistleblower report]] alleging that Bove had alerted select department lawyers that Trump would soon invoke the [[Alien and Sedition Acts|Alien Enemies Act]] in order to deport [[Venezuelan]]s to a prison in [[El Salvador]]. Bove purportedly stated that if a court order attempted to prevent the deportation flights, the Department of Justice would consider "telling the courts 'fuck you'" and "ignore any such order." The following day, in ''[[J.G.G. v. Trump]]'', Judge [[James Boasberg]] ordered planes in the air to return; despite Reuveni's concerns about contempt of court, Bove allegedly told the [[United States Department of Homeland Security|Department of Homeland Security]] that the flights did not need to return.{{Sfn|Barrett|2025a}} In April, Boasberg ruled that there was probable cause to start criminal contempt proceedings involving non-custodial [[Habeas corpus|writs of ''habeas corpus'']], though he lacked jurisdiction over the Venezuelans,{{Sfn|Joscelyn|Goodman|2025}} and in June, he issued a separate ruling involving custodial writs of ''habeas corpus''—which the court did have authority over—that the deportees had been deprived of their [[right to due process]].{{Sfn|Rosen|Montoya-Galvez|Quinn|2025}} Reuveni's lawyers later released copies of text messages from the day of the flights and an email from the day after that supported his allegations. [[Thomas Joscelyn|Tom Joscelyn]] and [[Ryan Goodman]] of ''[[Just Security]]'' wrote that the email and text messages implicate Bove in both contempt of court and violating due process rights.{{Sfn|Joscelyn|Goodman|2025}} Bove's tenure was widely viewed as controversial;{{Efn|Attributed to multiple sources: <ref>{{Harvnb|Mallin|Faulders|2025}}: President Donald Trump announced he will nominate Emil Bove, his former personal '''lawyer-turned-controversial''' top Department of Justice official, to serve as a federal appeals court judge on Wednesday.</ref><ref>{{Harvnb|Beitsch|2025a}}: Bove, now the principal associate deputy attorney general, has been '''at the center of a series of controversies''' during his brief tenure at the Justice Department – episodes listed by Democrats who oppose his nomination.</ref><ref>{{Harvnb|Reid|Gannon|Lybrand|2025}}: In that short time, he has proven himself to be a reliable ally for the president and also been '''embroiled in a series of major controversies''' – including dropping federal charges against New York City Mayor Eric Adams; investigating officials who worked on cases related to January 6, 2021; and pursuing Trump's deportation goals in ways that prompted a whistleblower to allege Bove intended to ignore court orders and mislead federal judges.</ref><ref>{{Harvnb|Hsu|2025}}: Bove has been '''at the center of some of the Justice Department's most controversial actions''', including a push to drop federal corruption charges against New York Mayor Eric Adams after he offered to support the Trump administration's immigration policies.</ref>}} Bove acknowledged that some of his decisions "generated controversy", but said that the media were promoting a "wildly inaccurate caricature" of him.{{Sfn|Beitsch|2025a}}

In October 2026, Jill Fields, a former FBI supervisory intelligence analyst, filed a whistleblower report alleging that in early 2025, Bove ordered the Los Angeles field office to investigate protesters' demonstrations outside the federal building, despite the protests being protected speech.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reilly |first=Ryan J. |date=October 6, 2026 |title=FBI whistleblower says White House pushed unconstitutional probe of protesters |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/justice-department/fbi-whistleblower-says-white-house-pushed-unconstitutional-probe-prote-rcna601599 |access-date=October 7, 2026 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref>

==Judge of the Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit (2025–present)==
In May 2025, ''[[The New York Times]]'' reported that Donald Trump was considering Bove for a seat on the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]], the one last held by [[Joseph A. Greenaway Jr.]]{{Sfn|Thrush|Savage|2025a}}{{Sfn|Headley|2025}} [[Joe Biden]] had previously nominated [[Adeel A. Mangi]] to fill that same vacancy.<ref>{{cite web |title=Nominations Sent to the Senate |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2024/01/08/nominations-sent-to-the-senate-133/ |website=bidenwhitehouse.[[archives.gov]] |language=en |date=January 8, 2024 |quote=Adeel Abdullah Mangi, of New Jersey, to be United States Circuit Judge for the Third Circuit, vice Joseph A. Greenaway, Jr., retired.}}</ref> On May 28, Trump announced he would select Bove for the appellate court.{{Sfn|Thrush|Savage|2025b}} Bove's name was formally sent to the Senate on June 16.{{Sfn|Headley|2025}} Bove appeared before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Committee on the Judiciary]] on June 25.{{Sfn|Stein|2025}} The committee's chairman, [[Chuck Grassley]], preemptively defended him from Erez Reuveni's allegations.{{Sfn|Barrett|2025b}} He faced questions over the decision to dismiss Eric Adams's case and the Department of Justice resignations that followed.{{Sfn|Barrett|2025c}} He was also questioned about Reuveni's allegations; Joscelyn and Goodman argue that if the allegations are true, then Bove made false statements in his testimony.{{Sfn|Joscelyn|Goodman|2025}} Grassley later characterized the allegations as nothing more than "aggressively litigating and interpreting court orders" on behalf of clients.{{Sfn|Hornung|2025}}

In July, six former federal prosecutors for the District of Columbia sent a letter urging the Senate Judiciary Committee to reject Bove's nomination, referring to him as "the worst conceivable nominee" to be appointed for an indefinite judicial position.{{Sfn|Hsu|2025}} The same month, more than seventy-five former state and federal judges wrote to the committee that Bove was disqualified on the basis of his "egregious record of mistreating law enforcement officers, abusing power, and disregarding the law itself". The judges expressed concern about Trump having nominated someone who had previously served as his personal lawyer and who might demonstrate "fealty" to Trump.{{Sfn|Hudspeth Blackburn|Gangel|2025}} Other groups in opposition to his confirmation included the Society for the Rule of Law Institute{{Sfn|Macagnone|2025}} and the [[Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights]],{{Sfn|Cassens Weiss|2025}} while Republican attorneys general from twenty states supported Bove in a letter.{{Sfn|Mansfield|2025}} The day before the committee was scheduled to vote on whether to advance Bove's nomination, Justice Connection sent the committee a letter signed by over nine hundred former Department of Justice attorneys voicing "deep concern" about Bove's nomination; in response, senator [[Dick Durbin]], the ranking member on the committee, encouraged the committee to postpone the vote.{{Sfn|Balk|2025}}

Before the Senate Committee on the Judiciary's scheduled vote, Democratic members called to have Reuveni testify,{{Sfn|Beitsch|2025b}} but the request was rebuffed.{{Sfn|Thorp V|Gregorian|2025}} On July 17, 2025, the committee voted to advance Bove's nomination. Prior to the vote, most of the Democrats on the committee walked out in protest, seeking to have further debate on the nomination prior to a committee vote.{{Sfn|Fuchs|2025a}} Grassley stated that proceeding was no different than what Democrats had done years prior as the controlling party by cutting off debate regarding two of president [[Joe Biden]]'s judicial nominees.{{Sfn|Fuchs|2025a}} Durbin's spokesperson said that the Republicans had violated Senate rules and the Democrats would question the parliamentarian about whether the vote was valid.{{Sfn|Thorp V|Gregorian|2025}} Later that month, two other whistleblowers came forward, one with evidence that allegedly contradicts Bove's Senate Judiciary Committee testimony about the dismissal of the case against Eric Adams,{{Sfn|Stein|Meyer|Leonnig|2025}} and another with evidence corroborating Reuveni's allegations.{{Sfn|Hornung|2025}} Both whistleblowers provided evidence to the Justice Department's inspector general.{{Sfn|Hornung|2025}}{{Sfn|Stein|Meyer|Leonnig|2025}}

On July 24, 2025, the U.S. Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination in a 50–48 vote,{{Sfn|Fuchs|2025b}} and on July 29, 2025 voted to confirm Bove by a 50–49 vote. Every Republican senator, with the exception of [[Susan Collins]] and [[Lisa Murkowski]], voted to confirm him, while every Democratic senator opposed his nomination. Senator [[Bill Hagerty]] was not present to vote.{{Sfn|Stein|Meyer|2025}} Bove received his judicial commission on August 20, but was not immediately sworn in. He continued to work at the Department of Justice through that time.{{Sfn|Barrett|2025d}} Bove was sworn in on September 2.{{Sfn|Alexander|2025}}

==Personal life==
In 2012, Bove married Sarah Samis in a ceremony officiated by Judge [[Richard J. Sullivan]].{{Sfn|"Sarah Samis, Emil Bove III". The New York Times}}

