'''Jennifer Lee "Jenn" Mascott'''<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/21-1319.html|title=Docket for 21-1319|website=www.supremecourt.gov}}</ref> ({{nee|'''Miller'''}}; born 1976) is an American lawyer and jurist serving as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]]. Prior to her judicial appointment, Mascott was a law professor whose research focused on [[separation of powers]] and [[litigation]]. She taught at the [[Columbus School of Law]] of [[Catholic University of America]] (2025), the [[Antonin Scalia Law School]] of [[George Mason University]] (2017–2024), and the [[George Washington University Law School]] (2011–2017).

==Early life and education==

Mascott was born Jennifer Lee Miller in 1976 in [[Westminster, Maryland]].<ref name="Questionnaire">{{cite web |last=Mascott |first=Jennifer Lee |date=September 3, 2025 |title=Mascott's Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/mascott_sjq1.pdf |website=www.judiciary.senate.gov |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=September 3, 2025 |archive-date=September 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250923052016/https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/mascott_sjq1.pdf |url-status=bot: unknown }}</ref> She graduated from the [[University of Maryland, College Park]] with a [[Bachelor of Science]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jennifer Mascott (Law professor) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Jennifer_Mascott_(Law_professor) |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref> 

Prior to entering law school she worked in communications and legislative roles for Representative [[Eric Cantor]] of [[Virginia]] and to Representative [[Anne Northup]] of [[Kentucky]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Russell-Sluchansky |first=Carmen |date=Oct 17, 2025 |title=Trump’s pick for 3rd Circuit Court of Appeals will soon join federal court |url=https://whyy.org/articles/jennifer-mascott-trump-third-circuit-appeals-court/ |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=WHYY |language=en-US}}</ref> She graduated from the [[George Washington University Law School]] in 2006, where she was a senior editor of the ''[[The George Washington Law Review|George Washington Law Review]]''.<ref name=":1" />

==Career==
After law school, Mascott was a [[law clerk]] to [[Brett Kavanaugh]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit]] from 2006 to 2007 and Justice [[Clarence Thomas]] of the [[Supreme Court of the United States|United States Supreme Court]] from 2008 to 2009.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2">{{FJC Bio|nid=13762064}}</ref> From 2011 to 2013 and 2015 to 2017, she was a lecturer at George Washington University Law School and from 2015 to 2017 was an Olin/Searle Fellow at [[Georgetown University Law Center]].<ref name=":2" /> From 2017 to 2024, she was an assistant professor of law at the [[Antonin Scalia Law School]] of [[George Mason University]].<ref name=":2" /> From 2017 to 2019, Mascott worked in private practice.<ref name=":2" />

During the [[first presidency of Donald Trump]], Mascott served as deputy assistant attorney general in the [[Office of Legal Counsel]] and later as associate deputy attorney general in the [[United States Department of Justice]].<ref name=":1" /> In 2021, Mascott returned to work as an assistant professor of law at the Antonin Scalia Law School at George Mason University, where she worked until 2024.<ref name=":2" /> Mascott inherited ownership of Adfero, a [[public relations]] firm, in 2023 following her husband's death.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Barnes |first=Daniel |last2=Wendler |first2=Jacob |date=2026-08-06 |title=‘Is she even allowed to be doing this?’: Inside a federal judge’s side gig running a PR firm |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/08/06/jennifer-mascott-federal-judge-adfero-pr-firm-01025871 |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> 

She served in the [[White House Counsel|White House Counsel's Office]] in the [[second Trump presidency]].<ref name=":1" /> She served as an associate professor of law at the [[Columbus School of Law|Catholic Law School]] from 2024 to 2025.<ref name=":2" />

=== Federal judicial service ===

On July 16, 2025, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Mascott to a seat on the United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit vacated by [[Kent A. Jordan]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jennifer Mascott |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Jennifer_Mascott |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> On September 3, 2025, she testified before the [[United States Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Raymond |first=Nate |date=September 3, 2025 |title=Trump nominee to Delaware appeals court seat questioned on lack of ties to state |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/trump-nominee-delaware-appeals-court-seat-questioned-lack-ties-state-2025-09-03/ |work= |location= |publisher=Reuters |access-date=September 3, 2025}}</ref> On October 1, 2025, her nomination was reported from the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] by a 12–10 [[party-line vote]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Farah |first=Lesley Clark, Niina H. |date=2025-10-02 |title=Panel approves another Trump appeals court pick |url=https://www.eenews.net/articles/panel-approves-another-trump-appeals-court-nominee/ |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=E&E News by POLITICO |language=en-US}}</ref> On October 9, 2025, Mascott's nomination was confirmed by a 50–47 vote.<ref name=":1" />

Following her judicial appointment, Mascott continued to work in the leadership of public relations firm Adfero until it went out of business in June 2026.<ref name=":0" />

==Personal life==
Mascott was married to Jeff Mascott, who died of [[pancreatic cancer]] in February 2023 at the age of 48.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blackman |first=Josh |date=2023-03-07 |title=Professor Jennifer Mascott, Winner of 2023 Joseph Story Award |url=https://reason.com/volokh/2023/03/07/professor-jennifer-mascott-winner-of-2023-joseph-story-award/ |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Reason |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Daniels |first=Eugene |last2=Lizza |first2=Ryan |last3=Bade |first3=Rachael |date=2023-03-10 |title=Playbook: The GOP’s 2024 picture snaps into focus |url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook/2023/03/10/the-gops-2024-picture-snaps-into-focus-00086519 |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref>

