'''Jill Anne Pryor''' (born in 1963)<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.martindale.com/Jill-A-Pryor/869843-lawyer.htm|title=Hon. Jill A. Pryor Lawyer Profile on Martindale.com|access-date=7 April 2017}}</ref> is a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit]]. Pryor was born in [[Harrisburg]], [[Pennsylvania]].

==Education==

Pryor received her [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in 1985 from the [[College of William & Mary]], graduating [[Phi Beta Kappa]] and [[Omicron Delta Kappa]]. She then attended [[Yale Law School]], where she was an editor of the ''[[Yale Law Journal]]''. She graduated with a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1988.

Pryor was a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[James Larry Edmondson]] of the United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit from 1988 to 1989.<ref name="archives.gov">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2012/02/16/president-obama-nominates-jill-pryor-serve-us-court-appeals|title=President Obama Nominates Jill A. Pryor to Serve on the US Court of Appeals|date=16 February 2012|access-date=7 April 2017}}</ref>

==Career==
Pryor joined the [[white shoe]] law firm of Bondurant, Mixson & Elmore LLP in [[Atlanta]] as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] in 1989, becoming a [[Law firm partner|partner]] in 1997, a position she held before going on the bench in 2014. In private practice, she represented both plaintiffs and defendants in [[civil litigation]] in state and federal courts at both the [[trial]] and [[appellate]] level, including in  [[tort law]], [[contract law]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.martindale.com/attorney/ms-jill-pryor-869843/|title=Ms. Jill Pryor &#124; Lawyer|website=Martindale.com|access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref>, [[intellectual property]], [[shareholder resolution]], and [[corporate governance]] cases.<ref>{{Cite web | title=Ms. Jill Pryor Profile {{!}} Atlanta, GA Lawyer {{!}} Martindale.com | url=https://www.martindale.com/attorney/hon-jill-a-pryor-869843/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230421164233/https://www.martindale.com/attorney/hon-jill-a-pryor-869843/ | access-date=2025-04-07 | archive-date=2023-04-21}}</ref> She has served on the State Bar of [[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]] Board of Governors and on the Board of Directors for the Georgia Legal Services Program. She has served as President of the Georgia Association of Women Lawyers and as Chair of the State Bar of Georgia's Appellate Practice Section. Additionally, Pryor was formerly a member of the Lawyers Advisory Committee of the United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit as well as a member of the Executive Committee of the American Bar Association's Council of Appellate Lawyers.<ref name="archives.gov"/>

==Federal judicial service==
On February 16, 2012, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Pryor to be a United States Circuit Judge of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit]] to replace Judge [[Stanley F. Birch Jr.]], who retired in 2010.<ref name="archives.gov"/> Both of Georgia's U.S. Senators, [[Johnny Isakson]] and [[Saxby Chambliss]], refused to return the "[[Blue slip (U.S. Senate)|blue slip]]s" on her nomination, effectively blocking the nomination. On January 2, 2013, her nomination was returned to the President because of the ''[[sine die]]'' adjournment of the Senate.

On January 3, 2013, she was renominated to the same judgeship.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/01/03/president-obama-re-nominates-thirty-three-federal-judgeships|title=President Obama Re-nominates Thirty-Three to Federal Judgeships|date=3 January 2013|access-date=7 April 2017}}</ref> She received a hearing before the full panel of the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on May 13, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|title=Judicial Nominations|date=13 May 2014 |url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/judicial-nominations-2014-05-13|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary}}</ref> On June 19, 2014 her nomination was reported out of committee by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting%20-%2006-19-14.pdf | title=Executive Business Meeting | publisher=Committee on the Judiciary | work=United States Senate | access-date=19 June 2014}}</ref>

On July 30, 2014, Senate Majority Leader [[Harry Reid]] motioned to invoke [[cloture]] on Pryor's nomination. On July 31, 2014, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on Pryor's nomination by a 58–33 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1132/vote_113_2_00256.htm|title=U.S. Senate: U.S. Senate Roll Call Votes 113th Congress - 2nd Session|website=Senate.gov|access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref> On September 8, 2014, her nomination was confirmed by a 97–0 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1132/vote_113_2_00257.htm?congress=113&session=2&vote=00257|title=U.S. Senate: U.S. Senate Roll Call Votes 113th Congress - 2nd Session|website=Senate.gov|access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref> She received her judicial commission on September 9, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/pryor-jill-anne|title=Pryor, Jill Anne – Federal Judicial Center|website=Fjc.gov}}</ref> She took the oath of office on October 6, 2014.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.ca11.uscourts.gov/judges/hon-jill-a-pryor | title=Hon. Jill A. Pryor | publisher=[[United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit]]}}</ref>

==Notable rulings==
* In ''Jones et al. v. DeSantis'', a 2020 voting rights case, Pryor wrote a scathing [[dissenting opinion]]. [[2018 Florida Amendment 4]] permitted former felons to vote; however, [[Florida Governor]] [[Ron DeSantis]] signed a law that required former felons to pay all legal fees before being eligible to vote again, despite some of them not knowing how much they owed.<ref>{{Bluebook journal |first=|last=Note|title=Recent Case: Eleventh Circuit Upholds Statute Limiting Constitutional Amendment on Felon Reenfranchisement|volume=134 |journal=[[Harvard Law Review|Harv. L. Rev.]] |page=2291|url=https://harvardlawreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/134-Harv.-L.-Rev.-2291.pdf|year=2021}}</ref> By a 6-4 vote, the 11th circuit upheld that law.<ref>{{cite court |litigants=Jones v. Governor of Florida|vol=975|reporter=F.3d|opinion=1016|court=11th Cir.|date=2020|url=https://scholar.google.com/scholar_case?case=14521898062337340878}}</ref> Pryor wrote "The majority today deprives the plaintiffs and countless others like them of opportunity and equality in voting through its denial of the plaintiffs’ [[due process]], [[Twenty-fourth Amendment to the United States Constitution|Twenty-fourth Amendment]], and [[equal protection]] claims. I dissent."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.courthousenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/fla-felons-ca11.pdf|title=KELVIN LEON JONES, ROSEMARY MCCOY, et al., PLAINTIFFS-APPELLEES, versus GOVERNOR OF FLORIDA, FLORIDA SECRETARY OF STATE, DEFENDANTS-APPELLANTS|website=Courthouse News|date=September 11, 2020|access-date=November 29, 2021}}</ref>
* In ''[[Adams ex rel. Kasper v. School Board of St. Johns County, Florida]] (2022)'', a [[transgender]] rights case, Pryor wrote a comprehensive [[dissenting opinion]].<ref>{{cite web |url= https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Adams_ex_rel._Kasper_v._School_Board_of_St._Johns_County,_Florida_(2022)/Opinion_of_Judge_Jill_Pryor |title=Adams ex rel. Kasper v. School Board of St. Johns County, Florida (2022)/Opinion of Judge Jill Pryor |last=Pryor |first=Jill |date=December 30, 2022 |access-date=May 26, 2025}}</ref> Pryor wrote that “the majority opinion simply declares — without any basis — that a person’s “biological sex” is {{sic|comprised|hide=y}} solely of chromosomal structure and birth-assigned sex… in disregard of the record evidence — evidence the majority does not contest — which demonstrates that gender identity is an immutable, biological component of a person’s sex… In sum, the majority opinion reverses the district court without addressing the question presented, without concluding that a single factual finding is clearly erroneous, without discussing any of the unrebutted expert testimony, and without putting the School District to its evidentiary burden.”
* In ''Warren v. DeSantis'', a 2024 First Amendment retaliation case, Pryor wrote a unanimous panel opinion vacating a lower court's decision. The lower court concluded that [[Florida Governor]] [[Ron DeSantis]]'s suspension of elected State Attorney [[Andrew H. Warren|Andrew Warren]] did not violate the [[First Amendment to the United States Constitution|First Amendment]].<ref name=":0">''Warren v. DeSantis'', [https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca11-23-10459/pdf/USCOURTS-ca11-23-10459-0.pdf 90 F.4th 1115] (11th Cir. 2024).</ref> Warren, a Democrat, adopted policies creating nonprosecution presumptions and signed advocacy statements, including one criticizing bills targeting the transgender community and one committing "to exercise [his] well-settled discretion and refrain from prosecuting those who seek, provide, or support abortions.”<ref name=":0" /> Based on Warren’s policies and advocacy, DeSantis suspended him from office and appointed a political ally to replace him.<ref name=":0" /> Warren sued, and the district court concluded that DeSantis would have suspended Warren based on activities unprotected by the First Amendment.<ref name=":0" /> The Eleventh Circuit disagreed, concluding that the district court erred in concluding that the First Amendment did not protect Warren’s support of a sentence in the advocacy statement about prosecuting abortion cases and in concluding that the First Amendment did not preclude DeSantis from suspending Warren to gain political benefit from bringing down a reform prosecutor.<ref name=":0" /> It sent the case back to the district court to reexamine whether DeSantis would have suspended Warren based solely on the unprotected activities that motivated the suspension.<ref name=":0" /> Judge [[Kevin Newsom]] [[ concurring opinion|concurred]], stressing that Warren's activities included an "expression of pure political speech made by an elected government official, and thus entitled to full First Amendment protection" and noting that Pryor's opinion "correctly reverses and remands the case for a re-weighing."<ref name=":0" />

