'''Joan Louise Larsen''' (born December 1, 1968) is an American attorney serving as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]]. She previously was an associate justice of the [[Michigan Supreme Court]] from 2015 to 2017.

== Early life and education ==
Larsen was born on December 1, 1968, in [[Waterloo, Iowa]]. She graduated from [[Herbert Hoover High School (Iowa)|Herbert Hoover High School]] in Des Moines in 1985, and she earned her [[Bachelor of Arts]] from the [[University of Northern Iowa]] and received her [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[Northwestern University Pritzker School of Law]],<ref name=Snyder>{{cite news |url = http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2015/09/30/snyder-appoints-joan-larsen-supreme-court/73079114/ |title = Snyder appoints Joan Larsen to Supreme Court |work = [[Detroit Free Press]] |first = Paul |last = Egan |date = September 30, 2015 |access-date = November 4, 2015 }}</ref> where she graduated first in her class in 1993.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://courts.mi.gov/Courts/MichiganSupremeCourt/justices/Pages/Justice-Joan-L.-Larsen.aspx |title = Justice Joan L. Larsen |publisher = [[Michigan Supreme Court]] |access-date = November 4, 2015 |archive-date = December 13, 2015 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20151213120858/http://courts.mi.gov/courts/michigansupremecourt/justices/pages/justice-joan-l.-larsen.aspx |url-status = dead }}</ref> While at Northwestern, she served as articles editor of the ''[[Northwestern University Law Review]]''.

== Career ==
Larsen became a professor at the [[University of Michigan Law School]] in 1998. She [[Law clerk|clerked]] for Judge [[David B. Sentelle]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit]] and for Justice [[Antonin Scalia]] of the [[United States Supreme Court]] during the 1994 term.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Larsen |first1 = Joan L. |title = What I Learned From Justice Scalia |url = https://www.nytimes.com/2016/02/16/opinion/what-i-learned-from-justice-scalia.html?_r=0 |access-date = April 13, 2017 |work = [[The New York Times]] |date = February 16, 2016 }}</ref> She served as Deputy Assistant Attorney General in the [[United States Department of Justice]] [[Office of Legal Counsel]] from January 2002 to May 2003 in the administration of President [[George W. Bush]].<ref>{{cite news |url = http://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2015/09/30/michigan-supreme-court/73079330/ |title = Snyder picks UM law professor Larsen for Supreme Court |work = [[The Detroit News]] |first = Gary |last = Heinlein |date = September 30, 2015 |access-date = November 5, 2015 }}</ref> Larsen did not contribute to the OLC's [[Torture Memos]], and in March 2002 she authored a memo addressing detainee court access.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Howe |first1 = Amy |title = Potential nominee profile: Joan Larsen |url = http://www.scotusblog.com/2017/01/potential-nominee-profile-joan-larsen/ |access-date = 30 June 2018 |work = [[SCOTUSblog]] |date = 9 January 2017 }}</ref>

=== Michigan Supreme Court ===
On October 1, 2015, Larsen was appointed to the [[Michigan Supreme Court]] by Governor [[Rick Snyder]]<ref name=Snyder/> to replace Justice [[Mary Beth Kelly]], who announced plans to resign and return to private practice, effective October 1, 2015.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2015/08/17/mary-beth-kelly-michigan-supreme-court/31844755/ |title = Kelly stepping down from Michigan Supreme Court |first = Paul |last = Egan |work = Detroit Free Press |date = August 17, 2015 |access-date = November 1, 2017 }}</ref> She was elected on November 8, 2016, to fill the remainder of Kelly's unexpired term, which ran through the end of 2018.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.crainsdetroit.com/article/20150930/NEWS01/150939859/um-law-professor-joan-larsen-appointed-to-michigan-supreme-court |title = UM law professor Joan Larsen appointed to Michigan Supreme Court |first = David |last = Eggert |work = Crain's Detroit Business |agency = Associated Press |date = September 30, 2015 |access-date = November 5, 2015 }}</ref> Larsen received 58.7% of the vote in a three-way race against Deborah Thomas and Kerry Morgan.<ref>{{cite news |title = Michigan Supreme Court Partial Term Results: Joan Larsen Wins |url = https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/michigan-supreme-court-partial-term |access-date = 26 April 2018 |work = [[The New York Times]] |date = August 1, 2017 }}</ref> She was on Republican presidential nominee [[Donald Trump]]'s May 2016 list of potential Supreme Court justices.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Colvin |first1 = Jill |title = Trump Unveils List of His Top Supreme Court Picks |url = http://hosted.ap.org/dynamic/stories/U/US_GOP_2016_TRUMP_SUPREME_COURT?SITE=AP&SECTION=HOME&TEMPLATE=DEFAULT&CTIME=2016-05-18-14-08-40 |access-date = May 18, 2016 |agency = Associated Press |url-status = dead |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160519104509/http://hosted.ap.org/dynamic/stories/U/US_GOP_2016_TRUMP_SUPREME_COURT?SITE=AP&SECTION=HOME&TEMPLATE=DEFAULT&CTIME=2016-05-18-14-08-40 |archive-date = May 19, 2016 }}</ref> Her service was terminated due to her appointment to the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]].

=== Federal judicial service ===
On May 8, 2017, President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Larsen to serve as a United States Circuit Judge of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]], to the seat soon to be vacated by Judge [[David McKeague]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/05/08/report-trump-name-justice-joan-larsen-6th-circuit/101421602/|title=President Trump names Justice Joan Larsen to U.S. 6th Circuit|first=Paul|last=Egan|work=Detroit Free Press|date=May 8, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release|url =https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2017/05/08/president-donald-j-trump-announces-judicial-candidate-nominations |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170509012131/https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2017/05/08/president-donald-j-trump-announces-judicial-candidate-nominations |url-status = dead |archive-date = May 9, 2017 |title = President Donald J. Trump Announces Judicial Candidate Nominations |date = May 8, 2017 |publisher = [[The White House]] }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title = Presidential Nomination 371, 115th United States Congress |date = May 8, 2017 |url = https://www.congress.gov/nomination/115th-congress/371 |publisher = [[United States Congress]] |access-date = June 30, 2018 }}</ref> Larsen's nomination was held up for months by Michigan's Senators, [[Debbie Stabenow]] and [[Gary Peters (politician)|Gary Peters]]. The two Democrats initially refused to return their [[Blue slip (U.S. Senate)|blue slip]]s for Larsen, an informal [[United States Senate]] practice that essentially gives Senators veto power over federal judicial nominees from their home state.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/07/04/trump-michigan-larsen-judicial-nominee-stabenow-peters/103427820/|title=Trump's Michigan judicial nominee hits a snag|first=Melissa|last=Nann Burke|work=The Detroit News|date=July 4, 2017|access-date=October 6, 2017}}</ref> Larsen met separately with Peters and Stabenow on July 26, 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2017/07/24/stabenow-peters-meet-larsen/103972892/ |title = Stabenow, Peters to meet with judicial nominee Larsen |first = Melissa |last = Nann Burke |work = The Detroit News |date = July 24, 2017 |access-date = October 6, 2017 }}</ref> Stabenow and Peters both returned blue slips in August 2017, allowing Larsen's hearing to move forward.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/08/04/joan-larson-nomination-federal-court/540658001/ |title = Stabenow, Peters let Joan Larsen nomination move forward |first = Todd |last = Spangler |work = Detroit Free Press |date = August 4, 2017 |access-date = October 6, 2017 }}</ref>

A hearing on Larsen's nomination before the [[Senate Judiciary Committee]] was held on September 6, 2017.<ref>[https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/08/08/2017/nominations United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary: Nominations for September 6, 2017]</ref> On October 5, 2017, the Judiciary Committee reported her nomination out of committee by an 11–9 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/10-05-17%20Results%20of%20Executive%20Business%20Meeting.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – October 5, 2017|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|accessdate=April 25, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/10/05/larsen-nomination-court-trump-michigan-judge/735206001/|title=Michigan Justice Joan Larsen inches closer to becoming federal appeals court judge |first = Todd |last = Spangler |work = Detroit Free Press |date = October 5, 2017 |access-date = October 6, 2017 }}</ref> Senate Majority Leader [[Mitch McConnell]] filed a [[cloture]] motion to limit debate on Larsen's nomination on October 26, 2017,<ref>{{cite web |url = https://www.senate.gov/pagelayout/reference/cloture_motions/115.htm |title = Cloture Motions - 115th Congress |publisher = United States Senate |date = October 26, 2017 }}</ref> clearing the path for the Senate to vote in the first week of November 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.rollcall.com/news/politics/mcconnell-sets-table-full-week-judicial-wars |title = McConnell Sets Table for Full Week of Judicial Wars |date = October 26, 2017 |work = Roll Call |first = Niels |last = Lesniewski |access-date = October 27, 2017 |archive-date = October 27, 2017 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20171027025917/https://www.rollcall.com/news/politics/mcconnell-sets-table-full-week-judicial-wars |url-status = dead }}</ref> On October 31, 2017, the Senate invoked cloture on the nomination by a 60–38 vote, with Stabenow and Peters voting to proceed with her nomination and give Larsen a final up-or-down vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=1&vote=00256|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Joan Louise Larsen to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Sixth Circuit)|publisher=[[United States Senate]]|date=October 31, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2017/11/01/senate-larsen-vote-nomination/107208350/|title=Senate set to vote this week on Larsen nomination|first=Melissa|last=Nann Burke|work=The Detroit News|date=October 31, 2017}}</ref> On November 1, 2017, Larsen was confirmed by a 60–38 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/11/01/joan-larsen-federal-appeals-court/820453001/|title=U.S. Senate confirms appointment of Joan Larsen to federal appeals court|first=Paul|last=Egan|work=Detroit Free Press|date=November 1, 2017}}</ref> Both Stabenow and Peters, along with six other Senate Democrats, joined all 52 Senate Republicans to confirm Larsen.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=115&session=1&vote=00257|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Joan Louise Larsen, of Michigan, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Sixth Circuit)|publisher=United States Senate|date=November 1, 2017}}</ref> She received her commission on November 2, 2017.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=3984221|inline=yes}}</ref><ref name=COMMISSION>{{cite web|url=http://www.ca6.uscourts.gov/judges|title=Judges|publisher=United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit|access-date=November 18, 2017}}</ref>

====Notable cases as a circuit court judge====

In ''In re MCP NO. 165'', 21 F.4th 357 (6th Cir. 2021), Larsen dissented from the panel's decision to dissolve a stay that enjoined an [[Occupational Safety and Health Administration|OSHA]] rule requiring certain employers with more than 100 employees to implement a mandatory vaccination policy or otherwise have unvaccinated employees wear masks and undergo weekly [[COVID-19]] testing.  Judge Larsen's dissent argues that the [[United States Secretary of Labor]] "lacks the authority to issue" the vaccine mandate, and that OSHA's rule would run afoul of the [[major questions doctrine]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=UNITED STATES COURT OF APPEALS |url=https://www.opn.ca6.uscourts.gov/opinions.pdf/21a0287p-06.pdf |access-date=March 2, 2025 |website=www.opn.ca6.uscourts.gov}}</ref> On appeal, the U.S. Supreme Court agreed with Judge Larsen and stayed the OSHA rule.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 13, 2022 |title=21A244 National Federation of Independent Business v. OSHA |url=https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/21a244_hgci.pdf |access-date=March 2, 2025 |website=www.supremecourt.gov}}</ref>

