'''John Carbone Porfilio''' (born October 14, 1934) is an inactive [[United States federal judge|Senior United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit]] and a former United States District Judge of the [[United States District Court for the District of Colorado]].

==Education and career==

Born '''John Carbone Porfilio''' in [[Denver]], [[Colorado]], his name was changed to '''John Porfilio Moore''' when his mother remarried and his step father adopted him.<ref>{{cite journal|url=http://heinonline.org/HOL/LandingPage?handle=hein.journals/denlr71&div=16&id=&page=|title=The Honorable John P. Moore, United States Court of Appeals, Tenth Circuit Judicial Profiles 71 Denver University Law Review 1993-1994|journal=Denver University Law Review|volume=71|page=33|last1=Kaplan|first1=Doris G.}}</ref> Porfilio received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree from the [[University of Denver]] in 1956 and a [[Bachelor of Laws]] from the [[University of Denver College of Law]] in 1959. He was in private practice in Denver from 1959 to 1962. He was an assistant state attorney general of Colorado from 1962 to 1968. He was the Deputy Attorney General of Colorado from 1968 to 1972 under Attorney General [[Duke W. Dunbar|Duke Dunbar]]. In 1972, Dunbar died in office. Governor [[John Arthur Love|John Love]] appointed Moore to the position of [[Colorado Attorney General]].<ref name="CO AG Bio"/> He served as Colorado Attorney General from 1972 until 1975.<ref name="fjc.gov">{{cite web|url=https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/porfilio-john-carbone|title=Porfilio, John Carbone - Federal Judicial Center|website=www.fjc.gov}}</ref> In the 1974 general election, [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] candidate and former state legislator [[J.D. MacFarlane]] defeated Moore for a full-term.<ref name="CO AG Bio">{{cite web|title=John P. Moore - 32nd Colorado Attorney General Term: 1973-1974|publisher=[[Colorado Attorney General]]|location=[[Denver, Colorado]]|url=https://coag.gov/about-us/history-colorados-attorneys-general/john-p-moore|access-date=February 3, 2022|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161129032409/https://coag.gov/about-us/history-colorados-attorneys-general/john-p-moore#_ftnref1|archive-date=November 29, 2016}}</ref>

==Federal judicial service==

On January 15, 1975, Chief Judge [[Alfred A. Arraj]] swore Moore into office as a [[United States bankruptcy court|United States Bankruptcy Judge]] of the District of Colorado, filling a long vacant position due to a rise in bankruptcy filings.<ref name="Kaplan 1993">Doris G. Kaplan, [https://digitalcommons.du.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2262&context=dlr The Honorable John P. Moore, United States Court of Appeals, Tenth Circuit], 71 Denv. U.L. Rev. 33 (1993).</ref> He served in this position from 1975 to 1982.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> On May 18, 1982, Porfilio was nominated by President [[Ronald Reagan]] to a seat on the [[United States District Court for the District of Colorado]] vacated by Judge [[Fred M. Winner]]. Porfilio was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] on June 24, 1982, and received his commission on June 25, 1982. Porfilio served in that capacity until May 13, 1985, due to elevation to the court of appeals.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

On April 5, 1985, Reagan nominated Porfilio to a seat on the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit]] vacated by Judge [[Robert Hugh McWilliams Jr.]] Porfilio was confirmed by the Senate on May 3, 1985, and received his commission on May 10, 1985. He assumed [[senior status]] on October 15, 1999.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> On January 8, 1996, Porfillio legally changed his name back to his birth name.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

