'''John Zihun Lee''' (born March 30, 1968)<ref>{{cite web | url=https://vettingroom.org/2022/04/25/judge-john-lee/ | title=Judge John Lee – Nominee to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit | date=April 25, 2022 }}</ref> is an American lawyer serving as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]]. He served as a United States district judge of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Illinois]] from 2012 to 2022.

==Biography==

Lee was born in [[Aachen]], [[Germany]].<ref name="Questionnaire">{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/JohnLee-PublicQuestionnaire.pdf|title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire}}</ref> He graduated from [[Harvard College]] in 1989 with a [[Bachelor of Arts]], ''[[Latin honors#United States|magna cum laude]]'', and from [[Harvard Law School]] in 1992 with a [[Juris Doctor]], ''cum laude''.

He first worked as a trial attorney in the [[United States Department of Justice Environment and Natural Resources Division]]. He worked as an associate at [[Mayer Brown]], a [[white shoe]] Chicago law firm, from 1994 to 1996 and the [[boutique law firm]] of Grippo & Elden LLC from 1996 to 1999. He was a partner at Freeborn & Peters LLP in Chicago, where he handled complex federal [[civil litigation]].<ref name="White House">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/10/president-obama-nominates-four-serve-us-district-court-bench|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|title=President Obama Nominates Four to Serve on the US District Court Bench|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|date=November 10, 2011|access-date=May 2, 2022|archive-date=January 19, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170119043107/https://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/10/president-obama-nominates-four-serve-us-district-court-bench|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393976|inline=yes}}</ref> In the fall of 2000, he was an [[adjunct professor]] at [[University of Illinois Chicago School of Law|The John Marshall Law School]] teaching a course on antitrust law. In the fall terms of 1990 and 1991, while a [[teaching fellow]] at [[Harvard University]], he taught a class on [[Greek mythology]].<ref name="Questionnaire" />

== Federal judicial service ==
=== District court service ===
On November 10, 2011, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Lee to be a [[United States federal judge|United States district judge]] for the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Illinois]].<ref name="White House"/> He was nominated to the seat vacated by Judge [[David H. Coar]], who assumed [[senior status]] on August 12, 2009. Lee received his hearing by the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on January 26, 2012, and his nomination was favorably reported by the committee on February 16, 2012, by a [[voice vote]], with Senator [[Mike Lee]] recording the only no vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/ExecutiveBusinessMeetingResults-02-16-2012.pdf|title= Results of Executive Business Meeting - February 16, 2012|date=February 16, 2012}}</ref> On May 7, 2012, his nomination was confirmed by a voice vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/112th-congress/1116|title=PN1116 - Nomination of John Z. Lee for the Judiciary, 112th Congress (2011-2012)|date=May 7, 2012}}</ref> He received his commission on May 8, 2012.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> His service as a district judge was terminated on September 12, 2022, when he was elevated to the court of appeals.<ref name="fjc.gov"/>

=== Notable rulings ===
In July 2018, Lee, sitting by designation, wrote for the unanimous panel of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]] in ''Doe No. 55 v. Madison Metropolitan School District'' when it found that the [[Madison Metropolitan School District]] was not liable for child sex abuse of a student by its security guard because the school's principal had not had actual knowledge of the abuse.<ref>{{Bluebook journal|last=|first=|title=Recent Case: Seventh Circuit Holds School Not Liable in Case of Child Sex Abuse|volume=132|journal=[[Harvard Law Review|Harv. L. Rev.]]|page=1550|url=https://harvardlawreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/1550-1557_Online.pdf|year=2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite court|litigants=Doe No. 55 v. Madison Metropolitan School District|vol=897|reporter=F.3d|opinion=819|court=7th Cir.|date=2018|url=https://scholar.google.com/scholar_case?case=1485820932200894170}}</ref> This decision was affirmed [[en banc]].<ref>C.S. v. Madison Metro. Sch. Dist., No. 17-1521, --- F.4th ----, 2022 WL 1466249 (7th Cir. May 10, 2022).</ref>

On May 3, 2020, Lee ruled that Governor [[J. B. Pritzker]]'s [[stay-at-home order]] during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Illinois]] was constitutional, after an [[Evangelical church]] sought an injunction against the order in order to continue worship services.<ref>{{Cite web|title=U.S. district judge rules Pritzker's stay-at-home order constitutional|url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/coronavirus/2020/5/3/21245936/stay-at-home-order-constitutional-us-district-court-john-lee-beloved-church-lena-illinois|last=Pope|first=Ben|date=May 3, 2020|website=Chicago Sun-Times|language=en|access-date=May 22, 2020}}</ref>

In 2026, Lee upheld a lower court's decision that ruled the possession of AI-generated child sex abuse material (CSAM) was protected by the [[First Amendment to the United States Constitution|First Amendment]]. He cited a previous Supreme Court ruling, which found that CSAM that did not portray a real person was not considered child pornography; he recommended the Supreme Court revisit the issue in the face of technological advances.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tiku |first=Nitasha |date=2026-08-27 |title=Federal judge warns law is being left behind by AI child sex abuse images |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2026/08/26/federal-judge-warns-law-is-being-left-behind-by-ai-sex-abuse-images/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

=== Court of appeals service ===

On April 13, 2022, President [[Joe Biden]] announced his intent to nominate Lee to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] for the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]].<ref name="WHBio">{{Cite press release|title=President Biden Names Sixteenth Round of Judicial Nominees|date=April 3, 2022|publisher=The White House|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/04/13/president-biden-names-sixteenth-round-of-judicial-nominees/|access-date=April 13, 2022|archive-date=April 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220413124655/https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/04/13/president-biden-names-sixteenth-round-of-judicial-nominees/|url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref> On April 25, 2022, his nomination was sent to the Senate. President Biden nominated Lee to the seat to be vacated by Judge [[Diane Wood]], who announced her intent to assume [[senior status]] upon confirmation of a successor.<ref>{{Cite press release|title=Nominations and Withdrawals Sent to the Senate|date=April 25, 2022|publisher=The White House|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/04/25/nominations-and-withdrawals-sent-to-the-senate-64/|access-date=May 2, 2022|archive-date=April 29, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220429015428/https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/04/25/nominations-and-withdrawals-sent-to-the-senate-64/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref> On May 11, 2022, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nominations|date=May 10, 2022|publisher=[[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary]]|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/meetings/05/11/2022/nominations}}</ref> During his confirmation hearing, Republican senators criticized him over his decision to uphold [[J. B. Pritzker]]'s stay-at-home order, which limited in-person church services.<ref>{{cite news|last=Raymond|first=Nate|title=Republicans grill Biden judicial pick for upholding COVID-19 religious service curbs|url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/republicans-grill-biden-judicial-pick-upholding-covid-19-religious-service-curbs-2022-05-11/|website=Reuters}}</ref> On June 9, 2022, his nomination was favorably reported by the committee by a 12–8–2 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/EBM%20Results%20-%202022-06-09.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – June 9, 2022|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|accessdate=June 9, 2022}}</ref> On August 7, 2022, Majority Leader [[Chuck Schumer]] filed [[cloture]] on his nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |title=PN1965 — John Z. Lee — The Judiciary |url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/117th-congress/1965 |access-date=2023-07-07 |website=Congress.gov}}</ref> On September 6, 2022, the [[United States Senate]] invoked cloture on his nomination by a 48–42 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|date=September 6, 2022|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: John Z. Lee to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Seventh Circuit)|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1172/vote_117_2_00326.htm|access-date=September 6, 2022|website=United States Senate}}</ref> On September 7, 2022, his nomination was confirmed by a 50–44 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1172/vote_117_2_00327.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: John Z. Lee, of Illinois, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Seventh Circuit)|date=September 7, 2022|website=United States Senate|access-date=September 7, 2022}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on September 9, 2022.<ref name="fjc.gov"/> He is the first Asian American judge to ever serve on the 7th Circuit.<ref>{{cite web|last=Raymond|first=Nate|title=Senate Democrats kick-off post-recess judicial nominee push with first vote|url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/senate-democrats-kick-off-post-recess-judicial-nominee-push-with-first-vote-2022-09-07/|website=Reuters|access-date=10 June 2023|language=en|date=8 September 2022}}</ref>

