'''Joshua Dale Dunlap''' (born 1983) is an American lawyer who has served as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit]] since 2025. Before that, he practiced law at the Maine firm [[Pierce Atwood|Pierce Atwood]] from 2009 to 2025.

==Early life and education==

Dunlap was born in 1983, in [[Waterville, Maine]].<ref name="Questionnaire">{{cite web |last=Dunlap |first=Joshua Dale |date=July 30, 2025 |title=Dunlap's Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/dunlap_sjq.pdf |website=judiciary.senate.gov |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=July 31, 2025}}</ref> He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]], ''[[summa cum laude]]'', in 2005 from [[Pensacola Christian College]]. In 2008, he received a [[Juris Doctor]], also ''summa cum laude'', from [[Notre Dame Law School]], where he earned the Colonel [[William J. Hoynes]] Award, the school's highest academic honor, and served as note editor for the ''[[Notre Dame Law Review]]''. Dunlap served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[Paul Joseph Kelly Jr.]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit]] from 2008 to 2009.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.pierceatwood.com/people/joshua-dunlap|title=Joshua D. Dunlap &#124; Pierce Atwood|website=www.pierceatwood.com}}</ref><ref name="Questionnaire"/>

==Career==

In 2009, Dunlap joined the [[law firm]] of [[Pierce Atwood]] in its [[Portland, Maine|Portland]] office as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]].<ref name="Questionnaire"/> In 2018, he was promoted to be a [[Law firm partner|partner]].<ref name="auto1">{{cite news |last1 = Raymond |first1 = Nate |last2 = Martinez |first2 = Christian |date = July 2, 2025 |title = Trump names two new nominees to serve as appeals court judges |url = https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/trump-nominates-maine-litigator-joshua-dunlap-first-circuit-court-2025-07-02/ |work = |location = |publisher = Reuters |access-date = July 3, 2025}}</ref><ref name="auto"/>  His focus was on  commercial litigation at the trial and appellate levels.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fetherston |first1=Jon |title=Senate Confirms Maine Attorney Joshua Dunlap to First Circuit in Party-Line Vote |url=https://www.themainewire.com/2025/11/senate-confirms-maine-attorney-joshua-dunlap-to-first-circuit-in-party-line-vote/ |access-date=31 December 2025 |work=The Maine Wire |date=5 November 2025}}</ref>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On July 2, 2025, President [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Dunlap to a seat vacated by Judge [[William J. Kayatta Jr.]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit]].<ref name="auto1"/> A hearing on his nomination was held on July 30, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nominations|date=July 30, 2025|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/nominations-07-30-2025|access-date=December 17, 2025}}</ref> On September 11, 2025, his nomination was advanced to the [[United States Senate]] by a 12–10 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/download/12/16/2025/20250911_ebm_resultspdf?download=1|title=Results of the Committee Executive Business Meeting|date=September 11, 2025|website=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=December 17, 2025}}</ref><ref name="Questionnaire"/> On October 30, 2025, the Senate invoked cloture on his nomination by a 51–47 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00601.htm|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Joshua D. Dunlap to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the First Circuit)|date=October 30, 2025|access-date=December 17, 2025|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> Dunlap was confirmed on November 4, 2025 by a 52–46 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00604.htm|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Joshua D. Dunlap, of Maine, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the First Circuit)|access-date=November 4, 2025|website=www.senate.gov}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on November 7, 2025.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762074}}</ref>

==Personal==

Dunlap resides in [[Scarborough, Maine|Scarborough]], Maine.<ref name="Questionnaire"/>

