'''Joshua Paul Kolar''' (born 1976)<ref name="Questionnaire">{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/kolar_sjq.pdf|title=Questionnaire for Judicial Nominees|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=September 5, 2023|archive-date=September 6, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230906002633/https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/kolar_sjq.pdf|url-status=bot: unknown}}</ref> is an American lawyer from [[Indiana]] who has served as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]] since 2024. He previously served as a [[United States magistrate judge]] of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Indiana]] from 2019 to 2024.

== Education ==

Kolar received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] from [[Northwestern University]] in 1999 and a [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Northwestern University Pritzker School of Law]] in 2003.<ref name="WHBio">{{Cite press release |title=President Biden Names Thirty-Sixth Round of Judicial Nominees |date=July 27, 2023 |publisher=The White House |location=Washington, D.C. |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2023/07/27/president-biden-names-thirty-sixth-round-of-judicial-nominees/ |access-date=July 27, 2023}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

== Career ==

From 2005 to 2006, Kolar served as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[Wayne Andersen]] of the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Illinois]]. From 2003 to 2005 and 2006 to 2007, he was an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] at [[Mayer Brown]] in their [[Chicago]] office. From 2007 to 2018, he served as an [[assistant United States attorney]] in the United States Attorney's Office for the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Indiana|Northern District of Indiana]]. Kolar also serves as a [[Lieutenant commander (United States)|lieutenant commander]] in the [[United States Navy Reserve]]. He has served in the United States Navy Reserve since 2009 and was on active duty in [[Afghanistan]] from 2014 to 2015.<ref name="WHBio" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Raymond |first=Nate |date=July 27, 2023 |title=Biden nominates 2 US Navy Reserve officers to federal appeals courts |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/biden-nominates-2-us-navy-reserve-officers-federal-appeals-courts-2023-07-27/ |access-date=December 12, 2023}}</ref>

=== Federal judicial service ===

==== Magistrate judge ====

In 2018, Kolar was selected as a United States magistrate judge for the Northern District of Indiana in the [[Hammond, Indiana|Hammond]] division.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ross |first=Doug |date=2018-08-13 |title=Kolar named Region's new federal magistrate |url=https://www.nwitimes.com/news/local/crime-and-courts/kolar-named-regions-new-federal-magistrate/article_ad0683fc-e5ba-5866-8696-f638bfe108d7.html |access-date=2023-12-12 |website=nwitimes.com |language=en}}</ref> He filled the vacancy left by the retirement of Judge Paul R. Cherry.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Selection of Magistrate Judge in Hammond Division |date=August 13, 2018 |publisher=United States District Court for the Northern District of Indiana |url=https://www.innd.uscourts.gov/news/selection-magistrate-judge-hammond-division |access-date=July 27, 2023 |archive-date=July 27, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230727143945/https://www.innd.uscourts.gov/news/selection-magistrate-judge-hammond-division |url-status=dead }}</ref> He assumed office on January 1, 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Joshua P. Kolar {{!}} United States Courts |url=https://www.uscourts.gov/judicial-milestones/joshua-p-kolar |access-date=July 27, 2023 |website=www.uscourts.gov |language=en}}</ref> He was later succeeded by Abizer Zanzi.

==== Court of appeals ====

On July 27, 2023, President [[Joe Biden]] nominated Kolar to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Times |date=2023-07-27 |title=Hammond-based federal judge to be nominated to U.S. Court of Appeals |url=https://www.nwitimes.com/news/local/hammond-judge-nominated-to-court-of-appeals/article_371ea2c8-2c96-11ee-853c-8fb18cb956c4.html |access-date=2023-12-12 |website=nwitimes.com |language=en}}</ref> President Biden nominated Kolar to the seat vacated by Judge [[Michael Stephen Kanne]], who died on June 16, 2022.<ref>{{Cite press release|title=Nominations Sent to the Senate|date=July 27, 2023|publisher=The White House|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/07/27/nominations-sent-to-the-senate-117/}}</ref> Republican Senator [[Todd Young]] released a statement after the president nominated Kolar, stating that he supports Kolar.<ref>{{Cite press release|title=Young Statement on Nomination of Judge Joshua P. Kolar to Seventh Circuit Court of Appeals|url=https://www.young.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/young-statement-on-nomination-of-judge-joshua-p-kolar-to-seventh-circuit-court-of-appeals|access-date=July 27, 2023}}</ref> On September 6, 2023, a hearing on his nomination was held before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nominations|date=September 5, 2023|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|location=Washington, D.C.|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/committee-activity/hearings/09/06/2023/nominations}}</ref> During his confirmation hearing, he was questioned by Senator [[Marsha Blackburn]] over a case that he presided over in 2019.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Carson|first=Daniel|title=Kolar fields 1st questions from Senate Judiciary Committee as 7th Circuit nominee|url=https://www.theindianalawyer.com/articles/kolar-fields-1st-questions-from-senate-judiciary-committee-as-7th-circuit-nominee|website=The Indiana Lawyer|access-date=September 7, 2023}}</ref> On September 28, 2023, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 16–5 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2023-09-28_-_ebm_-_results.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – September 28, 2023|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=September 28, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Headley|first=Tiana|title=Biden Bipartisan Circuit Court Picks Advance Out of Committee|url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/environment-and-energy/biden-bipartisan-circuit-court-picks-advance-out-of-committee|website=Bloomberg Law|access-date=September 28, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Weiss |first=Benjamin S. |date=September 28, 2023 |title=Durbin praises bipartisanship as Senate advances more judicial nominees |work=Courthouse News Service |url=https://www.courthousenews.com/durbin-praises-bipartisanship-as-senate-advances-more-judicial-nominees/ |access-date=December 12, 2023}}</ref> On January 3, 2024, his nomination was returned to the President under [[Standing Rules of the United States Senate, Rule XXXI|Rule XXXI, Paragraph 6]] of the [[United States Senate]];<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 8, 2023 |title=PN905 — Joshua Paul Kolar — The Judiciary |url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/118th-congress/905 |website=congress.gov}}</ref> and he was renominated on January 8, 2024.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Nominations Sent to the Senate |date=January 8, 2024 |publisher=The White House |location=Washington, D.C. |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2024/01/08/nominations-sent-to-the-senate-133/}}</ref> On January 18, 2024, his nomination was reported out of committee by a 16–5 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2024-01-18_-_ebm_-_results1.pdf|title=Results of Executive Business Meeting – January 18, 2024|publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|access-date=January 19, 2024|archive-date=January 19, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240119200221/https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2024-01-18_-_ebm_-_results1.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite press release |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Advances Twenty Judicial Nominations, One Executive Nomination to the Full Senate |date=January 18, 2024 |publisher=United States Senate Judiciary Committee |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/press/releases/senate-judiciary-committee-advances-twenty-judicial-nominations-one-executive-nomination-to-the-full-senate |access-date=January 18, 2024}}</ref> On January 25, 2024, the Senate invoked cloture on his nomination by a 66–29 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1182/vote_118_2_00024.htm|title=On the Cloture Motion (Motion to Invoke Cloture: Joshua Paul Kolar to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Seventh Circuit)|website=United States Senate|date=January 25, 2024|access-date=January 25, 2024}}</ref> On January 30, 2024, his nomination was confirmed by a 66–25 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 30, 2024|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Joshua Paul Kolar, of Indiana, to be United States Circuit Judge for the Seventh Circuit)|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1182/vote_118_2_00025.htm|access-date=January 30, 2024|website=United States Senate}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on January 31, 2024.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13761466|inline=yes}}</ref>

