'''Justin Daniel Smith''' (born 1985) is a lawyer who has served as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit]] since 2026. Nominated by [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump]], he was a [[Law firm partner|partner]] at the James Otis Law Group before becoming a federal judge.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://law.missouri.edu/mizzou-law-alumnus-nominated-by-president-for-eighth-circuit-judge-seat/|title=Mizzou Law alumnus nominated by president for Eighth Circuit judge seat|date=February 19, 2026|website=School of Law}}</ref>

== Education ==

Smith graduated from the [[University of Missouri–Kansas City]] with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, ''[[summa cum laude]]'', in 2007 and [[University of Missouri School of Law]] with a [[Juris Doctor]], ''[[magna cum laude]]'', in 2010. In 2010, he was an intern to Judge [[Nanette Kay Laughrey]] of the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Missouri]].<ref name="SJCQ">{{cite web |last=Smith |first=Justin Daniel |date=March 13, 2026 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/smith_sjq2.pdf |website=judiciary.senate.gov |location=Washington, D.C. |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=May 23, 2026}}</ref>

== Career ==
From 2010 to 2016, Smith was an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] at the law firm of [[Shook, Hardy & Bacon]] in [[Kansas City]], where he focused on [[civil litigation]] and [[regulatory law]]. From 2017 to 2018, he was Deputy Counsel to [[Missouri Governor]] [[Eric Greitens]]. From 2018 to 2019, he was [[General Counsel]] to the [[Missouri Department of Agriculture]]. From 2019 to 2023, Smith was a prosecutor in the [[Missouri Attorney General]]'s office. In 2023, Smith was a Senior Advisor to [[United States Senator]] [[Eric Schmitt]]. From 2024 to 2026, he was a [[Partner (law firm)|partner]] at the James Otis Law Group, LLC at its [[St. Louis|Saint Louis]] office.<ref name="SJCQ"/> During this period, Smith served as the personal lawyer of [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump]], and, alongside future [[Solicitor General of the United States|Solicitor General]] [[D. John Sauer]], successfully crafted the legal arguments that would outline the extent of [[presidential immunity]] in ''[[Trump v. United States]].''<ref>{{cite web |last1=Raymond |first1=Nate | url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/2nd-trump-personal-attorney-wins-us-senate-approval-become-appeals-court-judge-2026-06-15/ | title=2nd Trump personal attorney wins US Senate approval to become appeals court judge | website=[[Reuters]] | date=June 16, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref> Additionally, during and at the time of his federal judicial nomination, Smith actively represented Trump in [[E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump#Appeal effort (March 2024–present)|his appeal]] before the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] regarding Trump's civil verdict of [[sexual assault]] against journalist [[E. Jean Carroll]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Mantooth |first1=Fletcher | url=https://www.columbiamissourian.com/news/trump-lawyer-justin-smith-nominated-for-circuit-judge-seat/article_32eb3ab8-bd68-4370-9acd-f42fa37096d9.html | title=Trump lawyer Justin Smith nominated for circuit judge seat | website=[[Columbia Missourian]] | date=February 19, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.abajournal.com/news/article/trump-picks-personal-lawyer-from-e-jean-carroll-case-for-8th-circuit-vacancy | title=Trump picks former personal lawyer from E. Jean Carroll case for 8th Circuit judicial vacancy | website=[[ABA Journal]] | date=February 20, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref>

=== Federal judicial service === 

In late 2025, Senator Eric Schmitt recommended Smith to a seat on the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit]]. President Donald Trump formally nominated him on March 2, 2026 to succeed Judge [[Duane Benton]], who had taken [[senior status]].<ref name="SJCQ"/> On April 15, 2026, he testified before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] regarding his nomination. During his hearing he was questioned on the extent of his professional relationship with Donald Trump, and, whether having acted as his personal lawyer, he would be capable of having the impartiality necessary for a federal judge.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Alafriz |first1=Olivia | url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/us-law-week/trump-lawyer-tapped-for-appeals-court-advanced-by-senate-panel | title=Trump Lawyer Tapped for Appeals Court Advanced by Senate Panel | website=[[Bloomberg Law]] | date=May 14, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Alafriz |first1=Olivia | url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/us-law-week/senate-confirms-another-trump-attorney-to-appeals-court-seat | title=Senate Confirms Another Trump Attorney to Appeals Court Seat | website=[[Bloomberg Law]] | date=June 15, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref> Smith was further questioned regarding his views on whether or not [[Joe Biden]] legitimately won the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 election]], to which Smith responded that "[[United States Congress|Congress]] certified Joe Biden as the president."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Raymond |first1=Nate | url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/senate-democrats-question-loyalty-trump-personal-attorney-nominated-judgeship-2026-04-15/ | title=Senate Democrats question loyalty of Trump personal attorney nominated to judgeship | website=[[Reuters]] | date=April 15, 2026 |access-date=June 23, 2026}}</ref> On May 14, 2026, Smith's nomination was reported to the full Senate by a 12–10 party-line vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/851/7|title=PN851-7 - Nomination of Justin D. Smith for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=May 18, 2026|website=congress.gov}}</ref> On June 11, 2026, the Senate invoked cloture on a 47–43 vote, and on June 15, 2026, his nomination was confirmed by a 48–43 vote. He received his judicial commission on June 18, 2026.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762133}}</ref>

