'''Kimberly Ann Moore''' (née Pace; born June 15, 1968)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/109/crec/2006/09/05/CREC-2006-09-05-pt1-PgS8944.pdf|format=PDF|title=S8944 CONGRESSIONAL RECORD — SENATE|website=Congress,gov|date=September 5, 2006|access-date=July 9, 2026}}</ref> is an American lawyer and jurist serving as [[United States federal judge|chief United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]].

==Early life and education==
Moore was born in [[Halethorpe, Maryland]]. Moore received a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in [[electrical engineering]] in 1990 and a [[Master of Science]] in 1991 from the [[Massachusetts Institute of Technology]]. She received her [[Juris Doctor]], [[Latin honors|''cum laude'']], from the [[Georgetown University Law Center]] in 1994.

==Career==
From 1988 to 1992, Moore was employed in electrical engineering with the [[Naval Surface Warfare Center]]. She worked in private practice as an associate with the law firm of [[Kirkland & Ellis]] in [[Los Angeles]], [[California]] from 1994 to 1995, and then clerked for United States [[Federal Circuit]] Judge [[Glenn L. Archer Jr.]] from 1995 to 1997.

Moore taught at the [[Chicago-Kent College of Law]] from 1997 to 1999 and at the [[University of Maryland Francis King Carey School of Law]] from 1999 to 2000. She subsequently taught at the [[George Mason University School of Law]] as an associate professor from 2000 to 2004 and professor of law from 2004 until her appointment. Prior to her appointment, Moore also served as a mediator for the Federal Circuit Appellate Mediation Pilot Program. She also served as a lecturer for the [[Barbri]] Patent Bar Review, a review program for the [[USPTO registration examination]].

===Federal judicial service===
On May 18, 2006, President [[George W. Bush]] nominated Moore to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]]. She was nominated to fill a seat vacated by Judge [[Raymond C. Clevenger III]], who assumed [[senior status]] on February 1, 2006. On September 5, 2006, her nomination was confirmed in the Senate by a 92–0 vote.<ref>[[United States Senate]], [https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=109&session=2&vote=00231 On the Nomination (Confirmation Kimberly Ann Moore, of Virginia, to be U.S. Circuit Judge)]. Retrieved on September 12, 2006.</ref> She received her commission on September 8, 2006.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=1392481|inline=yes}}</ref>  On May 22, 2021, Moore became chief judge, succeeding [[Sharon Prost]].<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Announcement of Chief Judge Transition at the Federal Circuit |date=April 21, 2021 |location=Washington, D.C. |url=http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/announcements/announcement-chief-judge-transition-federal-circuit |quote=The Honorable Kimberly A. Moore will become Chief Judge of the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit on May 22, 2021. She will succeed the Honorable Sharon Prost who has served as Chief Judge since May 31, 2014 |access-date=April 27, 2021 |archive-date=April 27, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210427192936/http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/announcements/announcement-chief-judge-transition-federal-circuit |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.law.com/plc-nlj/2021/05/06/sharon-prost-ends-her-tenure-atop-a-more-unified-federal-circuit/?region=none&slreturn=20230308110005 |title=Sharon Prost Ends Her Tenure Atop a More Unified Federal Circuit|publisher=National Law Journal|first1=Scott|last1=Graham|date=May 6, 2021}}</ref>

In December 2015, Moore wrote the Federal Circuit opinion in ''[[In re Tam]]'', allowing Asian-American band [[The Slants]] to register their name, and overturning a previous ruling that had upheld the [[United States Patent and Trademark Office]]'s ability to reject trademarks it deemed offensive or disparaging to others.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Gardner |first1=Eriq |title=Rock Band Wins First Amendment Appeal Over "Disparaging" Trademarks |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/thr-esq/rock-band-wins-first-amendment-850689 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |accessdate=26 February 2019 |language=en |date=22 December 2015}}</ref>

===Publications===
Moore authored ''Patent Litigation and Strategy'' with Federal Circuit Chief Judge [[Paul Redmond Michel]], Raphael V. Lupo (1st and 2nd editions), Professor Timothy R. Holbrook of [[Emory Law School]] (3d and 4th editions), and [[John Frank Murphy]] (4th ed.).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://legalsolutions.thomsonreuters.com/law-products/Law-School-Casebooks/Moore-Holbrook-and-Murphys-Patent-Litigation-and-Strategy-4th/p/100095212 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130813190503/http://legalsolutions.thomsonreuters.com/law-products/Law-School-Casebooks/Moore-Holbrook-and-Murphys-Patent-Litigation-and-Strategy-4th/p/100095212 |url-status=dead |archive-date=August 13, 2013 |title=Patent Litigation and Strategy }}</ref> She was also editor-in-chief of the [[Federal Circuit Bar Journal]].

==Personal life==
Moore has four children with her husband, Matt, who is a partner at [[Latham & Watkins]] LLP.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kimberly Moore '90, SM '91 |url=https://www.technologyreview.com/2007/06/18/36984/kimberly-moore-90-sm-91/ |access-date=April 27, 2022 |website=MIT Technology Review |language=en}}</ref> Moore is Catholic, and attends the same church as the late Justice [[Antonin Scalia]] did. She has chambers in the [[Howard T. Markey National Courts Building]] in [[Washington, D.C.]]

