'''Matthew Alexander Schwartz''' (born October 1978)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://opengovny.com/attorney/4252466|title=Matthew Alexander Schwartz · Sullivan & Cromwell LLP · 125 Broad St, New York, NY 10004-2400|website=opengovny.com}}</ref> is a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] for the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit]], sworn in on July 24, 2026. He was a [[law firm partner|partner]] at [[Sullivan & Cromwell]].

==Early life and education==
Schwartz was born in October 1978 in [[New York City]].<ref>{{cite web |author=<!-- not stated --> |date=April 10, 2026 |title=Matthew Alexander Schwartz |url=https://www.truepeoplesearch.com/find/person/pxl662u064r4u98r94696 |website=www.truepeoplesearch.com |location= |publisher=True People Search |access-date=April 10, 2026}}</ref> He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]]  degree in 2000 from [[Princeton University]] and a [[Juris Doctor]] in 2003 from [[Columbia Law School]]. He clerked twice for Justice [[Samuel Alito]], firstly when Alito was a judge of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit|U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit]] from 2003 to 2004, then secondly when Alito was on the [[U.S. Supreme Court]] from 2006 to 2007.<ref name="S&C">{{Cite web|url=https://www.sullcrom.com/Lawyers/Matthew-A-Schwartz|title=Matthew A. Schwartz|website=Sullivan and Cromwell LLC}}</ref><ref name="SCQ">{{cite web |last=Schwartz |first=Matthew Alexander |date=April 15, 2026 |title=Senate Judiciary Committee Questionnaire |url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/schwartz_sjq_14sm571s0amh6.pdf |website=www.judiciary.senate.gov |location= |publisher=United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary |access-date=April 23, 2026}}</ref>

==Career==
Schwartz joined the [[white shoe law firm]] of [[Sullivan & Cromwell]] in [[New York City]] in 2007 as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] and was elected [[Partner (law firm)|partner]] in 2011.<ref name="S&C"/><ref name="SCQ"/> He focused mainly on [[securities law]], mergers and acquisitions, [[derivatives market|derivative suits]], antitrust law, [[bank regulation]], contracts, [[constitutional law]], administrative law, and general [[commercial litigation]].<ref name="S&C"/> He served as personal counsel to President [[Donald Trump]] in several cases, including Trump's appeal in the [[Stormy Daniels hush money payment scheme|Stormy Daniels hush money case]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Thomsen |first=Jacqueline |date=April 10, 2026 |title=Trump Picks One of Hush Money Case Lawyers for Appeals Court |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-04-10/trump-picks-big-law-partner-on-hush-money-case-for-appeals-court?embedded-checkout=true |work= |location= |publisher=Bloomberg |access-date=April 10, 2026}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===
On April 10, 2026, Trump announced his intention to nominate Schwartz to an unspecified seat on the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/116382563202541274|title=Donald J. Trump (@realDonaldTrump)|website=Truth Social}}</ref> On April 27, 2026, Trump nominated Schwartz to the seat being vacated by [[Debra Ann Livingston]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/nomination/119th-congress/938/2|title=PN938-2 - Nomination of Matthew A. Schwartz for The Judiciary, 119th Congress (2025-2026)|date=April 27, 2026|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref> On May 20, 2026, Schwartz had a confirmation hearing before the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary]]. On June 18, 2026, the committee advanced his nomination by a 12–10 party line vote. On June 24, 2026, the entire Senate invoked [[cloture]] on his nomination by a 52–45 vote. On July 14, 2026, the Senate confirmed him by a 50–45 vote. He received his judicial commission on July 24, 2026.<ref>{{FJC Bio|nid=13762140}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Schwartz is married to Amy Cayne and they have four children.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://abovethelaw.com/2006/12/justice-alitos-ot-2006-law-clerks/|title=Justice Alito's OT 2006 Law Clerks|first=David|last=Lat|date=December 19, 2006}}</ref> They reside in [[Westchester County, New York]].<ref name="SCQ"/>

