'''Raymond Joseph Lohier Jr.''' (born December 1, 1965) is a Canadian-born American lawyer who serves as the [[United States federal judge|chief United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit]]. Formerly, he was an [[assistant United States attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York|Southern District of New York]] and a senior trial attorney in the [[United States Department of Justice Civil Rights Division]]. He was the chief of the [[securities fraud|securities]] and [[commodities fraud]] task force in the criminal division of the U.S. Attorney's office. He was recommended by New York Senator [[Charles Schumer]] for nomination to the seat on the Second Circuit that was vacated by Judge [[Sonia Sotomayor]] when she was elevated to the [[Supreme Court of the United States]]. Lohier is the first [[Haitian Americans|Haitian-American]] to serve as an Article III federal judge and to be confirmed by the United States Senate as a judge.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.hot97.com/haitianindependence/index.aspx |title=Notable Haitians |access-date=March 26, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140326080519/http://www.hot97.com/haitianindependence/index.aspx |archive-date=March 26, 2014 }}</ref>

== Early life and education ==

Lohier Jr. was born in [[Montreal, Quebec]]. He is of [[Haiti]]an heritage.<ref name=schumer20100208>[http://schumer.senate.gov/new_website/record.cfm?id=322147 Schumer Pushes for Raymond J. Lohier to be Chosen for a Vacancy on the United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit], (February 8, 2010).</ref> He graduated from [[Friends' Central School]] in [[Philadelphia]] in 1984.<ref>{{Cite web|title = Alumni/ae|url = https://www.friendscentral.org/page.cfm?p=525|website = Friends' Central School|access-date = May 28, 2015}}</ref> He went on to earn a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, graduating ''[[cum laude]]'' from [[Harvard College]], followed by a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[New York University School of Law]],<ref name=schumer20100208/> where he served as Editor in Chief of the ''[[NYU Annual Survey of American Law]]''. He worked as a [[law clerk]] for Judge [[Robert P. Patterson Jr.]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York]].<ref name=schumer20100208/><ref name="fjc.gov">{{FJC Bio|nid=1393561|inline=yes}}</ref>

== Career ==

Early in his career, Lohier worked as an [[Associate lawyer|associate]] in the law firm [[Cleary Gottlieb Steen & Hamilton]] in [[New York City]]. From 1997 to 2000, Lohier served as a Senior Trial Attorney with the United States Department of Justice Civil Rights Division, where he worked under [[Bill Lann Lee]] and spearheaded employment discrimination-related litigation and worked on other civil rights matters of importance to the federal government.<ref>{{Cite web | url=http://www.ca2.uscourts.gov/judges/bios/rjl.html | title=Hon. Raymond J. Lohier }}</ref> He became an Assistant United States Attorney in 2000, and later became chief of the narcotics unit. He later became chief of the Securities and Commodities Fraud Task Force.<ref name=schumer20100208/><ref name="fjc.gov"/>

Lohier may be most known in his career at the U.S. Attorney's office for having helped to oversee the investigation into the [[Madoff investment scandal]] and worked as a prosecutor on the [[Marc Dreier]] case.<ref>Ashby Jones, [https://blogs.wsj.com/law/2010/02/10/from-us-attorneys-office-to-second-circuit-for-raymond-lohier/ From U.S. Attorney’s Office to Second Circuit for Ray Lohier?], ''Law Blog - Wall Street Journal'' (February 10, 2010).</ref>

=== Federal judicial service ===

On February 8, 2010, [[Chuck Schumer]] announced that he would recommend Lohier for a seat on the United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit that was vacated by [[Sonia Sotomayor]], who was elevated to the [[Supreme Court of the United States]] on August 6, 2009.<ref name="schumer20100208" /> On March 10, 2010, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Lohier to the seat.<ref>[https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/president-obama-nominates-raymond-lohier-jr-united-states-court-appeals-second-circ President Obama Nominates Raymond Lohier, Jr. for the United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit, Judge Kate O'Malley for the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216172847/https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/president-obama-nominates-raymond-lohier-jr-united-states-court-appeals-second-circ |date=February 16, 2017 }} (March 10, 2010).</ref> On December 19, 2010, the Senate confirmed Lohier by a 92–0 vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1112/vote_111_2_00284.htm|title= On the Nomination (Confirmation Raymond Joseph Lohier, Jr., of New York, to be U.S. Circuit Judge)|date=December 19, 2010|website=[[United States Senate]]|access-date=October 25, 2022}}</ref> He received his judicial commission on December 20, 2010.<ref name="fjc.gov" /> He became chief judge of the court in 2026.<ref name="fjc.gov" />

== Personal life ==

Lohier's wife, Donna Hae Kyun Lee, is Senior Associate Dean of Clinical Programs and a professor at the [[CUNY School of Law]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.law.cuny.edu/faculty/directory/lee/|title=Donna Lee profile| date=January 4, 2019 }}</ref> The couple married in 1999 and live in [[Brooklyn, New York]].<ref name=bde_20100209>Ryan Thompson, [http://www.brooklyneagle.com/categories/category.php?category_id=4&id=33451 Senator Chooses Brooklyn Community Board Officer For Circuit Court], ''[[Brooklyn Eagle|Brooklyn Daily Eagle]]'' (February 9, 2010).</ref> In 2021, Lohier was elected to the [[Harvard Board of Overseers]] for a six-year term ending in 2027.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.harvard.edu/about-harvard/leadership-and-governance/board-of-overseers/ | title=Board of Overseers }}</ref> In May 2026, Harvard University announced that Lohier would serve as the president of the Board of Overseers for the 2026-2027 academic year.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Overseers name new senior officers
|url=https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2026/05/overseers-name-new-senior-officers/|access-date=May 21, 2026|website=The Harvard Gazette|language=en}}</ref> Lohier is also an elected member of the [[American Law Institute]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|title=Members|url=https://www.ali.org/members/member/373776/|access-date=February 10, 2022|website=American Law Institute|language=en}}</ref>

