'''Raymond Tsong-he Chen''' ({{Lang-zh|t=陳中和|p=Chén Zhōnghé}}; born 1968)<ref name=questionnaire/> is an American lawyer who has served as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]] since 2013.

== Early life and education ==
Chen was born in 1968 in [[New York City]] to a [[Taiwanese American]] family.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-08-01 |title=CAPAC Applauds Confirmation of Raymond T. Chen to U.S. Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit {{!}} Congressional Asian Pacific American Caucus (CAPAC) |url=https://capac.house.gov/press-release/capac-applauds-confirmation-raymond-t-chen-us-court-appeals-federal-circuit |access-date=2025-01-21 |website=capac.house.gov |language=en}}</ref> He was raised in [[Huntington Beach, California]], where he graduated from [[Edison High School (Huntington Beach, California)|Edison High School]] in 1986.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=November 6, 2013 |title=Raymond Chen To Be Honored at OCA-DC Gala |url=https://www.asianfortunenews.com/2013/11/profile-raymond-chen-to-be-honored-at-oca-dc-gala/ |access-date=2024-11-30 |website=Asian Fortune |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Hall of Fame Inductees – 2018 {{!}} Edison High School Alumni Hall of Fame |url=https://www.edisonhbalumnihof.com/hall-of-fame-inductees-2018/ |access-date=2024-12-01 |website=www.edisonhbalumnihof.com}}</ref> Both of his parents earned doctorates; Chen's father, Paul Chen, was a senior chemical engineer at an engineering company in [[Irvine, California]], and his mother, Pei-ching Chen, worked as a [[software engineer]] at the [[NASA]] [[Jet Propulsion Laboratory]].<ref name=":0" /> Chen's maternal grandfather, Tsuin-chen Ou, was a member of the [[Kuomintang]] who served as the vice minister of education in [[Taiwan]].<ref name=":0" />

After high school, Chen received a scholarship from [[United Technologies]] to study at the [[University of California, Los Angeles]], graduating in 1990 with a [[Bachelor of Science]] (B.S.) in [[electrical engineering]] with membership in [[Tau Beta Pi]] and [[Eta Kappa Nu]].<ref name="questionnaire" /> From 1989 to 1991, he was employed as a scientist for the law firm of Hecker & Harriman (now Hecker Law Group).<ref name=":1" /> He then earned a [[Juris Doctor]] (J.D.) from the [[New York University School of Law]] in 1994.<ref name="fedcir" />

==Law Career==
Chen was admitted to the [[State Bar of California]] in 1994 and  joined the intellectual property law firm of Knobbe, Martens, Olson & Bear in [[Irvine, California|Irvine]], [[California]].<ref name="questionnaire" /> He prosecuted patents and represented clients in intellectual property litigation at that firm.<ref name="fedcir">Raymond T. Chen, US Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit, http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/judges/raymond-t-chen</ref> From 1996 to 1998, he served as a technical assistant at the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit, performing the functions of a staff attorney.<ref name="fedcir" /> From 1998 to 2013, he served in the United States Patent and Trademark Office as an assistant solicitor and was promoted to Solicitor in 2008.<ref name="fedcir" /> He represented the USPTO before the Federal Circuit, personally arguing twenty cases, including ''In re Bilski'', ''In re Nuijten'', and ''In re Comiskey''.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uspto.gov/about/bios/chen_bio.jsp|title=USPTO bio for Raymond T. Chen|access-date=2013-02-07|archive-date=2013-08-02|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130802191706/http://www.uspto.gov/about/bios/chen_bio.jsp|url-status=live}}</ref> In that role, he issued guidance to patent examiners, advised the agency on legal and policy issues and helped promulgate regulations. He has co-chaired the Patent and Trademark Office Committee of the Federal Circuit Bar Association and is a member of the Advisory Council for the Federal Circuit.<ref name="whitehouse.gov">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/02/07/president-obama-nominates-two-serve-us-court-appeals-federal-circuit|title=President Obama Nominates Two to Serve on the US Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit|date=7 February 2013|access-date=19 February 2021|archive-date=16 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216161447/https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2013/02/07/president-obama-nominates-two-serve-us-court-appeals-federal-circuit|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=questionnaire>{{cite web|url=https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Raymond-Chen-Senate-Questionnaire.pdf|title=Senate Judiciary Committee Nomination Questionnaire|access-date=2013-02-14|archive-date=2017-01-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170128125505/http://www.judiciary.senate.gov/nominations/113thCongressJudicialNominations/upload/Chen-Senate-Questionnaire.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Federal judicial service===

On February 7, 2013, President [[Barack Obama]] nominated Chen to serve as a United States circuit judge of the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit, to the seat vacated by Judge [[Richard Linn]] who assumed [[senior status]] on October 31, 2012. His nomination was reported by the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] on May 16, 2013, by a [[voice vote]].<ref name="whitehouse.gov"/> The Senate confirmed Chen's nomination on August 1, 2013 by a 97–0 vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=113&session=1&vote=00198|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Raymond T. Chen, of Maryland, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Federal Circuit)|access-date=2013-08-01}}</ref> He received his commission on August 2, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/chen-raymond-t.|title=Chen, Raymond T. – Federal Judicial Center|website=www.fjc.gov|access-date=2017-04-13}}</ref> He assumed office on August 5, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/judges/raymond-t-chen|title=RAYMOND T. CHEN – US Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit|access-date=2015-08-21|archive-date=2015-08-17|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150817073940/http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/judges/raymond-t-chen|url-status=live}}</ref> Chen is also the second [[Asian American]] judge to be on the [[Federal Circuit]], the first being [[Shiro Kashiwa]] (1982–1986).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.asianfortunenews.com/2013/11/profile-raymond-chen-to-be-honored-at-oca-dc-gala/|title=PROFILE: Raymond Chen To Be Honored at OCA-DC Gala – Asian Fortune|date=7 November 2013 |access-date=2016-11-21|archive-date=2016-11-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161122153305/http://www.asianfortunenews.com/2013/11/profile-raymond-chen-to-be-honored-at-oca-dc-gala/|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Chen's wife, Lisa Kuang-ling Hsiao, was an [[associate attorney]] at the law firm of Verner, Liipfert, Bernhard, McPherson and Hand. She graduated ''[[magna cum laude]]'' from [[Yale University]]. They married on August 23, 1997.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |date=August 24, 1997 |title=Lisa Hsiao and Raymond Chen |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1997/08/24/style/lisa-hsiao-and-raymond-chen.html |access-date=November 30, 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> 

As of 2024, Chen is a member of the board of trustees of the New York University School of Law.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Board of Trustees {{!}} NYU School of Law |url=https://www.law.nyu.edu/about/trustees |access-date=2024-12-01 |website=www.law.nyu.edu}}</ref> 

