'''Richard Gary Taranto''' (born May 6, 1957) is a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]].

==Biography==

Taranto was born in [[New York City]] on May 6, 1957.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.martindale.com/Richard-G-Taranto/372200-lawyer.htm|title=Richard G. Taranto Judge Profile on Martindale.com|access-date=2011-11-11|archive-date=2008-08-02|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080802033626/http://www.martindale.com/Richard-G-Taranto/372200-lawyer.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, summa cum laude, in 1977 from [[Pomona College]] in [[Claremont, California]]. He spent a semester each at [[Yale University]] and then the [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]] in their Ph.D. programs in Mathematics. He then received his [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Yale Law School]] in 1981. While in law school he served as an article and book editor for the ''[[Yale Law Journal]]''. Upon completion of law school, he clerked for Judge [[Abraham David Sofaer]] of the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of New York]] from 1981 to 1982. From 1982 to 1983, he clerked for Judge [[Robert Bork]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit]]. He then clerked for Justice [[Sandra Day O'Connor]] of the [[United States Supreme Court]]. From 1984 to 1986, Taranto worked at the law firm of Onek, Klein & Farr in [[Washington, D.C.]] From 1986 to 1989 he spent three years as an assistant to the [[United States Solicitor General|Solicitor General]]. In 1989, he returned to Onek, Klein & Farr (later named Farr & Taranto) as a partner.<ref name="2011-11-10">{{cite web|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/10/president-obama-nominates-richard-gary-taranto-serve-us-court-appeals|title=President Obama Nominates Richard Gary Taranto to Serve on the US Court of Appeals|date=10 November 2011|access-date=22 February 2021|archive-date=16 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170216163910/https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/11/10/president-obama-nominates-richard-gary-taranto-serve-us-court-appeals|via=[[NARA|National Archives]]|work=[[whitehouse.gov]]|url-status=live}}</ref>

Taranto has taught a course on patent law at [[Harvard Law School]] and various courses at [[Georgetown University Law Center]]. Since 2009, he has served as a member of the appellate rules advisory committee for the [[United States Judicial Conference]]. He has argued nineteen cases before the United States Supreme Court.<ref name="2011-11-10" />

==Federal judicial service==

On November 10, 2011, President Obama nominated Taranto to serve as a [[United States federal judge|United States circuit judge]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit]] to replace [[Paul Redmond Michel]], who retired in 2010.<ref name="2011-11-10" /> The previous nominee for this position, [[Edward C. DuMont]], withdrew after his nomination languished in the Senate for eighteen months without action. On January 2, 2013, Taranto's nomination was returned to the President, due to the [[Adjournment sine die|sine die]] adjournment of the Senate.

On January 3, 2013, Taranto was re-nominated to the same office. On February 7, 2013, the [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Senate Judiciary Committee]] reported his nomination to the floor by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Taranto Approved by Judiciary Committee, Nominee Now Awaits Full Senate Approval|url=http://about.bloomberglaw.com/law-reports/taranto-approved-by/|access-date=March 11, 2013|newspaper=Bloomberg Law|archive-date=April 10, 2013|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130410161640/http://about.bloomberglaw.com/law-reports/taranto-approved-by/|url-status=live}}</ref> On March 11, 2013, the Senate confirmed him by a 91–0 vote.<ref>{{cite news|title=On the Nomination (Confirmation Richard Gary Taranto, of Maryland, to be U.S. Circuit Judge for the Federal Circuit)|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1131/vote_113_1_00033.htm|access-date=November 6, 2022|publisher=United States Senate|date=March 11, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=After 17 Months, Senate Confirms New Federal Circuit Judge|url=http://legaltimes.typepad.com/blt/2013/03/after-17-months-senate-confirms-new-federal-circuit-judge.html |access-date=March 11, 2013|newspaper=Legal Times|date=March 11, 2013}}</ref> He received his commission on March 12, 2013, and assumed office on March 15, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/taranto-richard-gary|title=Taranto, Richard Gary – Federal Judicial Center|website=www.fjc.gov|access-date=2021-02-22}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/judges/richard-g-taranto|title=RICHARD G. TARANTO – US Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit|access-date=2021-02-22|archive-date=2021-02-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210222193300/http://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/judges/richard-g-taranto|url-status=live}}</ref>

